 }
 ```
 
+A provider turn explicitly ending in `length`, `content-filter`, or `error`
+produces an error finish, including when it contains only text. Text deltas may
+already have been delivered and the finish event retains that partial text.
+Tool batches with missing, empty, or non-string IDs or names, non-string
+arguments, or duplicate IDs within the same turn are rejected before any tool
+executes. Invalid JSON strings still produce paired tool errors. A missing or `other` finish reason is
+still accepted for provider compatibility; callers must not treat this as a
+complete validation of the provider stream.
+
+`abort()` stops waiting for a provider or tool even if it ignores cancellation.
+It cannot forcibly stop that underlying work: providers should honor the input
+signal and tools must manage their own cancellation and side effects. A provider
+that remains blocked may retain resources after the loop reports an abort. If a
+consumer stops iterating early, the loop also signals the provider and requests
+stream cleanup without waiting for it; that cleanup may continue afterward.
+
+Provider errors and tool failure reasons pass through the shared diagnostic
+scrubber before publication or a subsequent model turn. Redaction is heuristic,
+not a guarantee that every secret is removed; successful tool results are not
+scrubbed.
+
 ## Running the example
 
 The example script is an interactive REPL with mock tools and a real terminal command tool.
