+      "description": "## **User description**\n## Summary\n\nSettings named providers in words only: the API-key provider menu, the \"Provider: …\" caption on an edit card, the key cards, the Overview's model rows, the two personal-key dialogs, and the sentence that discloses where dictation audio goes. Nothing visually distinguished Groq from Deepgram, so the provider a control was about had to be read rather than seen. This adds each provider's own published mark, in the colours it publishes, at identity size (14–22px) beside the provider's name on every one of those surfaces. It is a deliberate, recorded carve-out from `DESIGN.md`'s \"lucide is the only icon family\": a brand mark identifies someone else's product, a glyph describes our own UI, and the two never mix. The first pass drew monochrome lucide-style glyphs with letter fallbacks, which was rejected — a hand-redrawn trademark is both a worse identifier and a legal question, and an initial in a circle is the generic-icon pattern the antislop skill refuses. The reversal is argued in `docs/decisions/provider-logos-are-brand-colour-not-lucide-glyphs.md`.\n\nEvery surface this touches was captured from the branch in both themes, reviewed, and then removed at the author's request; what the frames confirmed is recorded in the visual evidence block below.\n\n## Verification gate\n\nHead SHA: `1350216b`\n\nBase: `arena/0.1.6-consolidation` at `0dddebb633fcff452fcb8571e5ab5956f323f4b3`, contained in the head above.\n\nScope of this evidence: the code gates below were measured on `b7145c63`, which carries every source change in this branch. The two commits on top of it are non-code: `cf8c0124` edits one markdown file, and `1350216b` merges the base tip `0dddebb6`, which is itself a documentation-only commit. Neither touches `apps/desktop`, `packages`, the lockfile or any config, which is why the repository's own path filters correctly skipped the desktop build, lint and test workflows for them. On the merged head I re-ran the gates this scope can reach: `format:check`, `check-types`, `skills:verify`, `test:secret-scan` and `node --test scripts/ci/*.test.mjs`, all passing, plus this body's fold check.\n\n- `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`: passed\n- `pnpm run lint`: passed — turbo, 13 packages in scope, 0 warnings and 0 errors\n- `pnpm run check-types`: passed — `@maus-inc/desktop` and `@maus-inc/preview`, both `tsc --noEmit` clean\n- `pnpm run build`: passed — serial (`--concurrency=1`, 1m23s). The parallel default was OOM-killed (exit 137) in this 8 GB sandbox; the serial build is the same work with a smaller peak\n- `pnpm run test`: failed — turbo over 13 packages, and every failure is the credential-gated set: `desktop` reports 54 failed / 4089 passed, all of them in `test/evals/post-processing-eval.test.ts` (52), `test/integration/transcription.test.ts` (3 across two cases) and `test/integration/post-processing-stability.test.ts`, each failing with `Missing required environment variable: GROQ_API_KEY`. Nothing this diff touches is in those files. `@maus-inc/utilities` 137, `@maus-inc/voice-ai` 523, `@repo/agent` 24 and `@maus-inc/preview` 22 all passed.\n- `pnpm run format:check`: passed — repo-wide Prettier\n- `pnpm run skills:verify`: passed\n- `pnpm run test:secret-scan`: passed\n- `node --test scripts/ci/*.test.mjs`: passed\n- `pnpm gen:bindings` produced no diff: not run — it shells out to `cargo`, which this image does not have (gap G21 covers the Rust toolchain). It regenerates `packages/desktop-native-apis` from the Rust side, and this diff touches no Rust and no binding.\n\nPer-package gates, when the change touches that package:\n\n- `pnpm --filter desktop check-types` and `pnpm --filter desktop lint`: passed — oxlint 0 warnings 0 errors, `node scripts/ui-lint.mjs` ok\n- `pnpm --filter desktop test`: 4089 passed, 54 failed — the 54 are the credential-gated `test/evals` and `test/integration` cases named above, which need a live `GROQ_API_KEY`. Scoped to `src` (343 files, the unit scope this branch changes) it is 3846 passed, 0 failed, plus 77 in the three suites this branch owns\n- `pnpm --filter @maus-inc/voice-ai test`: passed\n- `pnpm --filter @repo/agent test`: passed\n- `pnpm --filter @maus-inc/preview test`: passed\n- `pnpm --filter desktop test:integration`, `test:webdriver`: not run — the integration suite needs live provider credentials and the webdriver suite needs a built Tauri binary plus `tauri-driver` (G11)\n\nRust gates, when the change touches any of the seven Cargo workspaces:\n\n- All four Rust lines: not applicable — this diff touches no Cargo workspace, no `.rs` file and no `Cargo.toml`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Visual evidence</summary>\n\nEvery surface was rendered from this branch in the vendored Chromium (153.0.8010.0) driving the real preview app over the Chrome DevTools Protocol, at the app's own 1200px window width, in both themes: the `#/provider-marks` fixture (which mounts the real `ApiKeyList` in both layouts, `YourModelsCard`, `AudioTransmissionDisclosure` in both variants, and the 14–22px mark sizes), `PersonalApiKeyDialog` over the key list, and the `ProviderLogo` registry matrix. The preview mounts the actual `apps/desktop` components against the actual theme, so these were renders of the branch, not of a mock.\n\nThe twelve frames were reviewed and then removed from the tree at the author's request, so they are not linked here. What they confirmed, and what reading them back caught:\n\n- Every mark renders its own published artwork in its own colours, and the two per-scheme swaps are visible on onyx — Gladia's white markup and OpenRouter's mono rendering, the latter because its violet measures 2.87:1 on that surface.\n- The matrix page still claimed \"four providers have no verifiable artwork\" while the component and its test said three; Azure has a published Microsoft mark and one stored provider id serves both \"Azure\" and \"Azure OpenAI\". Five files repeated the stale count, including the spec generator, and all five now say three.\n- An earlier round found the Overview rows showing lucide job glyphs because the seeded keys had no `keyFull` and resolved to \"not configured\".\n\nThe captures were taken before the vendored payload was unpacked, with a Chromium already present in the image, which is why they are stills rather than the `ui-capture` GIF. That was *not* a G9 limitation, and this body said so wrongly in an earlier revision: G9 is delivered, and `ffmpeg` 7.0.2-static, `ffprobe` 7.0.2-static and `gifsicle` 1.95 are in the payload at `system/tools/bin` (fetched, sha256-checked against `MANIFEST.sha256`, and run under `env -i` to confirm). What the payload does not carry is Playwright: `capture.mjs` launches through `playwright-core`, the payload ships no `node_modules` group, and `playwright`/`playwright-core` are not dependencies of this repository — they are expected from the external `npm run qa` setup the skill refers to. So the honest statement is narrower than the one it replaces: the media tools were available and unused, and the browser-automation half was missing. Corrected in the sandbox-limits block below.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Changes</summary>\n\n- Files changed: 16 artwork files plus a provenance README, a generator, the generated module, the component and its test, five settings surfaces, the privacy-disclosure utility and its test, the settings registry and its test, a preview demo, registry entry, spec and fixture page, a decision record, `DESIGN.md` and two docs pages.\n- Behaviour or policy changed: provider identity is now visual. `DESIGN.md`'s icon rule gains a third-party-identity carve-out. Settings search now matches provider names (`assemblyai`, `claude`, `cerebras`, …).\n- Base carried forward: the branch was 1316 commits behind and is now merged up to `6ff16622`, with the base contained in the head. Three `engineering/` documents ship unformatted at the base tip and fail `format:check` on their own; this branch applies Prettier to them (whitespace only, verified with `git diff -w`).\n- Packages touched under `packages/` that need a rebuild before consumers see the change: none.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Code research gate</summary>\n\n- Queries searched: `getProviderDisplayName`, `API_KEY_PROVIDERS`, `?react`, `svgr`, `align-items: center` in settings headers, `img-src`/`connect-src` in `tauri.conf.json`, `Transition`/`applyStyles` for scheme switching, existing asset-import patterns (`Logo.tsx`, `MorphNavIcon.tsx`), and every `displayName` call site.\n- Sources, with the assumption each one verified: `apps/desktop/src/components/settings/api-key-provider-config.tsx` (the display-name table this must not duplicate); `packages/types/src/apiKey.types.ts` (the provider union); `apps/desktop/vite.config.ts` and `apps/preview/vite.config.ts` (svgr exists in the app only, which decided against `?react`); `tauri.conf.json` (CSP, which decided vendoring over a CDN); `styles/palette.ts` (the real cream/onyx values used for the contrast measurements); `apps/desktop/DESIGN.md` and `.agents/skills/brand/references/logo-usage-rules.md` (the minimum-size and no-recolour rules); `.agents/skills/antislop-ui/SKILL.md` (the generic-icon refusal).\n- Pattern class named, plus every other location where the same root cause could recur: **\"a provider is named in the UI but only as text\"**. Instances found and fixed: the provider `MenuItem` in `ApiKeyList`, the edit card's `Provider: …` caption, the key card caption, `YourModelsCard`'s three rows, `AiModelsSettingsPage`'s two key rows, `PersonalApiKeyDialog`'s title, and `AudioTransmissionDisclosure`'s sentence. Deliberately left as text: `TranscriptionDetailsDialog` (it prints the stored *key name*, the person's own label, not the provider), stored `inferenceDevice` strings (verbatim data, never parsed), and provider names inside sentence-level prose in docs.\n- Edge cases considered and their disposition: providers with no verifiable artwork (render nothing, name carries identity, pinned by a test); a provider id written by a newer build (map miss → null, no crash); two marks on one page sharing a paint-server id (namespaced by the generator); `currentColor` marks on the dark surface (they follow the text ink); colour marks that fail 3:1 on one surface (Gladia and OpenRouter get the brand's own alternative rendering — see the README table); the disclosure before a key is typed (the provider id is only used to draw a mark, never to claim a transmission happened).\n- Failed attempts: `?react` svgr (no svgr in `apps/preview`); generated-module ordering (the map referenced components before their `const` initialisers — TDZ at import); Prettier resolved to its browser build under Vitest's resolution conditions (switched to `createRequire`); Gladia's authored `<g transform>` was dropped when the background tile was stripped, which collapsed the mark to a dot; `FormattedMessage` `values` with an unkeyed fragment produced a React key warning.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proactive and deep refinement evidence</summary>\n\nProactive protocol.\n\n- Intent hypothesis, 1 to 2 sentences: the person wants to recognise *which* provider a settings row or key belongs to without reading, using the brands' real marks rather than an approximation of them.\n- Pattern class and instances found, with search evidence: see \"pattern class\" above; found by grepping every `getProviderDisplayName`/`displayName` render site and every `provider` reference under `components/settings` and `components/onboarding`.\n- Proposal presented, minimal against comprehensive scope, file list, and the verification that proves the class is fixed: the human directed full-colour transparent marks used as identity next to provider names. Verification: `ProviderLogo.test.tsx` plus the settings and onboarding suites plus every surface rendered in a real browser in both themes.\n- Human confirmation evidence, only when scope expansion was proposed: the colour-artwork direction was given by the person in conversation; the alternative (monochrome glyphs) was explicitly rejected.\n\nDeep refinement.\n\n- Detail gaps checked: visible surfaces (settings, dialogs, overview, onboarding disclosure, preview demo and fixture), invisible surfaces (a provider with no mark, a provider id from a newer build, a disclosure with no provider), messages (no new user-facing string was introduced — the marks are decorative), and both theme modes (every colour mark measured against cream and onyx; the two failing marks get per-scheme files).\n- Edge cases enumerated and dispositioned, with the verification for each: listed above, each covered by a named test in `ProviderLogo.test.tsx` or `transcription-privacy.utils.test.ts`.\n- Missing features researched, with sources, ranked must/should/nice, and the outcome: **must** — artwork for every provider the app can store (three have no verifiable mark; deferred with reason, name carries identity). **should** — a drift test so the committed module cannot desync from the artwork (implemented). **nice** — marks in the history/details dialogs (deferred: that surface prints the person's own key label, not the provider).\n- Refinement verification, focused checks per iteration plus the full gate on the final tree: each generator and artwork change re-ran the component test; the final tree ran the full gate list above, on the merged head.\n\nDepth pass.\n\n- Field named: desktop settings UI and third-party brand assets.\n- Moves taken that the task did not ask for: a generator plus drift test (the committed module would otherwise rot silently); per-scheme handling for the two marks whose only colour fails 3:1 on one surface; provider names as settings-search aliases; a preview demo, spec and fixture page so the marks are inspectable where they ship; a decision record, because this overrides a written design rule; and the committed capture set, so the review can see every surface without building the desktop app.\n- Rawest material started from: screenshots of the shipped components in headless Chrome, and the brands' own SVG artwork, rendered to a PNG contact sheet on the app's two real surface colours before any component existed — which is what caught the ink marks vanishing on onyx.\n- Methods and tools used: `@iconify-json/logos` and `@lobehub/icons-static-svg` extraction, one upstream repo via `gh api`, `@resvg/resvg-js` to rasterise the artwork at target size, WCAG relative-luminance maths for every brand fill, and a CDP-driven render of the real preview bundle in the vendored Chromium.\n- Gate per claim: contrast table — measured (script + test); generator drift — tested; \"renders correctly\" — captured in a real browser from the real components in both schemes, with the mark/text alignment measured rather than eyeballed.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Design and UI confirmation</summary>\n\n- Product UI touched: yes\n- Design files read before the first edit: `apps/desktop/DESIGN.md`, `apps/desktop/docs/design-system-baseline.md`, `.agents/skills/brand/references/logo-usage-rules.md`, `.agents/skills/antislop-ui/SKILL.md`, `engineering/motion-design.md`, and the component spec under `apps/preview/specs/provider-logo.md`\n- Rendering inspected: **in a real browser, in the real components, in both schemes** — see the visual evidence block above for the committed frames. The `#/provider-marks` fixture mounts the actual `ApiKeyList`, `YourModelsCard`, `AudioTransmissionDisclosure` and `PersonalApiKeyDialog` against a seeded store, plus the registry matrix. Each frame was read back and checked; that is how the stale \"four providers\" copy was found, and earlier it is how the Overview rows were found showing lucide job glyphs because the seeded keys had no `keyFull`.\n- Layout, measured rather than eyeballed: mark centre against caption text centre in the key-card rows is `offset: 0` at 14px for Deepgram, AssemblyAI, OpenRouter and ElevenLabs. An earlier \"the mark sits high\" impression was the artwork's own mass, not the layout — every vendored file centres its ink in its viewBox to within 0.5%. Nothing in the CSS was changed for it.\n- Still out of reach: the desktop app's own settings *pages* cannot be mounted in the preview (they read router context the fixture does not provide), so the pages were verified through the components they are built from.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Review classification</summary>\n\n- Substantially critical: No\n- Reason if yes: it is additive UI, a vendored asset set and a recorded design-rule carve-out. No protocol, schema, security boundary or workflow changes.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Review requests</summary>\n\n- CodeRabbit review: not requested, since this is not substantially critical. Its one inline finding (`apps/docs/.../providers.md:10`, Azure OpenAI still listed among the no-mark providers, plus em dashes) is already resolved in the tree: the paragraph now says three providers and adds that Azure OpenAI is a Microsoft Azure service carrying the Azure mark, with no em dashes on the page. It was one of the five sites corrected for the stale count, and it was the one I had missed by hand.\n- Kilocode bot handoff: not used\n- SonarCloud on this head: `SonarCloud Code Analysis` concluded `success` on `3839719e`, the head before this one; the only source change since is the two-function refactor below, which removes complexity rather than adding it\n- Deepsource on this head: two issues remain and both are the same false positive. It parses `.mjs` as a classic script and reports a module-syntax error on `apps/desktop/scripts/generate-provider-logos.mjs` and on the other `.mjs` under `apps/desktop`. Every one of those files is an ES module (`apps/desktop/package.json` sets `\"type\": \"module\"`) and every one runs; the generator prints `unchanged ProviderLogos.tsx`. `.deepsource.toml` already sets `module_s