+      "description": "## **User description**\n## Summary\n\nThis follow-up hardens recently merged desktop transcription paths against stale writes, late Incognito changes, post-start provider failures, and schema-conversion gaps. It also bounds the StylingPage flex layout. It targets `arena/0.1.6-consolidation`, the base used by the selected merged changes.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Changes</summary>\n\n- Gemini: recursively convert schema-valued keywords and schema maps without rewriting literal `default` or `examples` data; blank transcription responses do not retry or re-upload audio.\n- Dictation and History: defer persistence during simulated typing; append delivery warnings with one atomic SQLite update and merge only warning deltas into current state. Update the native binding and browser preview too.\n- Streaming sessions: preserve post-start AssemblyAI/ElevenLabs errors through finalization, reject empty failed sessions, and keep transcript text already received. Stop AssemblyAI buffering after provider rejection.\n- Error classification and UI: exact-match structured authentication codes; constrain StylingPage height/overflow and assert computed styles.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Code research gate</summary>\n\n- Queries searched: warning append call sites and command registration; provider `Error`/`finalize` handlers; Gemini schema keyword traversal; structured authentication markers; StylingPage and ScrollListPage sizing.\n- Sources: repository TypeScript/Rust command paths, generated bindings, preview runtime and SQLite query; Google GenAI JavaScript types. Verified schema-valued keywords need recursion while values under `default` and `examples` must remain literal data.\n- Pattern class: async full-row snapshots can overwrite newer transcription fields; post-start socket errors can be lost; schema recursion can miss nested definitions; substring matching can misclassify structured codes.\n- Edge cases: out-of-order warning replies, removed warnings, missing rows, malformed/non-string JSON, Incognito toggled during typing, blank model output, and provider errors with/without accumulated text are covered.\n- Failed attempts: native Rust gates could not run because `cargo`, `rustc`, and `rustup` are absent. The exact SQL was exercised with Python SQLite; changed Rust files were formatted with the rustfmt WASM formatter.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Deep refinement and UI confirmation</summary>\n\n- Scope stayed within confirmed findings from the user-selected merged PR batch; already-resolved/stale findings and distinct open-PR changes were not duplicated.\n- StylingPage changes only constrain the parent flex container. A jsdom computed-style regression test covers `height`, `minHeight`, and `overflow`; no browser visual inspection was done.\n- No broader feature expansion was needed. Rust compilation, tests, clippy, and binding regeneration remain for CI because the local Rust toolchain is unavailable.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Review classification and requests</summary>\n\n- Substantially critical: No; this is a focused correctness and layout hardening pass, not a security or protocol change.\n- CodeRabbit: not requested because the change is not substantially critical.\n- This branch now includes base commit `15366084` from PR #320. The classifier-test merge conflict was resolved with both authentication-code and axios-timeout coverage retained.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Review and babysit log</summary>\n\n| Round | Head | What arrived | What changed | Disposition |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | `68a50ceb` | Rust formatting and folded-body gates failed; other CI started. | Formatted the changed Rust files and reshaped this description. | Fixes pushed in `40a88347`; rerun pending. |\n| 2 | `4b8176f4` | Inline review findings and a base-branch test conflict. | Added regression coverage, refactored the flagged helpers, merged `arena/0.1.6-consolidation`, and retained both classifier tests. | All known inline threads resolved; current-head CI is running. |\n| 3 | `a78c776d` | Follow-up complexity and class-helper findings from DeepSource. | Moved stateless validators out of the preview runtime class and split message-type selection from ElevenLabs dispatch; reran targeted tests and type-check. | Pushed; current-head CI is running. |\n| 4 | `884715e7` | Repeated-warning count loss, short-height empty-state overflow, and SonarCloud provider-error stringification. | Reconciled append deltas against prior/current counts, made empty-state margins overflow-safe, and shared safe provider-error formatting across AssemblyAI and ElevenLabs. | Pushed; current inline threads resolved; current-head CI is running. |\n| 5 | `365d45e0` | DeepSource flagged medium-risk cyclomatic complexity in warning merging. | Split occurrence calculation and appending into focused helpers; reran the warning tests, desktop type-check, and full desktop lint. | Pushed; the complexity thread is resolved; current-head CI is running. |\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Sandbox limits hit</summary>\n\n- Native Rust toolchain unavailable. Python SQLite covered the SQL behavior only; it does not substitute for Rust compilation or tests.\n\n</details>\n\n## Verification gate\n\nHead SHA: `365d45e0c6b0657f0e701ac43246344499d6eb6d`\n\n- At `884715e7`, desktop type-check and full desktop lint passed (Prettier, Oxlint across 906 files with no warnings, and UI lint); 77 focused tests across 5 files passed for warning merging, empty-state scrolling, and shared provider-error formatting. The unchanged Gemini test suite passed previously (57 tests).\n- After the warning-merge complexity refactor at `365d45e0`, reran all 37 warning-merge tests; desktop type-check and full desktop lint passed again (Prettier, Oxlint across 906 files with no warnings, and UI lint).\n- The prior head's full desktop unit suite passed (341 files, 3,810 Vitest tests and 4 Node tests); the voice-ai package build also passed before the base merge.\n- Current-head GitHub CI is running the cross-platform builds, tests, security and analyzer checks. Native Rust compilation, tests, clippy, and binding regeneration remain for CI because the local Rust toolchain is unavailable.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Confirmed the base is `arena/0.1.6-consolidation`; this PR does not target `main`.\n- [x] Ran desktop type-check, lint, unit tests, focused tests, and the dependency build.\n- [x] Recorded the code-research and edge-case notes above; no secrets or raw provider output are included.\n- [ ] Native Rust gates: unavailable locally; awaiting current-head CI.\n\n## Footer\n\nMausAgent | Filed by Arena.ai Agent Mode, with @Owie6789, on 2026-11-07\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nHarden desktop transcription persistence, streaming failure handling, Gemini response processing, and constrained-page layouts against stale, late, and empty-result edge cases.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Prevent transcription warning updates from overwriting newer History changes, losing concurrent warning occurrences, or restoring deleted state.\n- Honor Incognito changes made during simulated typing by deferring History persistence until delivery completes.\n- Preserve post-start AssemblyAI and ElevenLabs failures, reject empty failed sessions, and retain transcript text received before a provider error.\n- Stop Gemini transcription retries and duplicate audio uploads for blank responses, while correctly converting nested schema-valued keywords without altering literal data.\n- Classify structured authentication codes using exact matches and avoid misclassifying similar provider values.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Add a field-specific native transcription-warning append command across desktop bindings, repositories, SQLite, and browser preview.\n- Improve scrollable empty-state and StylingPage flex layouts for constrained heights.\n\nTests:\n- Add regression coverage for concurrent warning merges, simulated-typing persistence, provider finalization failures, Gemini schema and blank-response handling, error classification, preview commands, and constrained layouts.\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n<!-- coderabbit-ai-generated:v1 -->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n  * Transcription warnings are appended without overwriting newer transcription data or restoring warnings that were removed.\n  * AssemblyAI and ElevenLabs errors preserve text already received; errors are reported when no transcript text is available.\n  * Authentication errors are classified more accurately, and empty AI transcription responses no longer trigger unnecessary retries.\n  * Dictation history is saved at the appropriate time for simulated typing and remote delivery.\n  * Styling pages use available vertical space without overflowing.\n* **Improvements**\n  * AI streaming handles additional JSON Schema structures more consistently.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n\n___\n\n## **CodeAnt-AI Description**\n**Keep dictation history accurate and handle transcription failures correctly**\n\n### What Changed\n- Warning updates now preserve newer History edits and retain concurrent warning additions.\n- History waits for simulated typing to finish before saving, so an Incognito change made during typing is respected—even if the stop handler times out.\n- Streaming provider errors after startup now fail empty transcriptions while retaining any transcript text already received; authentication errors are classified only when their code matches exactly.\n- Blank Gemini responses stop without retrying or uploading the audio again, and nested tool schemas are handled without changing literal examples or defaults.\n- Styling pages and scrollable empty states remain usable when content overflows.\n\n### Impact\n`✅ Fewer overwritten History updates`\n`✅ Incognito changes honored after typing`\n`✅ Clearer streaming transcription failures`\n<details><summary><strong>💡 Usage Guide</strong></summary>\n\n### Checking Your Pull Request\nEvery time you make a pull request, our system automatically looks through it. We check for security issues, mistakes in how you're setting up your infrastructure, and common code problems. We do this to make sure your changes are solid and won't cause any trouble later.\n\n### Talking to CodeAnt AI\nGot a question or need a hand with something in your pull request? You can easily get in touch with CodeAnt AI right here. Just type the following in a comment on your pull request, and replace \"Your question here\" with whatever you want to ask:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Your question here</code>\n</pre>\nThis lets you have a chat with CodeAnt AI about your pull request, making it easier to understand and improve your code.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Can you suggest a safer alternative to storing this secret?</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Preserve Org Learnings with CodeAnt\nYou can record team preferences so CodeAnt AI applies them in future reviews. Reply directly to the specific CodeAnt AI suggestion (in the same thread) and replace \"Your feedback here\" with your input:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Your feedback here</code>\n</pre>\nThis helps CodeAnt AI learn and adapt to your team's coding style and standards.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Do not flag unused imports.</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Retrigger review\nAsk CodeAnt AI to review the PR again, by typing:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: review</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Check Your Repository Health\nTo analyze the health of your code repository, visit our dashboard at [https://app.codeant.ai](https://app.codeant.ai). This tool helps you identify potential issues and areas for improvement in your codebase, ensuring your repository maintains high standards of code health.\n\n</details>\n"
