+      "description": "## **User description**\n## Summary\n\nThis is the direct continuation of #292, which merged while the final deep-audit round was still in flight. That round re-read every file of the merged feedback surface hunting for regressions and rough edges, and it found four real defects. The bot review round on the first filing of this work (#318, closed as a duplicate of a sandbox branch reset) then found two more real defects in the same family and one complexity flag. The visibility fix among them drew a second review round on this PR, which found the list refresh dropping the row again and the row menu offering Retranscribe for rows with no stored audio; both are fixed too, and one report about a failure marker was declined with reasons on its thread. All are fixed here with pinning tests, and every user-facing sentence is translated in all ten locales. Nothing else changed in the product: no API, no schema, no Rust, no component structure. One infrastructure addition came in at human request during review: the PR body fold checker now stops at the MausAgent footer, so the sections review bots append below it can never fail the check.\n\nThe fixes, in one line each:\n\n1. An axios request timeout reports code `ECONNABORTED`, and the abort rule substring-matched `aborted` inside it, telling users their request was cancelled when it ran out of clock. The timeout code set now claims it first.\n2. The batch retranscription merge only checked message containment in one direction, so a truncation-only row merging before a fallback-plus-truncation row repeated the tail sentence inside a single toast. Both directions now keep the fuller message through one extracted helper.\n3. An audio import whose store write was rejected while persistence was allowed leaves no History row and no incognito memory row, yet the feedback still claimed the transcript was available in this session. A new `hasSessionRow` option keeps that claim only where a session row really exists.\n4. The dictation pill shows one toast at a time, so the edit-failure toast and the outcome toast fired back to back meant the second replaced the first mid-read. The persist tail now combines both into a single toast.\n5. The incognito import memory record was written to `transcriptionById` only, while the History page and the recent list render from `transcriptionIds`, so the session-availability wording pointed at a row the user could not find. The record now appears on the list.\n6. A store-rejected import after a local-styling fallback still said \"your local style was applied instead\" over a transcript that is gone. The fallback builder now sees the import context and reports the missing save honestly.\n7. The visible-list refresh replaces `transcriptionIds` from the repository, which never contains the incognito memory record, so the newly listed row vanished on the next History or Home mount. A session-only registry now carries it across refreshes at its created-at position.\n8. The row context menu offered Retranscribe for rows with no stored audio, where the flow can only fail. The menu now offers it only where an audio snapshot exists, matching the audio pill's existing gate.\n9. Two late review edges on the session-only row: a local-data clear opening mid-import could be repopulated by the memory write, which now runs under a blocked-while-clearing write lease, and deleted memory rows left their ids in the registry forever; a committed delete now unmarks the id at the source, with the refresh prune as the safety net.\n\n10. A human-requested infrastructure fix outside the product code: the PR body fold checker judged the sections review bots append below the footer as authored text, so an unbolded bot summary heading failed the check and every push needed a manual re-bold. The check now stops at the first unfolded footer line; bot territory below is exempt from every rule except the duplicate-footer count.\n\n<details>\n<summary>What changed, in detail</summary>\n\n## Timeout versus abort classification\n\n`apps/desktop/src/actions/post-process-error-category.ts`: added `econnaborted` to `STRUCTURED_TIMEOUT_CODES`. The classifier checks structured timeout codes before the abort rule, so the axios timeout shape now lands in `timedOut` instead of being substring-matched by the `aborted` entry in `STRUCTURED_ABORT_CODES`. The test pins both the bare-code shape and the full axios shape with `timeout of 15000ms exceeded` as the message.\n\n## Batch feedback merge dedup\n\n`apps/desktop/src/actions/transcriptions.actions.ts`: extracted `mergeRetranscribeMessages(existing, incoming)`. Containment in either direction keeps the longer message; otherwise the two join. `mergeRetranscribeBatchFeedback` now calls it. The previous one-way check handled the case where the existing merged message already contained the incoming one, but not the reverse order, where a short truncation-only row merged first and the fallback-plus-truncation row appended its tail a second time. A new batch test drives both rows concurrently and pins the exact merged sentence.\n\n## Honest wording when an import saved nothing\n\n`apps/desktop/src/actions/post-process-feedback.ts`: new `hasSessionRow` option on `PostProcessFeedbackOptions`, defaulting to true, plus two new messages: a styling-failed variant and an unusable-reply variant that both say the transcript could not be saved in History. They are selected only for the audio-import context when `canRecoverFromHistory` is false and `hasSessionRow` is false.\n\n`apps/desktop/src/actions/transcriptions.actions.ts` (`importAudioFile`): captures `isPersistenceAllowed()` once, reuses it for the incognito memory-record branch and for the new `hasSessionRow` computation, so the two reads cannot disagree. An incognito import still records a memory row and keeps the session wording; a store-rejected import with persistence allowed now gets the honest wording.\n\nBoth new messages are translated in all ten locales using each catalog's established terms for History, and both keys are pinned in `translation-completeness.test.ts`, including the Korean transcript-wording guard.\n\n## One combined dictation toast\n\n`apps/desktop/src/components/root/DictationSideEffects.tsx`: the two closure helpers in the persist tail became module-level builders, `buildPartialEditFailureToast` and `buildOutcomeFeedbackToast`, plus `combinePostProcessToasts`, which joins messages, keeps error severity if either toast is an error, takes the longer duration, and prefers the edit-failure action. `persist` now shows exactly one toast. `PostProcessToastOptions.duration` became required because every constructor sets it.\n\n## Review round fixes\n\nIncognito memory-record visibility (Sourcery and CodeAnt): `TranscriptionsPage` and `HomeRecentTranscriptions` render from `transcriptions.transcriptionIds`, and the store path in `transcribe.actions.ts` prepends every stored id there, but the import memory-record branch wrote only `transcriptionById`. The branch now prepends the id the same way, and a state test pins that the record is listed and holds the imported transcript.\n\nRejected fallback import (Sourcery): `getPostProcessFeedback` routed any non-null `postProcessError` through `getLocalFallbackFeedback` before context options were considered, so `hasSessionRow: false` was ignored for fallback imports and the snackbar claimed an applied style over a lost transcript. The fallback builder now takes the context and options and returns the unsaved styling-failed message, severity error, no action, for the rejected-import case only. Dictation inserts its text and the History contexts run from a stored row, so those keep the applied-instead wording.\n\nCombined-toast action precedence (Sourcery, design trade): a pill toast carries one action. When an edit failure combines with a fallback outcome, the open-History action wins, because a blocked insertion makes History the only copy of the words, while the settings shortcut stays reachable from the app. The precedence is documented in the combiner and pinned by a test.\n\nComplexity (DeepSource): `getFailedMessageDescriptor` reached cyclomatic complexity 6 and split into the context selector plus `getUnsavedFailedMessageDescriptor`, each below the threshold.\n\n## Second review round fixes\n\nSession-only rows across refresh (Sourcery and CodeAnt): `refreshVisibleTranscriptions` replaces `transcriptionIds` from `listTranscriptions`, which never contains the incognito memory record, so the row the first round listed vanished again on the next History or Home mount. A new registry, `utils/session-only-transcriptions.ts`, marks memory-record ids at creation, and the refresh keeps registered rows the state still holds, each inserted at its created-at position among the persisted ids. A delete removes the record from the state and the refresh drops registered ids no longer present, so deleted rows stay deleted. Three tests pin retention, ordering, and the drop after delete.\n\nRetranscribe gate (Sourcery): the row context menu offered Retranscribe for every row, but the flow loads audio from the repository by id, which memory records do not have. The menu now offers it only where `transcription.audio` exists, the same signal that already gates the audio pill's retranscribe button, which also covers persisted failure rows stored with audio retention off. A new `TranscriptRow.test.ts` case pins the reduced menu.\n\nDeclined (Sourcery): the report that a blank memory record should carry the \"[Transcription Failed]\" marker does not hold. The marker belongs to the dictation path's `transcriptionFailed` flag, which imports never set, and a persisted silent import stores the same blank transcript. The thread records the reasoning.\n\n## Late review edges\n\nClear race (CodeAnt): a local-data clear can open while an import is in flight, and the memory row was written through a bare `produceAppState`, so it could land after the clear wiped the session state and repopulate History. The write now runs under `withLocalDataWriteSync`, whose lease the clear gate blocks, and `hasSessionRow` comes from whether the write actually ran, so a blocked write gets the honest unsaved wording. A test holds an active clear across an incognito import and pins the empty list and the copy.\n\nRegistry pruning (CodeAnt): deleting a memory row left its id in the registry forever, growing the set across import-and-delete cycles and making every later refresh rescan stale ids. `refreshVisibleTranscriptions` now unmarks ids whose record left the state, except while a delete is pending so an undo can restore the row. Two tests pin the prune and the pending-delete retention. A CodeAnt re-review then caught that the prune runs only inside the refresh, so a History page left mounted never prunes and repeated import-and-delete cycles still grow the set; the committed delete now unmarks its id in `pending-transcription-delete.ts` itself, right after the repository delete succeeds, while the undo window keeps the mark alive for a possible restore. A fourth test pins the unmark at commit and the retention during the window.\n\nDeepSource triage: the branch report listed 24 JavaScript findings; 23 sit on lines this diff never touches (the pill event wiring's `void` operators and `useTauriListen` generics, double-bang ref probes, use-before-define notes around the ActivationController closures, callback complexity grades), all baseline patterns of the file, and DeepSource's own gate reports success against the branch baseline. The one finding overlapping this diff, `importAudioFile` at cyclomatic complexity 32 against 31 on base, was the `hasSessionRow` disjunction, which was redundant and removed; the later `hasSessionRow: memoryRowRecorded` form keeps the function at its base complexity.\n\n## Fold checker scope\n\nHuman request during review: `scripts/ci/check-pr-body.mjs` judged every visible line, including the sections review bots append below the MausAgent footer, so an unbolded `## Summary by Sourcery` heading failed R6 after a push and the growing bot tail charged against the unfolded-line budget in R4. The checker now treats the first unfolded footer line as the end of the authored body: lines below it are exempt from every rule except the duplicate-footer count, and a footer hidden inside a fold does not start the exemption, because hiding the footer is itself the R5 defect. Six new tests pin the boundary in both directions, and the AGENTS.md and CONTRIBUTING.md passages that describe the check now state the scope. The fold-check workflow runs the base-branch copy of the checker against a PR body, so this change cannot weaken the gate that judges its own PR; the current body passes both the old checker and the new one.\n\n## Tests updated on purpose\n\nThe existing import test `does not promise History when a failed import was not stored` mocked the store to return no row while persistence stayed allowed, and pinned the session-availability claim. That is precisely the false claim fix 3 removes, so the test was renamed and repinned to the new wording, and sibling tests were added for the incognito path that keeps the old wording. This is a deliberate behavior change, not a test bent to fit code.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>How to test it</summary>\n\n1. Timeout classification: force a post-processing request timeout through an axios-shaped error carrying code `ECONNABORTED` and confirm the failure message says the request timed out, not that it was cancelled. Pinned by `post-process-error-category.test.ts`.\n2. Batch merge: retranscribe two rows concurrently where one reports only fast-style truncation and the other reports a fallback plus the same truncation, and confirm the merged completion toast states the truncation sentence once. Pinned by the new batch test in `transcriptions.actions.test.ts`.\n3. Rejected import: with persistence allowed, make the store reject an import row after a failed or fallback styling run and confirm the snackbar says the transcript could not be saved in History and offers no action. Repeat in incognito mode and confirm the session wording stays. Pinned by five import tests.\n4. Incognito visibility: import audio in incognito mode, open History in the same session, and confirm the imported row is listed. Pinned by the state assertion test.\n5. Combined toast: dictate with a partial edit failure and fast-style truncation or a fallback outcome in the same run and confirm one toast carries both sentences with the open-History action. Pinned by two `DictationSideEffects.test.ts` cases.\n6. Refresh retention: import audio in incognito mode, navigate away and back to History so the visible-list refresh runs, and confirm the imported row is still listed at its created-at position. Delete it and confirm it stays gone across the next refresh. Pinned by three `visible-transcriptions.test.ts` cases.\n7. Retranscribe gate: open the context menu of the incognito memory row and confirm Retranscribe is absent while Copy, Details, and Delete remain. Pinned by the audio-less menu test.\n8. Delete-commit unmark: import audio in incognito mode, delete the memory row, let the undo window expire without a History refresh in between, and confirm the session-only registry is empty. Pinned by the pending-delete commit test.\n9. Fold check: append an unbolded bot-style section below the footer of a scratch body and confirm `pnpm run check:pr-body` passes it, while an unfolded review heading above the footer still fails. Pinned by six new tests in `check-pr-body.test.mjs`.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Changes</summary>\n\n- Files changed: 8 source files, 6 test files, 10 locale catalogs, 1 i18n completeness test, 1 CI checker and its test, 2 docs; 960 insertions, 109 deletions across eight commits.\n- Behaviour or policy changed: user-facing wording in four flows (timeout classification, batch m