+      "description": "## **User description**\n## Summary\n\nWhen post-processing fails, the app now tells you what actually happened and what to do about it, in your language, instead of showing raw provider text.\n\nBefore this change a failed styling request showed the provider's own error string. That text could leak API keys echoed by the provider, name models you cannot act on, and gave no next step. Now every failure is sorted into one of eight plain categories (quota or billing, rate limit, provider request limit, authentication, timeout, cancelled, network, general provider error) and each one gets a localized sentence plus the right action button. The same feedback appears in all four places post-processing runs: live dictation, retranscription from History, audio file import, and the History details dialog.\n\nTwo outcomes that used to blur together are now kept apart. A request that failed but was rescued by the built-in local style is delivered with its styling and explained as \"online styling failed, your local style was applied instead\" with a Fix button that opens the post-processing settings. A request that succeeded but returned an unusable reply keeps your previous text and says so. Retranscription no longer reports that second case as a finished job, and it no longer overwrites text you already had.\n\nThe branch is reconciled with the consolidated base: the base's routed settings pages, partial-edit rejection with its dedicated toasts, concurrent History writes, and automatic edit-retry recovery now coexist with this feedback module, the deferred persist closure, and word-boundary status classification.\n\n<details>\n<summary>What changed, in detail</summary>\n\n## Classification\n\n`classifyPostProcessErrorCategory` now takes the error object itself, not just its message. It reads machine-readable codes first (including codes nested in `cause`, `error`, `response`, `data` and `metadata` wrappers, with cycle protection), then the HTTP status, and only falls back to the message text last. Each structured field is matched on its own, so a marker with a space cannot match across two unrelated fields. A new `providerLimit` category covers provider request and usage limits: an unexplained 429, or an OpenRouter in-flight budget error, no longer claims your credits expired.\n\n## Feedback module\n\nA new `post-process-feedback` module is the single source of user-facing wording for exceptional outcomes. Expected local styling and normal success stay quiet. It picks the sentence from three facts (failed, fell back, recorded reason) and the surface it runs on, so dictation says \"not inserted\", History says \"previous transcript kept\", and import says where the original transcript went. Truncation warnings from fast styling use the same module and only promise History when the row was actually stored.\n\n## Actions\n\nFeedback carries a Fix action (opens the AI post-processing settings) or a History action (opens the transcriptions list). Both travel through the native pill's existing opaque action token, so no pill changes were needed. The duplicate dialog-opening logic that lived in three components is consolidated into `openPostProcessingSettings`.\n\n## Shared provider readers\n\nThe provider-error readers in `@maus-inc/voice-ai` now also read `statusCode` and `response.status` / `response.statusCode` wrappers. Errors that carried their status in those shapes used to classify as unknown and get retried; they now classify correctly. Every affected status (400, 401, 402, 403, 422) is deterministic, so this only stops pointless retries. Covered by new unit tests.\n\n## Translations\n\nAll new messages are translated in all nine non-English locales, and the translation-completeness test now pins every one of them: present in every locale, different from English, and with `{reason}` and `{droppedChars}` placeholders intact. Stale keys from replaced messages are pruned.\n\n## Merge reconciliation\n\nThe latest base merge resolved seventeen conflicting files without dropping either lineage:\n\n- `transcriptions.actions.ts`: the base's completion flow (`completeRetranscribeRun`, `failUnstyledRetranscribeRun`, `handleRetranscribeError`, `wasLocalDataCleared`) and automatic edit-retry recovery stay intact, with the feedback layer grafted in: success feedback combines immediately so the completion toast carries it, `failRetranscribeRun` accepts feedback and surfaces a snackbar action, and the truncation and unstyled messages are joined into one failure detail.\n- `resolveRetranscribedText` keeps the trim-based contract: a whitespace-only previous transcript no longer wins over fresh ASR text, which the base's blank-row retranscription flow depends on.\n- `DictationSideEffects.tsx`: the deferred persist closure survives next to the base's concurrent-history-write path; the pipeline resolves right after the pill goes idle and hands storage, the member refresh, and the feedback toast to a persist step that `stopRecording` fires only after its finally block reopens the stop gate.\n- `ai.utils.ts`: unusable-reply markers stay exported as the single producer/recognizer source and gained the base's `postProcessEdit*` metadata fields.\n- Tests: the base's three concurrent-persistence tests now assert the persist-closure contract (pipeline resolves early, store untouched until the concurrent write settles, handler order preserved), and duplicated hoisted mocks and imports from the auto-merge were removed.\n- Locales: all ten catalogs are the sorted union of both sides at 1184 keys, minus three orphaned keys whose messages the base replaced; the completeness test pins the base's renamed failure-audio message.\n- `SettingsPage.tsx` was deleted by the base's routed-settings rebuild; this branch's settings changes moved to the routed pages' shared action (`openPostProcessingSettings` in `settings.actions.ts`).\n\n## Review performed\n\nApplied the full review protocol from FULL-REVIEW.md, then worked fourteen automated review rounds (CodeRabbit, CodeAnt, SonarQube Cloud, DeepSource, DeepScan, Kilo, cubic):\n\n- Removed one duplicate warning log. The local-fallback event was logged both in the dictation strategy and in DictationSideEffects; the strategy copy (the pre-existing one, where the fallback decision is made) is kept.\n- Restructured the order-sensitive classification cascades and the feedback selectors into ordered rule tables and small helpers, keeping the check order identical while every function stays small.\n- Classified structured provider fields one field at a time instead of substring matching a joined string (CodeRabbit finding); added a regression test for the cross-field case.\n- Evaluated the provider-status widening against its Groq and Cerebras consumers. The new read paths only change errors that previously reported no status, and the change is always toward correct terminal classification. Kept as intended, tested.\n- Confirmed the new classifier keeps the base ordering semantics (quota before authentication, timeout before abort) and that persisted category strings from older builds still resolve.\n- Confirmed extractor idempotence: the i18n extract and sync change nothing on a second run.\n- Fixed the pre-existing stop-gate race on DictationSideEffects that CodeAnt flagged and Kilo repeated as a warning: the pill went idle while the stop gate stayed closed until the audio snapshot and the History row were written, so dictation starts clicked during a slow save were silently dropped. Post-processing now resolves right after the pill goes idle and hands storage, the member refresh, and the feedback toast back as a deferred persist step that stopRecording fires only after its finally block opens the gate. The strategy teardown stays inside the gate, persistence never blocks hold-to-talk restarts, and tests cover the deferred contract and the slow-store race (CodeAnt finding, Kilo warning).\n- 401 and 403 responses now run the message rules (in-flight budget, quota or billing, rate limit) before defaulting to authentication, so a billing failure that arrives under an auth status still routes to the billing guidance (CodeAnt finding).\n- Reworded the two without-History messages: incognito retranscription and import keep the transcript on the session's in-memory History row, so the wording now says it is available in this session but was not saved in History. New message ids across all nine locale catalogs (CodeAnt finding).\n- Two batch rows completing with feedback now merge into one completion toast instead of the second overwriting the first (CodeAnt finding).\n- The details dialog translates only the known unusable-reply markers into explanatory feedback; the markers are exported from ai.utils so producer and recognizer share one source, and unrelated warnings such as the glossary note stay verbatim (CodeAnt and CodeRabbit findings).\n- Removed redundant optional chaining on PostProcessResult.metadata and the strategy postProcessMetadata, which the types declare as required, resolving the eleven DeepScan insufficient-null-check findings (DeepScan finding).\n- Kept every touched function under DeepSource's cyclomatic-complexity threshold by splitting the dialog's warning mapping into small helpers (DeepSource findings).\n- Added the browser fetch rejection wording to the network markers: a Chromium WebView network outage carries no network keyword itself and used to classify as a generic provider error. Covered by regression tests for the bare TypeError and the SDK-wrapped shape (CodeAnt finding).\n- Consolidated the History navigation into one shared action in settings.actions, used by both the pill toast handler and the retranscription snackbar, and the success snackbar now reuses the shared feedback-to-action mapping instead of a hand-built copy (CodeRabbit finding).\n- Deduplicated identical per-row sentences in the merged retranscription batch toast: a batch whose rows all report the same outcome now shows that sentence once, pinned by a regression test (CodeRabbit finding).\n- Aligned the Spanish, Portuguese, and Italian raw-transcript and post-processing wording with each locale's established terminology (CodeRabbit finding).\n- Split the deferred persistence closure's toast shaping into small helpers, dropping its cyclomatic complexity from 10 to 5, and ended the remaining ignored surfacing promise in the toast action handler with a catch handler (DeepSource findings).\n- A gateway timeout (504) now classifies as the timeout category: the generic 5xx fallback ran before the explicit status map, making the 504 entry dead code. The map wins now, and a test pins 504 to the timeout wording while an unmapped 5xx stays a provider error (CodeRabbit finding).\n- Documented that structured-field matching is substring-based within a field on purpose, because provider codes are compound strings; rule order carries the tie-breaks (Kilo suggestion).\n- Documented the non-null metadata posture in the dictation side effects, keeping the optional chaining removed that DeepScan required (Kilo suggestion).\n- Gave the classification cascade and feedback selector shared priority rules and an exhaustiveness check so a category can no longer silently miss a table (cubic findings), extracted a dialog warning discriminator so the two marker sets stay distinct (cubic finding), ended every detached main-window surfacing promise through one helper with an encapsulated catch (cubic finding), hoisted the repeated raw-transcript column literal to a module constant (cubic nit), and pinned the truncation message to a distinguishable digit sequence in tests (cubic nit).\n- Rejected two cubic findings with evidence in-thread: the save-failure early-return claim read a variable set after the branch (the code is correct as written), and the literal-equality status discriminator would have broken the documented word-boundary contract (the digit-sequence assertion pins it instead).\n- Made the two HTTP-status rules that read prose match on word boundaries, so a request id or byte count containing 401 or 403 no longer routes a message-text match (CodeRabbit finding); the structured status rules stay exact.\n- Guarded the serial History write inside the deferred persist step: a rejected store used to reject the whole step, which the stop path only logs, so the transcript was neither inserted nor saved and the user got no message. The failure is now caught like the concurrent path catches its own, historyEntryAvailable stays false, and the not-saved feedback and the member refresh still run; a regression test pins the order and the wording (CodeRabbit Major finding, DeepSource complexity finding on the same function).\n- Dropped five redundant optional chains on postProcessResult.metadata in the incognito memory record, which the type declares required, and settled three more mixed optional-then-bare access patterns in two toast-action helpers and the dialog warning memo (DeepScan findings).\n- Joined the established History term in both split catalogs: the new German truncation message says Verlauf, and the six new zh-TW messages use the term the styling-failure copy already used; pre-existing base entries keep their own wording (CodeRabbit findings).\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>How to test it</summary>\n\n1. Point post-processing at an unreachable or quota-exhausted provider, then dictate. Expect \"Styling failed because...\" with the matching reason and, in History, the raw transcript saved.\n2. Use a tone with a built-in fast style while the provider is down. Expect styled text inserted and \"Online styling failed because ... Your local style was applied instead.\" with a Fix button that opens the post-processing settings.\n3. Retranscribe a History row with the provider down. Expect the row to keep its previous text, the snackbar to explain why, and no claim that retranscription finished.\n4. Import an audio file with the provider down and check the snackbar names the reason and says whether History has the raw transcript.\n5. Open a failed row in the History details dialog and check the warning shows the localized reason, not a category constant.\n6. Repeat in a non-English locale to see the translated wording.\n7. Stop a dictation and immediately start the next one while the first transcript is still saving. The second recording starts at once and the first transcript still lands in History.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Changes</summary>\n\n- Files changed: 36 against the base. Desktop actions (`post-process-error-category`, `post-process-feedback`, `transcriptions.actions`, `settings.actions`, `toast.actions`, `transcribe.actions` plus their tests), dictation strategy and side effects plus tests, `AppSideEffects`, `TranscriptionDetailsDialog` plus test, `PostProcessingDisabledTooltip`, `ai.utils`, `window.utils`, `toast.types`, the i18n completeness test, ten locale catalogs, and `packages/voice-ai/src/provider-error.utils` plus test.\n- Behaviour or policy changed: user-facing post-processing failure, fallback, truncation and unusable-reply feedback across dictation, retranscription, import and the History details dialog; deferred persistence so dictation restarts during slow saves; wider provider-status reads that stop pointless retries.\n- Packages touched under `packages/` that need a rebuild before consumers see the change: `@maus-inc/voice-ai` (root `pnpm run build` rebuilds it; no separate publish step in this repo).\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Code research gate</summary>\n\n- Queries searched: `surfaceMainWindow` call sites (detached-promise class), `AUTHENTICATION` / status-marker literals in classification (word-boundary class), `sanitizedTranscript` read and write sites (column-literal class), `String.raw` convention in scripts/ci (regex-source class), post-process marker producers vs recognizers (shared-source class), persist-closure callers and `pendingPersistence` references (deferred-contract class), styling and raw-transcript term families across the six Latin-script locales (terminology class).\n- Sources, with the assumption each verified: repo files at both merge sides (each conflict resolved against the originating side's original file, not the auto-merge result); SonarCloud issues API (0 open issues on the head); GitHub jobs and check-runs API metadata (CI parity where raw logs were unreachable); base branch git log 0aec307..f21a87d (which base features had to survive the merge).\n- Pattern class named, plus every other location where the same root cause could recur: substring-matched codes in prose. Fixed at the two prose rules (401, 403); audited the remaining status rules and confirmed 429, 402, 407, 408 and 504 match structured fields or run inside the message-rule table where order carries the tie-breaks, so no other site needs boundaries.\n- Edge cases considered and their disposition: whitespace-only previous transcript (trim contract restored, pinned by test); batch rows with identical outcomes (deduplicated, pinned); concurrent History write during a completed pipeline (base tests adapted to the persist-closure contract); unusable reply with and without prior text (keeps prior text, else explains); incognito surfaces (without-History wording, pinned across locales); 504 vs unmapped 5xx (map wins vs fallback stays, pinned).\n- Failed attempts: an early-return fix proposed for the save-failure path was reverted after the premise proved false; a literal-equality status discriminator was rejected because it would break the word-boundary contract; inlining the persistence await in the main function was evaluated and rejected because it would defer the member refresh and toast ordering the base tests assert.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proactive and deep refinement evidence</summary>\n\nProactive protocol.\n\n- Intent hypothesis: failures must explain themselves in the user's language and offer 