+      "description": "## Summary\n\nReorganizes settings into routed sections with a persistent rail and Overview; adds device-local profile editing and moves Clear local data to Account. Also includes accessibility, localization, recovery, local-data safety, and CI fixes from review and integration with the base branch.\n\nVisual evidence and its limitations: [.qa/pr288/README.md](https://github.com/maus-inc/mausVoice/blob/arena/4721d0b9-mausvoice/.qa/pr288/README.md). The captures do not establish exhaustive coverage of the requested 140+ edge cases and 100 issue-derived cases.\n\n## Verification gate\n\n- Local merge verification passed format, root typecheck, lint, build, secret scan, skills verification, desktop unit tests, i18n sync, Rust formatting, bindings, clippy and Rust library tests (407 passed, 1 ignored).\n- On 60ef0c5, desktop builds and lint passed on Linux, macOS and Windows; desktop unit and integration tests, Rust tests, i18n, SonarCloud and the folded-body check passed. Focused history recovery and visible-list tests, desktop lint and typecheck passed locally on 08d7a56; CI on that newer head is pending.\n- Native microphone/WebView timing, real IME and screen-reader behavior remain unverified. Full workspace integration/evaluation tests require a GROQ API key.\n- DeepSource JavaScript failed on 60ef0c5 and also on base commit fe060ab. Its dashboard reports 734 introduced issues and warns that the stacked-branch baseline may be inaccurate; the blocking finding is not identifiable from the public report. This is not proof every finding is pre-existing. Do not treat historical green CI claims below as current status.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Review history preserved below; all 235 threads were paginated and were resolved at the time of the latest check.\n- [ ] Confirm all current CI and bot reviews on the new head.\n- [ ] Complete outstanding native and visual verification.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Historical description and review records (point-in-time; superseded where noted above)</summary>\n\n## **User description**\n## What changed?\n\nSettings used to be one long scrolling page with a section rail that highlighted whatever happened to be under the cursor. Finding a setting meant scrolling and hoping, \"Privacy and data\" held both the incognito switch and the button that wipes your machine, and there was no way to change your own name or picture. This pull request gives every settings section its own route with a rail that stays put, adds search that lands on the exact row, and rebuilds the Account section around one profile dialog where you set a photo and edit your name. Clear local data moves out of Privacy and data into the Account danger zone, next to Delete account.\n\nThe Account dialog is a single popup that does one job: a photo you can set, replace, or remove, and a name field. It follows the profile-edit modal conventions collected from Dribbble and Pinterest (centred photo cluster with a camera affordance on hover, flat 1px-bordered surfaces, one primary Save that only activates once something changed, errors under the field they belong to), and it was checked against Material's dialog guidance and Apple's HIG for the wording and the failure paths.\n\nTo test it: open Settings from the rail (or `Cmd+,`) and click through the seven sections. Search for a setting from the rail search box and press Enter: it should open the right page and flash the row it found. Open Account, click the Name row, set a photo, change the name, save, and confirm the picture appears in the title bar chip and survives a restart. Then check the danger zone: Clear local data asks you to type `clear`, lists what it removes, and afterwards the photo, tips and settings are gone while you stay signed in.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\n- [x] Automated tests\n- [x] Manual verification\n- [x] Code inspection\n\n<details>\n<summary>Details (click to expand)</summary>\n\n### What was built\n\n**Information architecture.** Seven routes under `/dashboard/settings` (`account`, `dictation`, `ai-models`, `appearance`, `shortcuts`, `privacy-data`, `system`), plus an index and a wildcard redirect, so a stored link from the old single-page layout still lands somewhere useful instead of dropping to the default. `apps/desktop/src/components/settings/settings-registry.ts` is the one list of setting keys, the page each belongs to, its anchor id and its availability rule; the rail, the page headers, the search results and the tests all read from it. `settings-routes.ts` owns the base path, anchor ids, the legacy `?section=` map, the last-visited page and `?setting=` resolution. The command palette entry and the onboarding tips now deep-link to the row they name rather than the top of a page.\n\n**Account.** `ProfileDialog` sets, changes and removes a profile photo and edits the display name in one dialog, with a live crop preview, per-field validation and a Save that stays disabled until something actually changed. The photo is downscaled to a 256px square, re-encoded as JPEG (or PNG when the image has transparency), and stored as a data URL in local state keyed by user id: `setMyProfileImage` writes it, `useMyProfileImage` reads it, and there is no account column and no round trip because it never leaves the device. Clear local data keeps its typed `clear` confirmation and its list of consequences, and now also removes the app's own localStorage keys, which is where the photo, the dismissed tips and the account anchors live; it deliberately does not touch the sign-in session.\n\n**Shared parts.** `SettingRow` / `SettingGroup` / `SettingRowControls` replace the two bespoke row implementations that had drifted apart, so a row's height, radius, icon size, description colour and action shape are decided once. `SettingsLayout` owns the rail, the deep-link highlight, the sticky header and the search results panel. `ConsequenceList` is shared by both destructive dialogs, so each of them names what it removes in the same shape before it is armed. The theme gains a destructive contained variant and a `level2` surface so the danger zone stops hardcoding red fills and corner radii.\n\n### What was reviewed\n\nThe diff went through the `FULL-REVIEW.md` protocol: change inventory, boundary traces on the destructive paths (what Clear local data removes and what it refrains from removing), contract review, a test-validity review, and the verification matrix. Six independent pre-push repasses over the change set found and fixed: a dangling `SettingsPage` import path after the delete, radius literals that `ui-lint` bans, a destructive button that rendered in the neutral primary fill in dark mode, 22 new catalogue keys that had been extracted in English into all nine locales, and prose in the docs that still told people to look under Privacy and data.\n\n### Verification results (local, at the pushed head)\n\nThree passes have gone over this diff: the build, a first review round, and a second round that sharpened the parts which had defaulted to a generic shape. Each round's findings are fixed in a commit of their own, and every review thread on the PR is answered in-thread. CI is green on the head.\n\n| Gate | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| prettier repo-wide `format:check` | passed |\n| desktop `check-types` | passed |\n| desktop `lint` (prettier + oxlint + ui-lint) | 719 files, 0 warnings, 0 errors |\n| desktop unit tests | 3088/3088 (233 files) |\n| i18n extract and sync | 1064 keys, 0 added, all 9 locales |\n| docs `check-types` / `build` | passed / 101 pages |\n| preview package tests | 22/22 |\n| voice-ai package tests | 501/501 |\n| CI script suites (`run-with-diagnostics`, `dev-surface-contracts`, `pr28-contracts`, `updater-manifest`, `build-changelog`, `release-shell-contracts`) | 14, 2, 117, 19, 11, 32 tests, all passing |\n| `turbo run build --filter=desktop^...` | 6/6 tasks |\n| desktop integration tests | 2 GROQ_API_KEY-gated failures, a pre-existing sandbox condition (no key here, CI injects the secret, and the diff touches no integration file) |\n| Rust clippy and bindings check | no Rust diff in this PR and no toolchain in the sandbox; CI runs them and this note stays here until the jobs are green |\n\n### Out of scope\n\nNo Rust, IPC, migration, workflow, or dependency changes. No new Tauri commands. The account record is unchanged: the photo is deliberately device-local state, not a synced field.\n\n</details>\n\n\n## Follow-up pass: a quick-access Overview on the settings surface\n\nThe routed-pages work was approved, then held against the standard PR #296 codifies. One thing it had not fixed: the surface still opened on a list of things to configure, while the two questions people actually open settings to answer — which key does what, and which model is running — each cost a page hop. This pass adds the Overview that answers them and tightens the parts of our own language it exposed.\n\n**What the Overview is.** `/dashboard/settings/home`, titled **Overview** in the rail and first in it, is where a first-ever visit lands (returning users keep landing on the page they left). It is two cards. **Shortcuts** lists the keys you press while dictating — in the shortcuts dialog's own order and wording, with each row's combo read through the dialog's own selector, so platform defaults show even before a key is customised, and a cleared key reads \"Not set\". **Your models** reports the effective transcription, post-processing and assistant choices, naming the provider or local model and where it runs. Every row is a summary: it deep-links to `page#setting-<key>` and carries no anchor, no search entry and no registry identity of its own — those stay with the row that owns the setting.\n\n**The reference, weighed rather than copied.** The target look (`SpeakoFlow` `assets/readme/screens/home.webp`) pairs a shortcuts card with a models card. Three of its habits were deliberately left out, because the state behind them does not exist here: its \"Hold to talk / Tap to toggle\" control (no push-to-talk preference anywhere in state or actions), its \"Voice\" row (no text-to-speech path) and its accuracy/speed dot meters (no such data). Inventing a control to match a screenshot is precisely the defaults-slip this pass exists to remove. What carried over is what our world already had: the keycap cluster as the row's value, an icon tile leading each model row, and a titled card with one trailing affordance — expressed as our own footer rows instead of the reference's header link.\n\n**Natured into our language, and tightened where ours was loose.**\n\n- The group card drops from radius 2 to the app's card step; a settings card had been sitting at a large dialog's radius.\n- Rows gained the press state the design baseline asks for (`&:active`), which they were missing beside hover and focus-visible.\n- Trailing chevrons were MUI's 20px rounded glyph; they are now lucide at the sanctioned 1.9 stroke, tinted by the row rather than by the icon.\n- State words are borrowed from the pages that own them: \"Not configured\" is the AI models page's chip, \"Off\" is the mode state, \"Not downloaded yet\" the local-model state — one state, one name.\n- A row exists for work the app can do; a mode that is off says so. Hiding Assistant mode would read as a missing feature. The style-cycling keys do come and go, because those actions exist only while styling is manual.\n- The icons of the two pages these cards hand off to (Shortcuts, AI models) moved to the same family in the same pass, because a card whose chevron is one family and whose destination is another is the drift this pass removes. The remaining settings dialogs and pickers keep their MUI icons and are named in Out of scope.\n\n**Where the shared code went.** `SettingRow.tsx` was 663 lines; the chassis both row kinds are built from now lives in `setting-row-chassis.tsx` and the unanchored summary row in `SettingSummaryRow.tsx`, leaving 417 lines in the original. The shared style constants are typed `SystemStyleObject<Theme>` rather than `SxProps`: an `SxProps` value is the whole union — object, callback or array — and TypeScript refuses an imported one as an element of the array form `sx={[a, b]}`, which is how the two row kinds compose them.\n\n**Review rounds on this pass.** Automated findings, each verified against the source before anything was written. Round one: DeepSource put `SettingSummaryRow` at cyclomatic complexity 8, which was right about where the weight sat — the component was deciding both which kind of target it had and whether that target still existed while doing its own rendering — so the decision moved into `summaryTarget` and the component now asks one question before rendering; and SonarCloud caught that the page's nested ternary had only been half flattened.\n\nRound two, on the pushed head:\n\n- CodeRabbit's one actionable comment was real and is fixed in `7165d9f`. The model rows said \"Cloud\" for every API-backed provider, including the three whose endpoint is a URL — Ollama, Speaches and OpenAI-compatible servers, usually on loopback — so the one surface someone opens to check where their text goes was claiming a round trip that never happens. `getProviderLocation` now reads the configured base URL, and the row says \"On this computer\", \"Cloud\", or stops at the provider's name when the URL cannot be read, because a row that cannot tell must not guess.\n- The same rows report \"Not configured\" for a selection the runtime would not dispatch to at all — no key behind it, a provider this build cannot name, a mode that does not dispatch. A row must not describe a request the app never makes, and the page that owns the setting is one link away. `HomeSettingsPage.test.tsx` pins both halves.\n- Found while fixing the above: deleting the account ran only the native half of the local wipe. The database and the audio went; the profile photo — a data URL in `localStorage`, outside the database that wipe empties — stayed, so an account recreated on the same device came back wearing the deleted account's face. Both flows now share `utils/local-data.utils.ts` (`d49a751`), which also fixes the ordering the persist middleware punishes (the in-memory slice is reset *before* storage is cleared, or the next state change writes the old slice straight back out). The deletion's reporting was split for the same reason: only `deleteMyAccount` failing means the deletion failed, so a wipe that cannot finish now says what is still on the device instead of claiming an account that no longer exists could not be deleted.\n- A cold-launch `?setting=` link to a key-list row was being thrown away because three availability flags read a key list that had not loaded yet and reported `false`. An absent flag already means unknown, and unknown reads as available, so those three flags are now computed only once `apiKeysStatus` says the list has arrived (`c67e5c0`).\n- Findings that describe behaviour already in place were refuted in-thread rather than \"fixed\": a radius literal `ui-lint` bans, an `openUrl` call that already catches and logs, an empty group heading that is gated, and the remembered last settings page that already reads before hash handling.\n\n**Tests.** `HomeSettingsPage.test.tsx` pins the two cards, the bound/unbound combo wording, the manual-styling gate, the local-model and provider states, and both kinds of link target. `SettingSummaryRow.test.tsx` pins that a summary never carries a `setting-` anchor, that a page row navigates, and that a summary whose setting is no longer registered renders nothing. The route test now names the landing page through the registry, so which page holds that spot is decided in one place.\n\n### References cited for this pass\n\n| Reference | What was taken |\n| --- | --- |\n| `SpeakoFlow` `assets/readme/screens/home.webp` — the target look | Two-card layout, keycap cluster as a row value, icon-tile model rows, one trailing affordance per card |\n| `SpeakoFlow` `assets/readme/screens/models.webp` | \"Runs on · On this computer / Cloud\" as the words for where a model executes; its fabricated accuracy/speed meters were **not** taken |\n| macOS System Settings keyboard panes (lifewire / tidbits captures) | Action name left, key cluster right, and no control the OS does not have |\n| Raycast settings captures (manual.raycast.com) | Dense rows, label left and value right, one hairline per boundary |\n| `qa-artifacts/pr288` — `288-cmp-settings-surface.png`, `288-after-settings-*.png` | The committed world: rail width, card radius, 56px rows, 240px control column — what every measured change here was weighed against |\n| `apps/desktop/DESIGN.md`, `apps/desktop/docs/design-system-baseline.md` | Radius ladder (7 / 14 / 28 / 999), lucide at stroke 1.9 as the only icon family, press + focus-visible states, tokens over literals |\n| PR #296's `UI design recon` instructions | The process this pass follows: recon premium sources and qa artifacts, weigh every radius, density, state and label against the committed world, natify rather than copy, cite what was used |\n\nThe pass's working notes — the full weighed table, the deliberate omissions, and the decisions — are kept as `design-recon-home.md` alongside this PR's branch work; the decisions above are the durable record.\n\n### This pass's verification (local, at the pushed head)\n\n| Gate | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| desktop `check-types` | passed |\n| desktop `lint` (prettier + oxlint + ui-lint) | 728 files, 0 warnings, 0 errors |\n| desktop unit tests | 3112/3112 across 238 files |\n| i18n extract + sync | 1077 keys, no-op on re-run; the two new strings translated in all nine catalogues |\n| preview dev server | page and row modules compile and serve (HTTP 200); no browser exists in this sandbox, so visual capture of the new page is the one step left and is not claimed here |\n\nTwo notes on the gates. The root `check-types` also type-checks `@maus-inc/preview`, which fails on `apps/preview/src/demos/buttons.tsx` (`variant=\"blue\"` is not a MUI variant); that file was last touched by the base commit `86dc7c3` and is outside this diff, so the desktop workspace is the one this pass reports on. And no browser exists in this sandbox: the new page's visual sign-off comes from the preview server or from a screenshot, and is not claimed here.\n\n### Review round: every open thread answered, and the findings that were real\n\nThe review round on this branch raised 24 findings across CodeAnt, CodeRabbit, Kilo, DeepSource and SonarCloud. Each one was checked against the code at the head, answered in its thread, and the thread resolved. Thirteen were real and are fixed in the commits below; the rest are answered with the reason they do not apply.\n\nWhat was actually wrong, in one line each:\n\n| Finding | Disposition |\n| --- | --- |\n| Saves landing out of order lost the newer draft (`DictationSettingsPage`) | fixed `1f94064`, then again `136b896` for a reversion the first fix still dropped, then `ca22d95` for the cap that reintroduced it, `43d3b32` and `2d0780b` for the test that was meant to catch it |\n| `deleteMyAccount()` is a no-op in personal mode, so the dialog claimed a deletion | fixed `54e8ae5`: the consequences and the toast now describe what this device wipes, and the confirmation is a typed phrase |\n| An endpoint the build cannot place was labelled \"Cloud\" | fixed `69fc179` |\n| A JPEG whose size sits behind a long EXIF block skipped the pixel guard entirely | fixed `f0fd52e` |\n| A wipe that failed on the audio step left the profile photo in `localStorage` | fixed `fd7c554` |\n| A second window could write its stale local slice back after a wipe | fixed `dcd72e2` |\n| The dictation limit row disappeared for good if the key-list request failed | fixed `be3f370` |\n| A search hit for a row already in the address bar neither lit up nor scrolled | fixed `47f5b24` |\n| A header parse error escaped the picker's error handling as a rejection | fixed `f0fd52e`, `fdcd776` |\n| Three SonarCloud findings in the new avatar and wipe code | fixed `fdcd776`, `f0fd52e`, `3f2cf48` |\n| The two Chinese catalogues told people to retry a deletion that had already happened | fixed `4d281a3` |\n\nTwo structural changes came out of the same round, because the fixes took two files past the repository's file limit: reading an image's declared size is now `src/utils/image-size.utils.ts` with its own tests, and the draft-preserving number field is `src/hooks/use-saved-number-field.ts` — the same state machine the page previously carried twice.\n\nAnswers that were not changes, for the record: `mockResolvedValue(undefined)` stays (dropping the argument fails `check-types` with TS2554), `await act(async () => …)` stays (the async callback is what flushes the click's microtasks), the second window's scan window is capped in bytes rather than `file.size` (a slice beyond the file returns the file), and an `AggregateError` for two failed wipe steps was declined because every caller collapses both into the same message.\n\n### Verification at `737ab69`\n\n| Gate | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| desktop `check-types` | passed, 0 errors |\n| desktop `lint` (prettier + oxlint + ui-lint) | 0 warnings, 0 errors |\n| desktop unit tests | 3134/3134 across 240 files |\n| i18n extract + sync | 1075 keys, no-op on re-run; new strings translated in all nine catalogues |\n| SonarCloud (PR 288) | 0 open issues |\n| GitHub checks | builds (Linux/Windows/macOS), lint, unit, integration, Rust, i18n, CodeAnt, CodeRabbit, DeepScan, Socket: all passing |\n| preview dev server | `/dashboard/settings/home` and `/dashboard/settings/account` serve 200; no browser exists in this sandbox, so the visual pass is by screenshot or the preview |\n\nThree fixes are proven by mutation rather than by assertion count: reverting the draft guard, the fill-byte step, or the stop-failure report each makes its own test fail.\n\n### Rounds after that table, ending at `ceb38bb`\n\nSonarCloud's remaining finding was one convention fix: the caught error shadows the field's `error` state, and the checkout already spells that case `error_` (`generate-text.repo.ts`, `useTonePreview.ts`) — `21f16f5`, after which the PR has 0 open issues.\n\nTwo review claims were checked against the format spec rather than taken on trust, and both were wrong in the other direction. The WebP offsets the review called critical read a size out of the reserved field (VP8X) and out of the start code (VP8, lossy). `image-size.utils.test.ts` now builds both variants — a 300x200 extended canvas, a 640x480 lossy frame — and reads them back with the offsets in this PR: the suggested ones give `1x300` and `413x42`. Neither variant had a fixture before, so they were worth having.\n\nThe new hook had no tests, and `use-saved-number-field.test.tsx` found a defect the moment it ran: the seeding effect listed `format` in its dependencies, so any caller passing an inline formatter had the draft written back over after every keystroke — the failure the hook exists to prevent, on a path the page's two stable formatters never take. The formatter is read through a ref now; keying the effect back on it fails four of the seven cases.\n\n`ceb38bb` carries no tree change. It re-runs the secret scan after the `Install Gitleaks` step failed downloading the release asset — `release-assets.githubusercontent.com`, which is unreachable from the authoring sandbox too — where every earlier run of that workflow on this branch passed, one of them four minutes earlier. The build, lint and test workflows are path-filtered, so they do not re-run on an empty commit; the tree is byte-identical to `21f16f5`, where all of them passed.\n\n| Gate at `ceb38bb` / `21f16f5` | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| desktop `check-types` | 0 errors |\n| desktop `lint` (prettier + oxlint + ui-lint) | 0 warnings, 0 errors |\n| desktop unit tests | 3143/3143 across 241 files |\n| SonarCloud (PR 288) | 0 open issues |\n| unresolved review threads | 0, checked in both directions of the thread list |\n\n### The last three findings, and the last three commits\n\n| Finding | Disposition |\n| --- | --- |\n| A successful key save showed a hard-coded English snackbar | fixed `6a0b16d`: the provider table carries a `MessageDescriptor` and formats it at the call site, translated in all ten catalogues |\n| A stop failure disappeared when the native wipe also failed | fixed `2fec8d7`: one throw now carries both messages, in the order the steps ran |\n| `useOwnWrites` had a duplicate doc block | already gone: the hook moved to `src/hooks/use-saved-number-field.ts` in `1dcc651`, where its documentation is a single block |\n| The rapid-commit test said \"ten\" over nine values | already fixed in `43d3b32`: the values are built by the loop and the count is derived from them |\n| The locale-aware snackbar had no test holding it there | `PersonalApiKeyDialog.test.tsx` added in `e9ec6f1`: four cases rendered in German, two of which fail if the confirmation goes back to the table's English text |\n| Two \"hardcoded spacing\" values in `ConsequenceList.tsx` | not applied — `px: 2` and `py: 1.5` are MUI spacing multipliers (`theme.spacing`), not `2px` and `1.5px`. There are 367 numeric spacing props across this app and no `--spacing-*` tokens anywhere in it; the design doc's \"4px base spacing rhythm\" is exactly what `theme.spacing(2)` and `spacing(1.5)` give. The suggested names do not exist to switch to. |\n\n| Gate at `e9ec6f1` | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| desktop `check-types` | 0 errors |\n| desktop `lint` (prettier + oxlint + ui-lint) | 0 warnings, 0 errors, `ui-lint ok` |\n| desktop unit tests | 3148/3148 across 242 files |\n| SonarCloud (PR 288) | 0 open issues |\n| review threads | 138 of 138 resolved; none open |\n| CodeRabbit | approved at `6a0b16d` |\n\n### The 14:00 review, and the refinement pass it arrived with\n\n| Finding | Disposition |\n| --- | --- |\n| The key field took focus from the dialog's open effect, which runs before its content mounts | fixed `381d251`: focus moved to `slotProps.transition.onEntered`, where the ref exists. The comment records the two things that do not work — the effect (ref is null, MUI's focus trap is left holding the paper) and `autoFocus` (fires as markup, cannot be deferred) |\n| `useSavedNumberField` seeded the field from an outside change but left `requested.current` at the value the store held when the field mounted | fixed `381d251`: an outside change moves the baseline with the field, so typing the older number back in commits it instead of returning early while the store keeps the newer one. The same ref had the sibling case: a write in flight spent by the wrong arrival, so `record`/`claim` now count per value rather than listing them in a set |\n| The German \"Manual\" styling mode read \"Handbuch\" (a printed handbook), and the verbatim example translated the phrases the feature matches | fixed `381d251` in all ten catalogues: \"Manuell\", and the trigger phrases stay the words the person actually says. The same two mistakes were corrected wherever else they appeared in the translated locales |\n| A zero width or height counted as a measured image size | fixed `381d251`: an unresolved measurement is unresolved |\n\nAll three threads are answered and resolved; the replies name the commit that carries each fix.\n\n### Refining the Overview: quick access, not a summary of every row\n\nThe Overview reported the right settings and looked like a form while doing it: shortcut labels repeated from the dialog, model rows stacking a title, a model name and a location on one line, a \"see all\" row at the bottom of each card that was not a setting, and a rail whose selected item was painted as a box the rest of the app never uses.\n\n| What changed | Why |\n| --- | --- |\n| Shortcut copy lives in `shortcut-copy.tsx`, read by the shortcuts dialog and by the card | a key named on one surface and described on another is two chances to disagree |\n| The card shows the four keys someone reaches for without opening settings — dictation, cancel, add to dictionary, and the chat window only when assistant mode means there is one to open | a key that opens nothing is not a shortcut, it is a question the person then has to answer |\n| Explanations moved behind the info glyph on the row, on hover | \"too many subtexts\" was a second line under every row; the label says what the key is, the tooltip says what it does |\n| The models card is the settings chassis with an eyebrow line, using the AI models page's own words for the label and its own line for the answer | one row shape for the whole surface, and one name per setting |\n| \"All shortcuts\" and \"Manage models\" moved onto their card's heading | the rows of a card are settings; a row that opens a page reads as one of them when it shares their chassis |\n| The two cards are centred in the surface | the settings layout now claims the rest of the scroll box (`minHeight: 100%` on the surface, `alignSelf: stretch` + a column on the content, `flexGrow: 1` on the page), so a page can use space that is genuinely spare |\n| Appearance's second segment is no longer clipped | the shared control column is a column width for values that line up down a list, not a budget for what a control may draw — a control-bearing row now sizes to its content |\n| The rail's selected item is colour and weight, not a box | the theme's language for a selected nav item, and the nav no longer reads as a separate surface |\n| The account email and plan rows are gone | they were the two rows the account page should not carry |\n\n`c1d7bfc` prunes the catalogue entries those rows leave behind: `i18n:extract`'s prune step was no longer idempotent against the committed catalogue, which is what failed the hygiene job on the first push of this pass.\n\n### The 16:00 review of the refinement pass\n\n| Finding | Disposition |\n| --- | --- |\n| `contentControlColumnSx` still inherited `maxWidth: \"60%\"`, so a control wider than the cap was clipped even with room to spare | fixed `6d38d50`: the cap is the label's floor and stays at `xs`, where the row has nothing to spare, and is lifted from `sm` up. A value that grows has an ellipsis to say so; a control cannot shorten its own text, so there the cap can only clip |\n| The tooltip was attached to the glyph beside the title, which cannot be focused — a keyboard user never opened the explanation | fixed `6d38d50`: the tooltip now wraps the row through the shared `ConditionalTooltip` with `describeChild`, and `HomeSettingsPage.test.tsx` opens it from the row and reads it back through `aria-describedby` (the assertion fails on a null `aria-describedby` without the wrapper) |\n| `useSavedNumberField` could keep a claim for a write that lands on the value the store already holds | fixed `e16a596`, after the reviewer walked through the case that made my first answer wrong: 7 → 10 → 7 leaves a claim, and a later external 42 → 7 is then read as ours while the field keeps showing 42. The write knows at the moment it is issued that it is asking for what the store already holds, so the claim is retired when the save resolves. The test walks the same table and fails on `expected '42' to be '7'` without it |\n\n| Gate at `c1d7bfc` | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| desktop `check-types` | 0 errors |\n| desktop `lint` (prettier + oxlint + ui-lint) | 0 warnings, 0 errors, `ui-lint ok` |\n| desktop unit tests | 3154/3154 across 242 files |\n| `i18n:extract` | idempotent — a second run changes nothing |\n| SonarCloud (PR 288) | quality gate passed: 0 open issues, 0.0% duplicated lines on new code |\n| Format and i18n Hygiene | success |\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n<a href=\"https://cubic.dev/pr/maus-inc/mausVoice/pull/288?utm_source=github\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"