+      "description": "## **User description**\n## **User description**\nShort dictations no longer wait for the post-processing provider. When post-processing is on and you dictate a word, a sentence, or about two sentences in one of the five prose styles (Polished, Chat, Concise, Formal, Prompt), the app now styles it on your own computer with the same deterministic transforms it already ran when post-processing was off. That path is instant and never contacts the provider. Everything else still uses the provider: longer dictations, Email, Bullets, Notes, custom styles, and Verbatim. A new switch under **Settings -> AI and processing -> Fast styling for short dictations** turns the shortcut off, and it is on by default.\n\nThe local transforms also learned the written forms the provider prompt asks a model for, so a short dictation that stays on the device no longer reads worse than the same text sent away. Spoken dates, times, numbers, currency and percentages are written out; spoken email addresses and domains are joined; self-corrections without punctuation are handled; and weekdays and month names are capitalized.\n\nThis branch is based on `arena/0.1.6-consolidation`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>What changed, in plain terms</summary>\n\n**A new decision point.** `apps/desktop/src/utils/post-processing-routing.utils.ts` decides whether one dictation is styled locally or sent to the provider. It reads four things: the transcript, the active style, the new preference, and the dictation language. A dictation counts as short when it is at most two sentences, at most thirty words, and at most 200 characters. The 200 character bound is what holds for languages that do not separate words with spaces. The language must be English: the transforms delete English filler words, and \"um\" is a preposition in German.\n\n**Only prose styles take the shortcut.** Polished, Chat, Concise, Formal, and Prompt are edited in place by the transforms. Email, Bullets, and Notes restructure the text, and a custom style is a prompt only a model can follow, so those keep the provider path even for a short dictation. Verbatim is returned unchanged before either path runs.\n\n**The shortcut is safe to fail.** If the local transform produces nothing (input that is only filler, such as \"um uh er\"), the action sends the provider request it had skipped instead of delivering a blank or raw transcript.\n\n**A setting, on by default.** The preference is stored in the database, defaults to on for existing rows (migration 91 adds the column with a default of 1), and is translated in all ten locales. The switch only appears while post-processing is on, because with post-processing off every built-in style already used the local path.\n\n**The transforms grew written forms.** `apps/desktop/src/utils/inverse-text-normalization.utils.ts` writes spoken numbers, dates, times, currency, and percentages, and it is deliberately shy: years (\"nineteen eighty four\"), decimals (\"zero point five\"), and attributive currency (\"the million dollar question\", \"a five dollar bill\") keep their words rather than becoming a number the speaker did not say. `fast-style.utils.ts` gained spoken domains and email addresses, unpunctuated self-corrections, weekday and month capitalization, and dictionary casing that never recases an ordinary word.\n\n**History says which path ran.** A local run records the `fast` post-processing mode with a null model, which History already renders as **Fast (local)**; the provider path records `api` as before.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Why removing that wait matters</summary>\n\nMeasured with the real prompt builders, a short dictation pays a fixed cost. For the Polished style with a ten-term glossary and one replacement, an 11-character transcript built a 2,955-character prompt (a 1,785-character system message plus a 1,170-character user message, about 739 tokens, 269 times the transcript), and a 160-character transcript built 3,104 characters. The request also carries a 2,048-token output floor. So a three-word dictation waits about as long as a paragraph does.\n\nThe local pass costs microseconds: 800 calls per tone on short inputs measured 8.8 us (Prompt) to 26.6 us (Formal), with Polished at 14.3 us. That is the whole reason for the split: the provider buys restructuring, and a short prose dictation does not need any.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>What I verified locally</summary>\n\n- `pnpm --filter desktop run lint` (Prettier, oxlint, ui-lint), `pnpm --filter desktop run check-types`\n- `pnpm --filter desktop run test:unit`: 231 files, 3095 tests, plus the Tauri script tests\n- Focused suites: `fast-style.utils.test.ts` 141, `inverse-text-normalization.utils.test.ts` 19, `post-processing-routing.utils.test.ts` 11, `post-processing-failure.test.ts` 27, settings and registry 140, `preferences.repo.test.ts` 25\n- `pnpm run format:check` repo-wide, and `pnpm --filter desktop run i18n` with the locale cache cleared (no diff, no untracked locale)\n- `pnpm --filter docs run check-types` (0 errors) and `pnpm --filter docs run build` (101 pages)\n- A 45-sentence probe through the composed pipeline before and after each fix, including the sentence that has to stay wordy, the escalations, and the domain, decimal, currency, and year cases\n\nNot verified here: anything Rust. This environment has no cargo, so the migration, the query change, the ts-rs binding, cargo test, clippy, and fmt are CI's to prove. `test:webdriver` and the provider-backed integration suite need a display and a `GROQ_API_KEY`.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Known limits in this change</summary>\n\n- The written-form pass is English only. Other dictation languages pass through it unchanged.\n- A dictation that is only a domain (\"example dot com\" with no address frame) keeps its words. `dot` is as common as `at`, and inventing a domain from any noun in front of it wrote \"we use.com\", so the rule requires the frame an address is spoken with.\n- The switch's description does not list which styles it affects. The docs do: short dictations in Email, Bullets, Notes, or a custom style still go to the provider.\n- Local numbers stop at millions; \"billion\" is not in the scale table, so it stays a word.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Review report (FULL-REVIEW.md format)</summary>\n\n# Review report: local styling for short dictations\n\nProtocol: `REVIEW.md` / `FULL-REVIEW.md` (fetched from `main`; the workspace copy is byte-identical, `git show origin/main:FULL-REVIEW.md` = 1209 lines, `diff -q` clean). Report format per FULL-REVIEW.md section 11.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**Status:** Ready\n**Confidence:** Medium\n**Mergeable:** Yes\n**CI verification:** Passing or explicitly noted per row below\n\nConfidence is Medium rather than High for one reason: this environment has no Rust toolchain, so the Rust half of the change (migration, column plumbing, ts-rs bindings) is verified by inspection and by CI, not locally, and the WebDriver and provider-backed integration suites cannot run here. Every JavaScript and TypeScript gate ran locally and passed.\n\n## Change inventory\n\n| Area | Changed contract | Risk | Required verification | Result |\n| ---- | ---------------- | ---- | --------------------- | ------ |\n| `utils/post-processing-routing.utils.ts` (new) | Pure decision: local vs provider, from transcript, tone id, the preference, and the dictation language | High | Unit tests for every tone, preference state, language, and length bound; end-to-end action test | Passed |\n| `actions/transcribe.actions.ts` | `applyPostProcessing` routes short English prose dictations to the local transform and escalates to the provider when the local output is empty; the provider-failure fallback passes the dictionary terms | High | `post-processing-failure.test.ts` routing block, plus the full suite; probe of the composed pipeline | Passed |\n| `utils/fast-style.utils.ts` | Spoken domains and email addresses, unpunctuated self-corrections, calendar casing, dictionary casing, written-form finalize pass | High | 140 unit tests in `fast-style.utils.test.ts`; probe of 45 sentences before and after | Passed |\n| `utils/inverse-text-normalization.utils.ts` (new) | Spoken numbers, dates, times, currency, percentages | High | 20 unit tests; probe of 45 sentences plus the round-two and pound cases | Passed |\n| Preference plumbing (`preferences.types.ts`, `bindings.ts`, `preferences.repo.ts`, `settings.state.ts`, `ai.utils.ts`, `user.actions.ts`, `onboarding.actions.ts`) | New on-by-default preference `fastStyleShortDictationsEnabled` | Medium | Round-trip tests, default tests, action test that turns it off, type check | Passed |\n| Rust (migration 091, `db/mod.rs`, `db/preferences_queries.rs`, `domain/preferences.rs`) | New column with default 1; column list, bind order, and row reader extended; migration registered at version 91 | High | CI `cargo test --locked --lib`, clippy, fmt; local inspection of the column list, placeholder list, and bind order | Pending CI |\n| `packages/desktop-native-apis/src/bindings.ts` | Generated binding for the new field | Medium | CI `scripts/check-bindings.sh` (needs cargo, unavailable here) | Pending CI |\n| `SettingsPage.tsx`, `settings-registry.ts` | New switch under AI and processing, with an availability rule and a search entry | Medium | Settings and registry tests (140), lint, anchor-to-registry test | Passed |\n| i18n (`apps/desktop/src/i18n/locales/*.json`, 10 locales) | Two new messages, translated in every locale | Medium | Translation completeness test (32), `pnpm --filter desktop i18n` idempotence in CI; manual check that no locale carries the English text | Passed |\n| Docs (`apps/docs/.../built-in-styles.md`, `dictation-workflow.md`, `styles-overview.md`) | User-facing description of the shortcut, the switch, and History's Fast (local) label | Low | `pnpm --filter docs run check-types` (0 errors) and `pnpm --filter docs run build` (101 pages) | Passed |\n\n## Findings\n\nAll findings below were found by the review passes or by the PR's review bots (never by CI, because none of them reached CI), reproduced by measurement before any change was made, and fixed in this change. Two bot claims were checked and did not reproduce as described: the spoken-email duplication needs a dotted local part (it was real, and is finding 8), and the claimed separate-then-merged number case is a comma inside one run, which is finding 6. None of the findings remain open.\n\n### High Number words at the end of a sentence were half-converted\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/inverse-text-normalization.utils.ts`, `applyCardinalRules`\n**Diff cause:** The new number pass recognised a number word only when the token carried no punctuation, so the last word of a run that ended a sentence failed the test and the run was cut short.\n**Evidence:** `applyFastStyle(\"we ordered twenty three.\", \"default\")` returned `\"We ordered 20 three.\"` (probe run in this environment). A following sentence could also merge into the previous run: `one thousand. Twenty five arrived` reached the parser as one run.\n**Reachable scenario:** Any dictation that ends in a spoken number, which is a common short dictation.\n**Impact:** A wrong number written into the text, and silently: the digits were plausible and the leftover word looked like speech.\n**Required change:** Strip trailing sentence punctuation when testing and expanding number tokens, put the punctuation back after the written number, and end the run at sentence or clause punctuation.\n**Verification:** `inverse-text-normalization.utils.test.ts` assertions for `we ordered twenty three.`, `the total is two hundred and fifty.`, and `we sold one thousand. Twenty five arrived late`; probe of the composed pipeline.\n\n### High A contraction in front of a correction marker was deleted with its words\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/fast-style.utils.ts`, `fixSelfCorrections` and `SELF_CORRECTION_LEAD_STOPLIST`\n**Diff cause:** The new unpunctuated correction rule compares the word in front of the marker against a stoplist of ordinary grammar words, but the token was compared as written, so a contraction never matched the word it wraps.\n**Evidence:** `applyFastStyle(\"there's no wait at the clinic\", \"default\")` returned `\"At the clinic.\"` (probe in this environment; raised by CodeAnt and confirmed here, because `theres` is not in the stoplist).\n**Reachable scenario:** Any dictation that uses a contraction in front of `no wait` or another marker. The stoplist already covers \"there\", so the plain form was safe and the contracted form was not.\n**Impact:** Two thirds of the sentence deleted, including its subject, with no sign in the output.\n**Required change:** Compare a possessive or contraction as the word it wraps, so \"there's\" is looked up as \"there\" and \"don't\" as \"do\".\n**Verification:** `fast-style.utils.test.ts` case for `there's no wait at the clinic`; the existing cases for `there is no wait at the clinic` and for the corrections that must still fire.\n\n### Medium Spoken domains were joined to whatever preceded the dot\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/fast-style.utils.ts`, `joinSpokenDomains`\n**Diff cause:** The first version of the dot rule consumed the space in front of the tld word with nothing required before it.\n**Evidence:** `the dot com bubble` became `The.com bubble.`, `put it in the dot com folder` became `Put it in the.com folder.` (probe). With the host added, `we use dot com for links` became `We use.com for links`, so the host alone was not enough either.\n**Reachable scenario:** Any sentence that uses the words \"dot com\" as a phrase rather than an address, which the bubble-era idiom makes common.\n**Impact:** A fabricated token in the delivered text.\n**Required change:** Require a host word and require that host to be introduced by one of the frames an address is spoken with (`to`, `at`, `is`, `on`, `from`, `via`, `visit`, `called`, `named`), the same evidence the email rule already requires for its local part.\n**Verification:** `fast-style.utils.test.ts` cases for `the dot com bubble`, `put it in the dot com folder`, `we use dot com for links`, and `we bought the domain dot com`, plus the address cases that must still join (`go to example dot com`, `our site is mausvoice dot dev`, `send it to mike at example dot com`).\n\n### Medium An attributive currency name was read as a unit\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/inverse-text-normalization.utils.ts`, `applyCardinalRules`\n**Diff cause:** Any currency word after a number run was treated as a unit and folded into the number.\n**Evidence:** `the million dollar question` became `The $1,000,000 question.`; `a hundred dollar bill` became `$100 bill.`; `a five dollar bill` became `A $5 bill.` (probe).\n**Reachable scenario:** Any dictation containing a compound like \"million dollar question\", \"hundred dollar bill\", or \"five dollar bill\".\n**Impact:** A quantity that was never spoken written into the text, and a dropped article.\n**Required change:** A singular currency name in front of a noun is attributive: a scale word alone in front of one is not converted at all, and the article in front of a converted run is kept (`a $5 bill`).\n**Verification:** `inverse-text-normalization.utils.test.ts` cases for `the million dollar question`, `a hundred dollar bill`, `a five dollar bill`, `twenty dollar bills`, and `it costs twenty thousand euro`; `fast-style.utils.test.ts` case for `a five dollar bill`.\n\n### Medium Decimals were half-converted\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/inverse-text-normalization.utils.ts`, `applyCardinalRules`\n**Diff cause:** The pass has no decimal form, but it still converted the tail of one when a currency or percent word followed.\n**Evidence:** `zero point five percent` became `Zero point 5%`; `two point five million` became `two point 5,000,000` (module probe).\n**Reachable scenario:** Any dictated decimal (\"zero point five percent\", \"two point five million\").\n**Impact:** Mixed words and digits in one number, which reads as an error in the delivered text.\n**Required change:** A run introduced by \"point\" or \"dot\" is left as the speaker said it.\n**Verification:** `inverse-text-normalization.utils.test.ts` \"leaves a decimal in words\" for four shapes.\n\n### Medium Repeated number words were collapsed as stutters\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/fast-style.utils.ts`, the repeated-word pass\n**Diff cause:** The pass replays any repeated word as a stutter, and the new written-form pass reads the repetition to tell a year from a count, so the two disagreed.\n**Evidence:** `twenty twenty six was a good year` became `26 was a good year.`; `twenty twenty five` became `20 five.`; `nine nine nine` became `Nine nine.` (probe).\n**Reachable scenario:** A dictation that names a year, or any number spoken with a repeated word.\n**Impact:** A year turned into a different number.\n**Required change:** The repeated-word pass skips number words; the written-form pass keeps its existing year guards.\n**Verification:** `fast-style.utils.test.ts` cases for `twenty twenty six was a good year` and `nine nine nine`; `isSpokenNumberWord` covered in the inverse tests.\n\n### Medium A comma inside a number run merged two numbers\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/inverse-text-normalization.utils.ts`, `collectNumberRun`\n**Diff cause:** The run collector joined number words across any whitespace, and only the last token's punctuation was restored, so a comma inside the run was dropped and its two numbers were added together.\n**Evidence:** `normalizeSpokenForms(\"we sold twenty, three of them\")` returned `\"we sold 23 of them\"` (probe; raised by CodeAnt and confirmed here).\n**Reachable scenario:** A dictated list or a hesitation that arrives with punctuation: \"we sold twenty, three of them\" is twenty and three, not twenty-three.\n**Impact:** A count that was never spoken written into the text, with the comma that separated the numbers removed.\n**Required change:** A comma continues a run only when a scale word already opened the number, which is the one place it groups digits (\"one thousand, two hundred and thirty four\" stays 1,234); anywhere else the run ends before it and the comma is kept.\n**Verification:** `inverse-text-normalization.utils.test.ts` cases for `we sold twenty, three of them`, `one thousand, two hundred and thirty four`, and `the total is one thousand, two hundred and fifty.`\n\n### Medium Dictionary terms touching a non-ASCII letter were never recased\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/fast-style.utils.ts`, `applyDictionaryCasing`\n**Diff cause:** The candidate filter looked for an ASCII capital and the match used `\\b`, which is ASCII-only, so a term such as \"Beyoncé\" could never match.\n**Evidence:** With `dictionaryTerms: [\"Beyoncé\", \"École\", \"Über\", \"Zürich\"]`, `i saw beyoncé at école and took über to zürich` came back with only \"Zürich\" corrected (probe; raised by CodeAnt).\n**Reachable scenario:** Any dictionary term that begins or ends with a non-ASCII letter, which is ordinary for names.\n**Impact:** A spelling the user explicitly added to the dictionary silently not applied.\n**Required change:** Compare the term against its lowercase form instead of a `[A-Z]` test, and match with Unicode-aware lookarounds under the `u` flag instead of `\\b`.\n**Verification:** `fast-style.utils.test.ts` case for a term starting and a term ending in a non-ASCII letter, next to the existing casing cases.\n\n### Medium Short non-English dictations were styled by the English-only transforms\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/post-processing-routing.utils.ts`, `apps/desktop/src/actions/transcribe.actions.ts`\n**Diff cause:** The route decision looked at length, tone, and the preference, but the local transforms are English: they delete English filler words, and a word can be a filler in English and a preposition in another language.\n**Evidence:** `applyFastStyle(\"wir treffen uns am montag um drei uhr im buero\", \"default\")` returned `\"Wir treffen uns am montag drei uhr im buero.\"` (probe; raised by CodeAnt and confirmed here).\n**Reachable scenario:** A short dictation in any non-English language with post-processing on. Before this change the dictation went to the provider and was styled; with routing alone it would lose words.\n**Impact:** Words deleted from a user's dictation in a language the transforms do not understand.\n**Required change:** The dictation language is a hard condition on the local route, read from the same resolver the transcription call uses (the file's language on the import path, the active or overridden dictation language on the live path). Auto-detect and the primary sentinel are not English, so they keep the provider path they had before this feature.\n**Verification:** `post-processing-routing.utils.test.ts` cases for `de`, `fr-CA`, `auto`, `primary`, and the empty string, plus the English cases; `post-processing-failure.test.ts` case that a German dictation with the override set reaches `genRepo.generateText`.\n\n### Medium The provider-failure fallback dropped the dictionary casing\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/actions/transcribe.actions.ts`, the catch block of `runPostProcessingRequest`\n**Diff cause:** The fallback called the local transform without the dictionary terms, which the two other local call sites pass.\n**Evidence:** Raised by CodeAnt; the call site had no `dictionaryTerms` argument while the routing and no-provider call sites both had one (read of the final code, and the new test fails without the argument).\n**Reachable scenario:** A provider failure on a dictation that contains a dictionary term.\n**Impact:** The user's spelling applied on every path except the one that exists to rescue a failed request.\n**Required change:** Pass `getFastStyleDictionaryTerms(state)` to the fallback as well.\n**Verification:** `post-processing-failure.test.ts` case that a rejected provider request still returns `I use GitHub daily.` for a `GitHub` term, and that the row keeps its fallback flag.\n\n### Medium A spoken email address could be delivered twice\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/fast-style.utils.ts`, `joinSpokenEmails` and `SPOKEN_EMAIL_RE`\n**Diff cause:** The replacement recovered the frame by cutting the match at the first `\" at \"` and then re-emitted the local part, so the local part was already inside the frame it printed.\n**Evidence:** `send it to john.smith at example dot com` returned `\"Send it to john.smith john.smith@example.com.\"` (probe; raised by CodeAnt, whose example used \"to mike at example.com\"). The simple `mike` case looked correct only because the repeated-word pass collapsed `mike mike`; a local part with a dot in it survived as a visible duplicate.\n**Reachable scenario:** Any dictated email address whose local part is not a repeated-word candidate, such as a first and last name joined by a dot.\n**Impact:** A duplicated name in the delivered text.\n**Required change:** Capture the frame in the pattern and use the capture group in the replacement.\n**Verification:** `fast-style.utils.test.ts` case for `send it to john.smith at example dot com` next to the existing `mike` case; ten further address shapes probed.\n\n### Medium A weight phrase was written with a currency symbol\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/inverse-text-normalization.utils.ts`, `readTrailingUnit`\n**Diff cause:** The new unit reader maps every currency word, and \"pound\" is both a currency and a weight, so a weight phrase was written with a pound sign.\n**Evidence:** `normalizeSpokenForms` returned `£5 of sugar` for `five pounds of sugar`, `£2 weights` for `two pound weights`, and `£5` for `the parcel weighs five pounds` (probe; raised by CodeRabbit).\n**Reachable scenario:** Any dictation about weight: a recipe, a parcel, a person.\n**Impact:** A weight written as money, which changes the meaning of the sentence.\n**Required change:** Treat pound as a weight when the word after it is \"of\", \"weight\", or \"weights\", or when a form of \"weigh\" stands in front of the number. Without a cue the word is money, which is what a bare \"twenty pounds\" almost always is. A weight run still gets its count written when the count is past a single small number.\n**Verification:** `inverse-text-normalization.utils.test.ts` covers two weight phrases, the \"weighs\" cue, the \"weigh\" cue with a larger count (200 pounds), three money phrases that must still convert, and the sentence boundary (a second review pass found that `it costs five pounds. Of course` read the next sentence's \"of\" as a weight cue; the cue now stops at a sentence end, and that case is a test). The comma case went the same way in a third pass (`it costs five pounds, of course`), and both sentences are tests. Twelve shapes were probed.\n\n### Low A correction marker at the start of a dictation deleted its own words\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/utils/fast-style.utils.ts`, `fixSelfCorrections`\n**Diff cause:** The new unpunctuated rule matches a marker from the token in front of it, so a marker at the start of the text handed its own second word in as the value being corrected.\n**Evidence:** `no wait make that thursday` became `No Thursday.`; `sorry i meant to call you` became `To call you.` (probe).\n**Reachable scenario:** A dictation that opens with a correction phrase, or says \"I meant to\" in the infinitive.\n**Impact:** Spoken words deleted from the text.\n**Required change:** A lead that is itself part of a marker phrase is not a value being corrected, and an infinitive after the marker is left alone.\n**Verification:** `fast-style.utils.test.ts` cases for both sentences, next to the corrections that must still fire (`send the report by friday no wait thursday`, `order two make that three coffees`, `meet me at three or rather four`).\n\n### Low Stale comments and an over-claiming privacy sentence\n\n**Location:** `apps/desktop/src/actions/transcribe.actions.ts` (comment and log line), `apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/db/open.rs` (comment), `apps/docs/.../built-in-styles.md`, `apps/docs/.../styles-overview.md`\n**Diff cause:** The escalation path and migration 91 changed what these sentences describe, and two docs sentences claimed the whole dictation stayed on the computer.\n**Evidence:** The comment said the caller falls back to the raw transcript, which is now only true when no provider is configured; the Rust comment said the build ships \"1-70 plus 89 and 90\"; the transcription stage can still be a cloud provider, so \"nothing leaves the computer\" over-claimed what the post-processing shortcut does.\n**Reachable scenario:** Reading the code or the docs.\n**Impact:** A maintainer or user misled about behavior.\n**Required change:** Describe what each path actually does; scope the privacy claim to the post-processing request.\n**Verification:** Read of the final text; `prettier --check` and the docs build pass.\n\n### Low Three docs sentences described behavior the code does not have\n\n**Location:** `apps/docs/.../styles-overview.md`, `built-in-styles.md`, `dictation-workflow.md`\n**Diff cause:** The new docs paragraph claimed post-processing is on by default, listed Verbatim among the styles sent to the provider, and omitted the character bound the route applies.\n**Evidence:** An unset post-processing mode resolves to `none` (`getGenPrefsInternal` resolves `config.mode` with a `none` fallback); Verbatim carries `shouldDisablePostProcessing`, which returns the raw transcript before either path runs; and the route has a 200-character bound, so \"at most two sentences and roughly thirty words\" is not the whole condition (read of the code; raised by CodeAnt).\n**Reachable scenario:** Reading the docs.\n**Impact:** A user told that a