left alone.\n- Dictionary casing rewrites a term's spelling but never recases an ordinary word (\"IT\" no longer rewrites \"it\").\n- The switch renders under Settings, AI and processing only while post-processing is on, and its registry entry matches its anchor.\n- Both new messages exist in all ten locales with real translations.\n- The self-correction stoplist now covers contractions: `there's no wait at the clinic` is delivered as spoken, while `send the report by friday no wait make that thursday` still becomes `Send the report by Thursday.`\n- A comma keeps its meaning in numbers: `we sold twenty, three of them` becomes `We sold 20, three of them.`, while `one thousand, two hundred and thirty four` still becomes `1,234`.\n- Dictionary casing applies to terms that begin or end in a non-ASCII letter (`Beyoncé`, `École`, `Über`).\n- Pound is money unless the phrase says weight: `five pounds of sugar`, `two pound weights`, and `the parcel weighs five pounds` keep their words, `i weigh two hundred pounds` becomes `i weigh 200 pounds`, and `it costs five pounds` becomes `it costs £5`.\n- Non-English dictations are not styled locally: `de`, `fr-CA`, `auto`, `primary`, and an empty language all route to the provider, and `en`, `en-US`, and `EN` route locally when the dictation is short and the tone is a prose tone.\n- Spoken email addresses are not duplicated, including a dotted local part: `send it to john.smith at example dot com` becomes `Send it to john.smith@example.com.`\n\n## Assumptions and unknowns\n\n- The ts-rs field for `fastStyleShortDictationsEnabled` was written by hand to match the generated style of the surrounding fields (doc comment on its own lines, field inline with the following fields, position matching the Rust struct order). CI regenerates the file and compares; if it disagrees, the fix is to run the generator and take its output.\n- The Rust migration and query changes are verified by reading: the column is appended to `USER_PREFERENCES_COLUMNS`, the bind order and the placeholder list are generated from that array, and the row reader falls back to `true` when the column is absent. CI's cargo jobs are the execution proof.\n- A dictation that is only a domain (\"example dot com\" with no address frame) keeps its words. That is deliberate: `dot` is as common as `at`, and inventing a domain from any noun in front of it wrote \"we use.com\".\n- Spoken number conversion covers English only, so a dictation whose language is not English keeps the provider path it had before this feature. Auto-detect cannot be resolved to a language before the transcript exists, so it is treated as not English; the alternative, routing it locally, is the case that was measured to delete words. The docs state the condition on all three pages that describe the shortcut.\n- The switch's own description does not enumerate the languages the transforms understand; the docs page does. The description still describes what the switch does when its conditions hold, and changing it would mean re-translating ten catalogs.\n- The settings description does not enumerate which styles the switch affects. The docs do, and a user in Email, Bullets, or Notes still sees the provider path, which is the intended split between restructuring (the model) and prose editing (the transforms).\n\n</details>\n\nMausAgent | Filed by a helpful agent on Arena.ai for @Owie6789, on 2026-10-06.\n\n\n___\n\n## **CodeAnt-AI Description**\n**Style short English dictations on your device instead of waiting for a provider**\n\n### What Changed\n- Short English dictations in prose styles are styled locally by default, without contacting the post-processing provider. The local styling also formats spoken dates, times, numbers, currency, and percentages, joins spoken email addresses and domains, and applies dictionary spelling.\n- Longer or non-English dictations, structured styles, and custom styles continue to use the provider. If local styling produces no text, the provider is used instead.\n- A setting lets you turn off local styling for short dictations.\n\n### Impact\n`✅ Faster short English dictations`\n`✅ Short prose dictations stay on your device`\n`✅ Clearer written dates, times, and numbers`\n<details><summary><strong>💡 Usage Guide</strong></summary>\n\n### Checking Your Pull Request\nEvery time you make a pull request, our system automatically looks through it. We check for security issues, mistakes in how you're setting up your infrastructure, and common code problems. We do this to make sure your changes are solid and won't cause any trouble later.\n\n### Talking to CodeAnt AI\nGot a question or need a hand with something in your pull request? You can easily get in touch with CodeAnt AI right here. Just type the following in a comment on your pull request, and replace \"Your question here\" with whatever you want to ask:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Your question here</code>\n</pre>\nThis lets you have a chat with CodeAnt AI about your pull request, making it easier to understand and improve your code.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Can you suggest a safer alternative to storing this secret?</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Preserve Org Learnings with CodeAnt\nYou can record team preferences so CodeAnt AI applies them in future reviews. Reply directly to the specific CodeAnt AI suggestion (in the same thread) and replace \"Your feedback here\" with your input:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Your feedback here</code>\n</pre>\nThis helps CodeAnt AI learn and adapt to your team's coding style and standards.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Do not flag unused imports.</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Retrigger review\nAsk CodeAnt AI to review the PR again, by typing:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: review</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Check Your Repository Health\nTo analyze the health of your code repository, visit our dashboard at [https://app.codeant.ai](https://app.codeant.ai). This tool helps you identify potential issues and a