+      "description": "## **User description**\n# Cut stop-to-History latency: raw audio IPC and concurrent row write\n\nTwo independent bottlenecks delayed the History row after a dictation ended. This PR removes both without changing what gets stored.\n\n## Bottleneck 1: JSON sample array on the save path\n\n`store_transcription_audio` took the recording as `samples: number[]`. Every sample crossed IPC as roughly 14 bytes of JSON text plus full stringify and parse. For 48 kHz audio that is ~6.7 MB of text for 30 seconds and ~27 MB for 2 minutes, benchmarking in the hundreds of milliseconds of pure serialization on the save critical path.\n\nThe command now takes a raw `tauri::ipc::Request` body packed as `[u32 LE sample rate][f32 LE samples]`, the exact framing `stop_recording` already uses on the way out, with the row id in the `x-transcription-id` header. Validation is unchanged (rate > 0, non-finite sample filter, empty rejection). The frontend encodes with the new `encodeRecordedAudio`, which round-trips through the existing `decodeStopRecordingPayload` used by the stop direction, so one wire format describes a recording both ways.\n\nBecause Specta cannot describe `ipc::Request`, the command is registered in `app.rs` but dropped from `collect_commands!`, mirroring `stop_recording`. The wrapper was removed from `bindings.ts` to match.\n\n## Bottleneck 2: the row waited for delivery\n\nThe History write started only after output delivery completed: hands-free delay, paste, or `simulate_type` per-character typing, the last one being O(text length) and the long pole. The row now appears when the WAV write plus DB insert finish (tens of ms) instead of after typing finishes (seconds on long dictations).\n\n- `DictationStrategy.handleFinalBulkTranscript` starts the write the moment the transcript is final and returns the promise as `pendingPersistence` with `historyOwner: \"concurrent\"`. The stop path awaits that promise instead of writing a second row, keeping the session locked exactly as long as the serial path would.\n- The overlap only fires when delivery cannot change the stored text: no review-before-insert and no remote target. Both keep the serial path.\n- `skipReview` is pinned to true during a concurrent write and the review-adopt branch guards on `!concurrentPersistence`, so a preference flipped mid-delivery cannot open a review whose Save would write a second, edited row.\n- Warning parity: the outer failure path appends its warning to the already-written row via the new `appendTranscriptionWarnings` (repo update plus state refresh, missing-row and empty-list no-ops, repository failure swallowed like its siblings).\n- The truncation toast now checks `isPersistenceAllowed()` before promising the unstyled ending is in History. Before, it made that promise even under incognito or ephemeral sessions where nothing was stored. This is the one intentional behavior change.\n\nSafety note: the persisted `audio_path` is descriptive only. Playback, re-transcribe, purge, and delete all re-derive the file from the transcription id, so the row can land while the WAV write is still in flight on the serial path and none of that changes here.\n\n## Verification\n\nLocal gates, all green on the pushed head:\n\n- `test:unit` (src + scripts): 229 files, 3054 tests pass.\n- `tsc --noEmit`: clean.\n- `pnpm --filter desktop run lint` (prettier + oxlint + ui-lint): clean.\n- `pnpm run format:check` at repo root: clean.\n- i18n extraction idempotent, no locale drift.\n\nNew tests:\n\n- Wire contract: the store invoke carries a `Uint8Array` body that decodes back to the exact samples (f32-rounded) and a header equal to the created row id. Negative assertions rewritten to filter by command so the 3-arg invoke cannot make them vacuous.\n- 4 encoder tests including caller-buffer non-mutation and empty recording.\n- 7 strategy tests: overlap starts during delivery, skipReview pin, serial fallback for review-before-insert, remote target, and missing hook, no rejection when persistence fails, no concurrent write after post-processing failure.\n- 2 stop-path tests: awaits `pendingPersistence` without storing again, truncation toast uses the stored wording for a concurrent row.\n- 5 `appendTranscriptionWarnings` tests.\n- 3 Rust unit tests for `decode_recorded_audio`: literal wire-layout bytes, short and truncated body rejection, encoder roundtrip.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- Rust was not compiled locally (no cargo in this environment). The raw-request pattern follows the official Tauri v2 docs and the `stop_recording` precedent in this repo. CI clippy, rustfmt, `cargo test --lib`, and the check-bindings regeneration are the authoritative check for the Rust side of this diff.\n- `bindings.ts` was edited to match what `scripts/bindings.sh` will now generate. The CI `check-bindings.sh` step regenerates it from the Rust source and diffs against the committed file, so any drift fails loudly there rather than silently.\n- Three pre-existing test files (post-processing eval, transcription integration, post-processing stability) require `GROQ_API_KEY` and are unaffected by this change.\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n<a href=\"https://cubic.dev/pr/maus-inc/mausVoice/pull/282?utm_source=github\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Review in cubic\" src=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"></picture></a> <a href=\"https://www.cubic.dev/action/auto-fix/pr/maus-inc/mausVoice/282?returnTo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fmaus-inc%2FmausVoice%2Fpull%2F282&source=description\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/turn-on-auto-fix-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/turn-on-auto-fix-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Turn on auto-fix\" src=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/turn-on-auto-fix-dark.svg\"></picture></a>\n<!-- End of auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nReduce post-dictation latency by saving eligible History rows during delivery and transferring recorded audio through compact binary IPC.\n\nNew Features:\n- Persist eligible local dictations to History concurrently with output delivery so entries become available sooner.\n- Use a shared packed raw-audio IPC format for recording uploads and downloads.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Preserve delivery warnings on concurrently persisted History rows.\n- Prevent truncation notices from claiming content was saved when persistence is disabled.\n- Avoid duplicate History rows when concurrent persistence completes before the stop path.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Keep serial persistence for review-before-insert, remote delivery, post-processing failures, and other cases where delivery can alter stored content.\n- Add validation and warning-update handling for raw audio persistence.\n\nTests:\n- Add coverage for raw audio encoding and decoding, concurrent persistence behavior, warning propagation, stop-path coordination, and fallback conditions.\n\nChores:\n- Update generated native bindings to exclude the raw-request audio command from Specta bindings.\n\n## Review rounds\n\n- rustfmt: the roundtrip assert is split the way the pinned formatter wants it.\n- Compile fixes after the first CI round: the command internals use explicit matches and plain loops, and the `tauri::ipc::Request` parameter carries its lifetime (`Request<'_>`), which the bare type omitted.\n- CodeAnt flagged a real race: the concurrent gate read the remote target captured before the post-processing await, while routing reads live preferences a tick later. A receiver paired during the polish window would ship remote while the row recorded null remote fields. The gate now re-reads the preferences in the same tick as routing, and a new test flips the pairing inside the post-processing await.\n- Known consequence, telemetry only: with the overlap, the `persisted` pipeline mark can land before `inserted`. `summarizePipeline` already clamps stage durations at zero, so the sample degrades to zero for those two stages rather than reporting nonsense. Rewriting the tracer for overlapping stages is out of scope here.\n\n\n___\n\n## **CodeAnt-AI Description**\n**Show dictations in History sooner and speed up audio saving**\n\n### What Changed\n- History rows can appear while text is still being delivered, instead of waiting for typing or pasting to finish.\n- Long recordings are sent in a compact format, reducing the delay when saving audio.\n- Delivery failures are added to the History row, and truncation notices only promise a History copy when one will be saved.\n\n### Impact\n`✅ Faster History availability after dictation`\n`✅ Less delay saving long recordings`\n`✅ Delivery failures recorded in History`\n<details><summary><strong>💡 Usage Guide</strong></summary>\n\n### Checking Your Pull Request\nEvery time you make a pull request, our system automatically looks through it. We check for security issues, mistakes in how you're setting up your infrastructure, and common code problems. We do this to make sure your changes are solid and won't cause any trouble later.\n\n### Talking to CodeAnt AI\nGot a question or need a hand with something in your pull request? You can easily get in touch with CodeAnt AI right here. Just type the following in a comment on your pull request, and replace \"Your question here\" with whatever you want to ask:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Your question here</code>\n</pre>\nThis lets you have a chat with CodeAnt AI about your pull request, making it easier to understand and improve your code.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Can you suggest a safer alternative to storing this secret?</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Preserve Org Learnings with CodeAnt\nYou can record team preferences so CodeAnt AI applies them in future reviews. Reply directly to the specific CodeAnt AI suggestion (in the same thread) and replace \"Your feedback here\" with your input:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Your feedback here</code>\n</pre>\nThis helps CodeAnt AI learn and adapt to your team's coding style and standards.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Do not flag unused imports.</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Retrigger review\nAsk CodeAnt AI to review the PR again, by typing:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: review</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Check Your Repository Health\nTo analyze the health of your code repository, visit our dashboard at [https://app.codeant.ai](https://app.codeant.ai). This tool helps you identify potential issues and areas for improvement in your codebase, ensuring your repository maintains high standards of code health.\n\n</details>\n"
