+      "description": "## **User description**\n## What this changes\n\nWhen the styling model returns a list of edits and one of them cannot be applied, the app used to paste the half-edited text anyway. A single unmatched edit could therefore splice, drop, or reorder words in text that then went straight into the user's document. This pull request changes the outcome: any declared edit that cannot be applied now rejects the whole edit batch, the app inserts nothing, and History keeps the complete raw transcript with a notification that explains why. If that same History item fails this way three times, the app runs one automatic full-audio retranscription after a short randomized wait, updates the same History row, and never creates a second one. The style preview in the tone editor is unaffected and still shows what a style managed to change, because it never inserts text.\n\n## Details\n\n<details>\n<summary>Why the old behaviour was the bug, not the symptom</summary>\n\n`resolveProcessedTranscription` in `apps/desktop/src/utils/ai.utils.ts` preferred an applied edit list over the model's rewrite. It applied whatever edits matched, counted the rest as a warning, and returned the partially edited text as a success. `DictationStrategy` then pasted it. The warning was recorded on the History row but never blocked insertion, so the user's document received text the model never fully expressed.\n\nThe resolver now rejects the batch when any declared edit is skipped: a missing or ambiguous `find`, a `find` that sits inside a word, a missing replacement, a malformed edit list, or an unreadable edit entry. Production falls back to the raw transcript and a semantic failure flag. The preview caller passes `{ allowPartialEdits: true }` explicitly, which keeps the two policies separate and visible.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>How the recovery pass works</summary>\n\n- Each semantic failure increments a persisted per-row counter, `postProcessEditFailureCount`, and sets `postProcessEditFailed`.\n- Once the counter passes two failures, `scheduleAutomaticPostProcessEditRetry` claims the row by writing `postProcessEditAutoRetryUsed = true` before it waits, which makes the single-pass rule survive a restart.\n- The wait is bounded exponential full jitter (1s base, 8s cap) from `apps/desktop/src/utils/post-process-retry.utils.ts`, so a slow provider is not hammered and no single row waits without bound.\n- The pass calls the existing `retranscribeTranscription` against the same row id with the same style and language the utterance used, so History is updated in place rather than duplicated. The engine only reports the outcome it stored; the manual dialog entry, `retranscribeTranscriptionWithRecovery`, schedules the recovery pass from that outcome, the same way the dictation and import paths schedule theirs.\n- The claim persists that style and language in two dedicated columns, so the delayed delivery and the post-restart resume restore them from the row instead of falling back to whatever the user has selected by then. An empty string records that the failed run used no tone or no language override; a claim whose columns are null, because it predates them, is settled without a pass so the raw transcript is never restyled under a different style. The delivery hands the claimed row to the same resume path a restart uses, so a claim that lost its context, or whose pass never reached post-processing, is settled at delivery time too.\n- A manual retranscription cannot race the automatic one: the in-flight id set blocks the second start while the scheduler owns the row, and the durable marker blocks every later chain.\n- A successful styled run clears both the counter and the marker, so the next chain starts from a clean row.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Persistence and compatibility</summary>\n\nMigration `091_post_process_edit_failure.sql` adds three nullable integer columns to `transcriptions` and is registered in `db/mod.rs` as version 91, above the retired 71 to 88 window. Migration `092_post_process_edit_retry_context.sql` adds the two nullable text columns that carry a claim's recorded tone and language, registered as version 92. Existing databases keep working because the columns are nullable and defaults are absent, which is the repository pattern. The wire shape is updated in `packages/types`, the manually updated Specta binding, the local repository, the preview runtime, and the Rust domain and query layer, and the Rust round-trip test now asserts all three fields.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Verification run locally before this push</summary>\n\n- `pnpm --filter desktop test:unit`: 230 files, 3056 tests, plus 4 Node TAP tests, all passing\n- `pnpm --filter desktop lint`: Prettier, oxlint, and UI lint clean\n- `pnpm --filter desktop check-types`: clean after `turbo run build --filter=desktop^...`\n- `pnpm --filter @maus-inc/voice-ai test`: 501 tests passing\n- `pnpm --filter @repo/preview test`: 22 tests passing\n- `pnpm --filter @repo/agent test`: 24 tests passing\n- `pnpm --filter desktop i18n` then a diff check: catalogs in sync, no untracked locale files\n- `pnpm run format:check`: clean\n\nThe user-facing contract of the recovery behaviour is documented in `apps/docs/src/content/docs/configuration/post-processing.md`. The Rust changes are covered by the existing `cargo test --lib`, `cargo fmt --check`, and clippy jobs in `Test Desktop Unit` and `Lint Desktop`, which run on this branch push. Rust tooling is not available in the environment this branch was written in, so those results come from CI rather than a local run, and the Specta binding was updated by hand to match the generator output.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Files worth reading first</summary>\n\n- `apps/desktop/src/utils/ai.utils.ts` and its test, for the resolver policy and the preview opt-in\n- `apps/desktop/src/actions/transcribe.actions.ts`, for the semantic failure metadata\n- `apps/desktop/src/actions/transcriptions.actions.ts`, for scheduling and single-pass ownership\n- `apps/desktop/src/strategies/dictation.strategy.ts`, for the blocked insertion and the notification\n- `apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/db/migrations/091_post_process_edit_failure.sql` and `092_post_process_edit_retry_context.sql`, plus `db/mod.rs`, for the schema steps\n\n</details>\n\n- MausAgent | Filed by `Arena.ai Agent Mode`, with @Owie6789, on 2026-10-07\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n<a href=\"https://cubic.dev/pr/maus-inc/mausVoice/pull/290?utm_source=github\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Review in cubic\" src=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"></picture></a> <a href=\"https://www.cubic.dev/action/auto-fix/pr/maus-inc/mausVoice/290?returnTo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fmaus-inc%2FmausVoice%2Fpull%2F290&source=description\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/turn-on-auto-fix-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/turn-on-auto-fix-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Turn on auto-fix\" src=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/turn-on-auto-fix-dark.svg\"></picture></a>\n<!-- End of auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nReject incomplete styling edits to protect inserted dictation, retain the raw transcript in History, and recover repeated failures with one durable same-row retranscription.\n\nNew Features:\n- Add automatic, single-pass audio retranscription recovery for repeated styling-edit failures while preserving the original History entry and its style/language context across restarts.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Prevent partially applied or malformed styling edit batches from being inserted, preserving the complete raw transcript instead.\n- Notify users whether the raw transcript was saved in History and provide a History link only when a row was actually stored.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Keep style previews permissive while enforcing strict all-or-nothing edit handling for production transcription.\n- Persist edit-failure state, retry ownership, retry context, and claim progress throughout the transcription data layers.\n\nDocumentation:\n- Document the raw-transcript preservation and automatic recovery behavior for failed styling edits.\n\nTests:\n- Expand coverage for strict edit resolution, blocked dictation insertion, History notifications, retry scheduling, restart recovery, concurrency, and persistence round trips.\n\n\n___\n\n## **CodeAnt-AI Description**\n**Prevent incomplete styling edits from reaching documents and recover repeated failures**\n\n### What Changed\n- If any requested styling edit cannot be applied, discard the entire edit batch, keep the complete raw transcript, and do not insert it. The notification explains whether the transcript was saved in History; its History action appears only when a row was saved.\n- After three edit-application failures on the same History item, automatically retranscribe its audio once using the original style and language, updating the existing item. A claimed recovery interrupted by an app restart resumes when History loads.\n- Style previews can still show partially applied edits because preview text is not inserted into documents.\n\n### Impact\n`✅ No partially styled dictation inserted`\n`✅ Complete raw transcript kept after edit failures`\n`✅ One automatic recovery after repeated edit failures`\n<details><summary><strong>💡 Usage Guide</strong></summary>\n\n### Checking Your Pull Request\nEvery time you make a pull request, our system automatically looks through it. We check for security issues, mistakes in how you're setting up your infrastructure, and common code problems. We do this to make sure your changes are solid and won't cause any trouble later.\n\n### Talking to CodeAnt AI\nGot a question or need a hand with something in your pull request? You can easily get in touch with CodeAnt AI right here. Just type the following in a comment on your pull request, and replace \"Your question here\" with whatever you want to ask:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Your question here</code>\n</pre>\nThis lets you have a chat with CodeAnt AI about your pull request, making it easier to understand and improve your code.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Can you suggest a safer alternative to storing this secret?</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Preserve Org Learnings with CodeAnt\nYou can record team preferences so CodeAnt AI applies them in future reviews. Reply directly to the specific CodeAnt AI suggestion (in the same thread) and replace \"Your feedback here\" with your input:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Your feedback here</code>\n</pre>\nThis helps CodeAnt AI learn and adapt to your team's coding style and standards.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Do not flag unused imports.</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Retrigger review\nAsk CodeAnt AI to review the PR again, by typing:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: review</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Check Your Repository Health\nTo analyze the health of your code repository, visit our dashboard at [https://app.codeant.ai](https://app.codeant.ai). This tool helps you identify potential issues and areas for improvement in your codebase, ensuring your repository maintains high standards of code health.\n\n</details>\n"
