+      "description": "## **User description**\n## Summary\n- Emit the microphone-save acknowledgement through the root Sonner toaster only after persistence succeeds. The direct Sonner call avoids losing it to a competing state-backed snackbar update.\n- Keep the dialog open and preserve `Preference saved.`; keep failures inline and suppress the preference action's duplicate global error toast.\n- Disable close, Escape, and backdrop dismissal while persistence is pending so an inline-only failure cannot be hidden; re-enable dismissal once the save settles. Keep the microphone tester available so an in-progress capture can still be stopped.\n- Keep the visible toast outside the dialog and add a visually hidden polite status inside the active modal so screen readers can hear the success while MUI hides the app root.\n- Add real-action/repository-path coverage, a concurrent-snackbar regression check, dismissal-during-save and tester-availability coverage, bottom-right toaster assertions, and the updated snackbar spec source mapping.\n\n## Verification\n- Desktop unit suite: 230 files / 3,045 Vitest tests plus 4 Node tests passed.\n- Preview suite: 4 files / 22 tests passed.\n- Root `lint`, `check-types`, `build`, and `format:check` passed after the final code changes; `i18n:extract` produced no locale changes.\n- Browser audit of the actual shared Sonner toaster in light and dark themes found no axe-core WCAG A/AA violations; keyboard focus reaches the toast and labelled close control.\n- The full root `pnpm test` suite could not be certified without `GROQ_API_KEY` (54 API-backed eval/integration failures across three files); the scoped desktop and preview suites pass.\n\n## Follow-up\nThe existing shared Sonner close control is 20×20px, below the design guidance for pointer/touch targets. It is unchanged in this focused call-site PR; the audit caveat is recorded in `docs/decisions/microphone-preference-save-toast.md`.\n\nStacked on PR #236: this branch's parent is the current head of `arena/0.1.6-consolidation`, so that branch is the PR base to avoid duplicating its diff.\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nUse the shared bottom-right Sonner toast for successful microphone preference saves while keeping failures recoverable within the dialog.\n\nNew Features:\n- Route microphone preference save confirmations through the shared global bottom-right Sonner toast after persistence succeeds.\n- Provide an accessible in-dialog success announcement while keeping the visible confirmation outside the dialog.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Prevent microphone-save success notifications from being lost when concurrent snackbar updates occur.\n- Keep the dialog and microphone tester usable during pending saves, while preserving inline-only failure feedback and preventing duplicate global error toasts.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Allow preference updates to selectively suppress their generic global error toast.\n- Update snackbar preview specifications to reflect the shared Sonner-based bottom-right notification system.\n\nDocumentation:\n- Document the microphone preference save feedback behavior, accessibility considerations, and verification evidence.\n\nTests:\n- Add coverage for persisted success and failure paths, concurrent notifications, dismissal attempts during saves, tester availability, accessibility announcements, and shared toaster placement.\n\n\n___\n\n## **CodeAnt-AI Description**\n**Show microphone save results clearly and keep failures recoverable**\n\n### What Changed\n- After a microphone preference is saved, show a bottom-right success toast and announce it to screen readers while keeping the dialog open.\n- On failure, keep the error in the dialog without showing a duplicate global error toast.\n- While saving, prevent the dialog from closing, but keep microphone tests available so an active test can be stopped.\n\n### Impact\n`✅ Save confirmations appear only after preferences are saved`\n`✅ Microphone-save failures stay visible and recoverable`\n`✅ Active microphone tests remain stoppable during saves`\n<details><summary><strong>💡 Usage Guide</strong></summary>\n\n### Checking Your Pull Request\nEvery time you make a pull request, our system automatically looks through it. We check for security issues, mistakes in how you're setting up your infrastructure, and common code problems. We do this to make sure your changes are solid and won't cause any trouble later.\n\n### Talking to CodeAnt AI\nGot a question or need a hand with something in your pull request? You can easily get in touch with CodeAnt AI right here. Just type the following in a comment on your pull request, and replace \"Your question here\" with whatever you want to ask:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Your question here</code>\n</pre>\nThis lets you have a chat with CodeAnt AI about your pull request, making it easier to understand and improve your code.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Can you suggest a safer alternative to storing this secret?</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Preserve Org Learnings with CodeAnt\nYou can record team preferences so CodeAnt AI applies them in future reviews. Reply directly to the specific CodeAnt AI suggestion (in the same thread) and replace \"Your feedback here\" with your input:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Your feedback here</code>\n</pre>\nThis helps CodeAnt AI learn and adapt to your team's coding style and standards.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Do not flag unused imports.</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Retrigger review\nAsk CodeAnt AI to review the PR again, by typing:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: review</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Check Your Repository Health\nTo analyze the health of your code repository, visit our dashboard at [https://app.codeant.ai](https://app.codeant.ai). This tool helps you identify potential issues and areas for improvement in your codebase, ensuring your repository maintains high standards of code health.\n\n</details>\n"
