+      "description": "## **User description**\nThis gives the desktop window one material and real depth. The title bar, the navigation rail and the page panel all share a single wash, the rail sits flush against the left edge with rounded corners only where it faces the page, and the page panel mirrors that rail across one visible seam. The title bar no longer throws a shadow down onto the page, and the close button tints faintly instead of turning solid red, because the main window hides to tray rather than discarding anything. It also repairs the browser preview, which could not boot at all, so you can see the result without building the native app.\n\nStacked on #236. Merge this branch into `arena/0.1.6-consolidation` after that one lands.\n\n<details>\n<summary>What changed, in plain terms</summary>\n\n**One material across the window.** `chromeWash` in `apps/desktop/src/styles/palette.ts` paints the title bar, the navigation rail and the routed page panel. Before this, the rail carried its own gradient and the bar carried a separate fill, so the two could be retuned apart and leave a visible step where they meet.\n\n**The rail reads as an edge, not a floating card.** It is flush against the window's left edge with no margin, square where it touches the frame and rounded on its right, so the corner never leaves a notch.\n\n**The page panel mirrors the rail.** Rounded on its left only, square on the right and bottom, so one seam of canvas separates the two surfaces and they read as raised and recessed against each other.\n\n**Depth that points somewhere.** `raisedEdge` casts the rail onto the panel beside it and `recessedEdge` pushes the panel away in the opposite direction. Both are skewed sideways on purpose, because a symmetrical shadow makes a rail look like it is hovering in the middle of the window.\n\n**The title bar stopped hovering.** `titleBarShadow` is now one inset rim and no drop at all. A shadow thrown straight down out of the bar reads as the bar floating over the page.\n\n**A close button that tells the truth.** The main window intercepts `CloseRequested` and hides to tray, so closing discards nothing. The hover tint moved from a solid destructive red to `dangerHoverSoft` at 0.14 alpha, and the removed `dangerHover` theme token is gone from `theme.ts` and `mui.d.ts`.\n\n**The browser preview boots again.** It could not start because of an import cycle: `preview/tauri/core.ts` stood in for a Tauri leaf module that much of the app imports, and its static import of the preview runtime closed a loop back through the store into `login.state`. It now loads the runtime on first use, and `PreviewOperationError` moved into its own dependency-free module so a stub can raise it without dragging in the whole app.\n\n**One place that knows the active color scheme.** Five components read `useColorScheme()` on their own, in three slightly different shapes, and each treated `mode` as if it were the scheme even though `mode` is `\"system\"` whenever the user has not picked one, which is this app's default. `useIsDarkMode` in `apps/desktop/src/hooks/color-scheme.hooks.ts` now owns that resolution.\n\n**Documentation that matches the layout.** `apps/desktop/DESIGN.md` no longer claims the title bar and the rail form one L-shaped surface. The page header sits between them, and the page panel shares the chrome wash rather than changing material.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>What I verified locally</summary>\n\n- `pnpm run build`, `pnpm run check-types`, `pnpm run lint`, `pnpm run format:check`\n- `pnpm --filter desktop run test:unit` — 229 files, 2965 tests\n- `pnpm --filter desktop run test:integration` — 2 tests, including the live post-processing stability check\n- `pnpm --filter @maus-inc/preview test` — 22 tests\n- `pnpm --filter desktop run i18n` — 1003 messages per locale, no drift\n\nNot verified: native traffic lights, the Windows and Linux caption buttons, drag regions and resize grips. Those need a real Tauri run on each platform, since `decorations: false` removes the OS chrome and `WindowResizeHandles` supplies the grips by hand.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Known limits in this change</summary>\n\n- Dark mode leans on hairlines rather than shadows. A near-black canvas swallows a black cast, so if the separation ever reads too weak, raise the wash alpha before reaching for a stronger shadow.\n- The title bar and the rail share paint but are not contiguous. The page header sits between them, and closing that gap is a layout change rather than a token change.\n- With `decorations: false` the window cannot be dragged while unfocused, which is a known Tauri limitation ([tauri#4316](https://github.com/tauri-apps/tauri/issues/4316)).\n\n</details>\n\nMausAgent | Filed by `stealth/space-bunny-alpha`, with `@Owie6789`, on 2026-10-04\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n<a href=\"https://cubic.dev/pr/maus-inc/mausVoice/pull/280?utm_source=github\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Review in cubic\" src=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"></picture></a>\n<!-- End of auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nRefresh the desktop window chrome with a unified material, clearer depth, consistent controls, and a working browser preview.\n\nNew Features:\n- Unify the desktop title bar, navigation rail, and window surfaces under a shared chrome material with intentional depth and edge treatment.\n- Refresh Windows and Linux caption controls with inset rounded targets, consistent neutral interaction states, responsive sizing, and scheme-aware styling.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Restore browser preview startup by removing the import cycle in the preview Tauri stubs and isolating preview operation errors.\n- Resolve light and dark appearance consistently, including system-selected color schemes.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Refine frameless window geometry, resize-grip coordination, title-bar depth, and navigation rail/page surface separation.\n- Remove destructive close-button styling to reflect hide-to-tray behavior.\n- Update desktop design documentation and expand window chrome and shadow test coverage.\n\nDocumentation:\n- Document the updated window surface, control, custom chrome, and known platform limitations.\n\nTests:\n- Add coverage for caption control appearance, interaction states, responsive geometry, focus dimming, and directional surface shadows.\n\n\n___\n\n## **CodeAnt-AI Description**\n**Redesign desktop window chrome and restore the browser preview**\n\n### What Changed\n- The title bar and navigation rail share one material, while the routed content stays flat; the rail sits flush with the window edge and the title bar no longer casts a shadow onto the page.\n- Windows and Linux window controls appear as inset rounded squares with consistent neutral hover and press states, including Close.\n- The browser preview starts reliably, and components using the system color scheme match the light or dark appearance already shown on screen.\n\n### Impact\n`✅ Browser preview launches without a native build`\n`✅ System theme colors match the displayed interface`\n`✅ Window controls have consistent, non-destructive feedback`\n<details><summary><strong>💡 Usage Guide</strong></summary>\n\n### Checking Your Pull Request\nEvery time you make a pull request, our system automatically looks through it. We check for security issues, mistakes in how you're setting up your infrastructure, and common code problems. We do this to make sure your changes are solid and won't cause any trouble later.\n\n### Talking to CodeAnt AI\nGot a question or need a hand with something in your pull request? You can easily get in touch with CodeAnt AI right here. Just type the following in a comment on your pull request, and replace \"Your question here\" with whatever you want to ask:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Your question here</code>\n</pre>\nThis lets you have a chat with CodeAnt AI about your pull request, making it easier to understand and improve your code.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Can you suggest a safer alternative to storing this secret?</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Preserve Org Learnings with CodeAnt\nYou can record team preferences so CodeAnt AI applies them in future reviews. Reply directly to the specific CodeAnt AI suggestion (in the same thread) and replace \"Your feedback here\" with your input:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Your feedback here</code>\n</pre>\nThis helps CodeAnt AI learn and adapt to your team's coding style and standards.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Do not flag unused imports.</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Retrigger review\nAsk CodeAnt AI to review the PR again, by typing:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: review</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Check Your Repository Health\nTo analyze the health of your code repository, visit our dashboard at [https://app.codeant.ai](https://app.codeant.ai). This tool helps you identify potential issues and areas for improvement in your codebase, ensuring your repository maintains high standards of code health.\n\n</details>\n"
