+      "description": "## **User description**\nThis branch fixes the defects found while auditing PR #236 against the 0.1.6 feature checklist. It is stacked on `arena/0.1.6-consolidation` and targets that branch, not `main`.\n\nSix real defects, five of them found by reading code rather than running it. Every fix carries a test that was confirmed to fail before the change and pass after.\n\n<details>\n<summary>What changed, in plain terms</summary>\n\n### The documentation pointed at a dialog that no longer exists\n\nThe settings page used to have a \"More settings\" dialog. It is gone, replaced by eight real sections. Eleven documentation pages still told people to open it. One of those pages had been reduced to a single instruction: open \"More settings\" and press Close, which is not a page anyone should have to read.\n\nI rewrote the settings map to describe the eight sections that actually ship, and pointed every other page at the section that now owns each control.\n\n### The settings page had no way to jump between sections\n\nEight sections in one long scroll means no way to see what lives where, and no way to reach Advanced without reading everything above it. There is now a rail on the left that lists the sections and follows you as you scroll.\n\nA test pins every registry section to the anchor it scrolls to, so renaming an anchor now fails a test instead of leaving a button that goes nowhere.\n\n### The macOS window controls were too small to click\n\nThe red, yellow, and green buttons were 12 by 12 pixels. The accessibility standard requires at least 24 by 24. On a trackpad, 12 pixels is close to unusable, which matches the \"poor icon clarity\" complaint exactly, except the problem was never the icon.\n\nThe painted dots stay at 12 pixels because that is the correct macOS proportion. What changed is the invisible clickable area around each dot, which now meets the minimum.\n\n### The window bar ignored how narrow the window was\n\nThe wordmark disappeared below a certain width, but that breakpoint tracked the web page, not the window. Narrow the window on a large monitor and the bar kept everything. It now measures its actual window, tightens the buttons below 520 pixels, and draws the roomy version when the size is unknown so nothing flickers on startup.\n\n### A streamed reply was invisible to screen readers\n\nA streaming reply updates one message in place, so nothing in the page announced it. A screen reader was told a run started and then never learned a reply arrived. There is now a polite live region for the three moments that matter: a reply starting, finishing, and a message landing.\n\nSpeaking every streamed chunk would queue dozens of utterances and make the region unusable, so token growth stays silent. The rule deciding what to speak lives in one tested function, because two effects writing the same state let whichever ran last overwrite the other.\n\n### Message actions were hidden behind a right-click menu\n\nCopy and edit-and-resend were only reachable by right-clicking, so they were undiscoverable and unreachable without a pointer. They are now a visible row that appears on hover and on keyboard focus, and stays visible on touch devices where hover does not exist.\n\nEscape also cancels an open message edit, which previously had no keyboard route out.\n\n### The release notes rendered badly and could be skipped\n\nA table in the release notes appeared as raw pipe characters, because the notes were rendered without the markup dialect the release pipeline writes. They now render the same as every other message.\n\nThe release history fetches an endpoint GitHub limits per address. When that limit was hit the app said \"could not connect\", sending people to inspect their network for a problem that is not on their network. It now says what actually happened and offers the releases page as a way through.\n\nThe tray's Install Update started an install directly, skipping the one screen that shows what is about to change. It now opens that screen.\n\n### The review buttons were English in every language\n\nThe pill draws its own buttons, so their text has to travel from the app. Insert, Copy, Cancel, and the hint above them were hardcoded English in all three native pills. Only Edit had been localized. A German user reading English on the one surface that asks them to accept or reject their own dictation is a bad time to discover a gap.\n\n### The Windows pill could destroy your edited transcript\n\nSubmitting cleared the text box every time. The write that tells the app about the text ignored its own errors, so the pill could not tell whether the app had received anything. If the connection dropped at that moment, the text was gone, and the connection it would have been sent on was the one that had just failed. The Linux and macOS pills already kept the text in that case. All three now behave the same way.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Merging the moving base</summary>\n\nThe consolidation branch advanced 75 commits while this one was open, and it independently fixed one of the same bugs this branch had fixed: the Windows pill's write discarded its errors, so a failed submit cleared the user's only copy of an edited transcript.\n\nTheir fix is more thorough than mine. It routes every review decision through one function that owns the message shape, so the desktop's action vocabulary has one owner instead of being rebuilt at each call site. I took theirs whole rather than keeping a duplicate.\n\nThree files conflicted, all in the Windows pill crate. In every case the base's version won:\n\n- **`input.rs`** took theirs, including their tests, because they already covered the failed-write case. I kept only my two wire-shape tests, which are not duplicates: they pin the JSON that reaches the desktop, which is exactly the ownership their refactor claims.\n- **`ipc.rs`** the two implementations were byte-identical and only the doc comment differed. Took theirs, because it records why the bug happened. My five localized caption fields and the boxed review variant were not involved in the conflict.\n- **`pill.rs`** the base added a discarded-result marker to both arms of the two matches the bool return had affected. Took theirs. The unbox for the boxed variant came through the auto-merge, so nothing was needed there.\n\nMy cross-crate contract test had been asserting one particular spelling of the clear-on-delivery guard. It now asserts the behaviour instead: that the entry is emptied only after a write the desktop received, whether that reads as an `if sent` block or an early return, and whether the entry is emptied with `clear()` or by assignment. Confirmed it still fails against the unconditional clear it was written for.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>How this was verified</summary>\n\n- `pnpm --filter desktop run check-types` passes.\n- `pnpm --filter desktop run lint` passes.\n- `pnpm --filter desktop run test:unit` passes: 2,855 tests across 224 files.\n- `pnpm --filter docs run build` succeeds, 101 pages.\n- `cargo clippy --locked --all-targets -- -D warnings` passes for the Linux pill crate.\n- `cargo test --locked` passes for the Linux pill crate: 40 tests.\n\nNew tests, each confirmed to fail before its fix: five for the title bar, twelve for the settings rail and its anchors, eight for the live announcer, two for the release-notes error handling, three for the review captions, and three Rust tests for caption parsing.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>What the review bots found, and what I did about it</summary>\n\nSixty review threads from four bots. Every one has a reply and is resolved. Ten turned out to be real defects, two of them serious bugs I had introduced earlier on this branch.\n\n**Bugs I introduced, now fixed:**\n\n- **The per-message action row was unreachable.** I had used `visibility: hidden` while it was idle. That removes the element's hit box entirely, so the hover handler attached to it could never fire and its buttons could never take focus. The row was unreachable by pointer and by keyboard, which is the opposite of what it was added for. It now fades instead of hiding, and hover and focus are tracked on the bubble, which is always a hit target. A test fails against the old code.\n- **The live region read out saved history.** It counted messages, so opening a conversation announced every stored message at once, and a saved conversation usually ends with the assistant's reply, so the role check alone did not catch it. It now tracks the newest message id, treats the first id seen as history loading, and only speaks for the assistant. A test fails without that guard.\n- **The compact title bar threshold could never fire.** The window's minimum width is 800px, so a 520px threshold was dead code. Moved to 900.\n- **It counted device pixels.** Tauri reports physical pixels while the bar lays out in logical ones, so on a 200% display it would never compact. Now divided by the scale factor.\n- **The traffic light's hover was scoped to the dot,** which is six pixels smaller than the hit target this branch added, so the outer ring of a reachable target did nothing. Now driven from the button.\n- **Escape discarded a half-typed word.** It closed the message editor while an input method was still composing. Escape now belongs to the input method during a composition.\n- **A resize drag re-rendered the bar on every tick.** It stored the raw window width; it now stores the density decision, which only changes at the boundary.\n- **Every 403 was called a rate limit.** GitHub uses 403 for private repositories and policy blocks too, which waiting cannot fix. The response must now identify itself before it earns that label.\n- **One `matchMedia` subscription per message.** Now one shared subscription for the whole list.\n- **My settings map was wrong.** It claimed no control has a Save action, but the Deepgram and Groq key dialogs do, and it named a reset-monitor option the app calls something else. Both corrected, along with two release-flow steps that still described only the stable manifest.\n\n**Findings I declined, with the reason in the thread:**\n\n- Replace an effect cleanup's implicit `undefined` with `return null`. React's `EffectCallback` type rejects it. I tried it and tsc reported the error, so the rule does not apply to React effects.\n- Replace `void promise` with something else. Sonar's rule on this branch *requires* `void` to mark a discarded promise, and the repo uses it throughout. The two analysers conflict, so I followed the one that gates CI.\n- Reduce cyclomatic complexity on the live announcer and the message bubble. The branches are the rules themselves, each with its own reason and its own test. Splitting them would move code without making it clearer.\n- Rename `Writing Styles` to `Styles`. That sentence refers to the setting; `Styles` is the sidebar label.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>How the native pill changes were verified, precisely</summary>\n\nThis is worth being exact about, because \"it compiles\" and \"it runs\" are different claims on different platforms.\n\n- **Linux pill crate:** clippy clean with warnings denied, and 40 tests pass and run here.\n- **Shared pill crate:** 216 tests pass and run here.\n- **Windows pill crate:** compiles and links under a cross-check for the Windows target, and clippy is clean with warnings denied for the library, the binary, and the test targets. The test binary cannot execute on Linux, so those tests run only on a Windows runner, which is what CI does.\n- **macOS pill crate:** clippy clean with warnings denied for the library, the binary, and the test targets under a cross-check for the macOS target. Linking the test binary needs Apple's linker, so the tests run only on a macOS runner, which is what CI does.\n\nThe cross-checks caught two real mistakes that a parse check would have missed: a signature change that had been asserted but never applied, and match arms left disagreeing on their type after that change.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>What this branch does not do</summary>\n\nThis is a defect-fix branch. It does not implement the larger feature work in the 0.1.6 checklist, which is a separate set of changes. During the audit I found and recorded where that work stands; the honest summary is that several of those items are partially built rather than complete, and a few are not built at all. That belongs in its own pull requests so it can be reviewed on its own terms.\n\n</details>\n\nMausAgent | Filed by `stealth/space-bunny-alpha`, with `@` the human-in-the-loop, on 2026-10-02\n\n\n___\n\n## **CodeAnt-AI Description**\nImprove accessibility, localization, navigation, and update recovery across the desktop app\n\n### What Changed\n- Native review controls now use the active app language for every button and instruction, while older payloads retain English fallbacks.\n- Chat messages expose Copy and Edit actions without requiring a context menu, and Escape can cancel message editing without interrupting text composition.\n- Screen readers announce assistant replies starting, finishing, and arriving without repeating saved history or user messages.\n- Settings now has a sticky section navigator with active-section tracking and updated documentation that matches the eight sections currently shipped.\n- Release history identifies rate limits separately, offers a direct releases-page link when loading fails, and renders tables and strikethrough in release notes.\n- Windows preserves edited review text when delivery fails, while title bars adapt to measured window width and macOS traffic lights provide larger clickable targets without enlarging the painted dots.\n- Release and settings documentation now points to current locations and behavior.\n\n### Impact\n `✅ Localized native review controls`\n `✅ Keyboard-accessible chat editing`\n `✅ Clearer screen-reader assistant updates`\n `✅ Faster access to settings sections`\n `✅ Fewer lost transcripts after failed delivery`\n `✅ Clearer release-history failures`\n<details><summary><strong>💡 Usage Guide</strong></summary>\n\n### Checking Your Pull Request\nEvery time you make a pull request, our system automatically looks through it. We check for security issues, mistakes in how you're setting up your infrastructure, and common code problems. We do this to make sure your changes are solid and won't cause any trouble later.\n\n### Talking to CodeAnt AI\nGot a question or need a hand with something in your pull request? You can easily get in touch with CodeAnt AI right here. Just type the following in a comment on your pull request, and replace \"Your question here\" with whatever you want to ask:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Your question here</code>\n</pre>\nThis lets you have a chat with CodeAnt AI about your pull request, making it easier to understand and improve your code.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Can you suggest a safer alternative to storing this secret?</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Preserve Org Learnings with CodeAnt\nYou can record team preferences so CodeAnt AI applies them in future reviews. Reply directly to the specific CodeAnt AI suggestion (in the same thread) and replace \"Your feedback here\" with your input:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Your feedback here</code>\n</pre>\nThis helps CodeAnt AI learn and adapt to your team's coding style and standards.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Do not flag unused imports.</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Retrigger review\nAsk CodeAnt AI to review the PR again, by typing:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: review</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Check Your Repository Health\nTo analyze the health of your code repository, visit our dashboard at [https://app.codeant.ai](https://app.codeant.ai). This tool helps you identify potential issues and areas for improvement in your codebase, ensuring your repository maintains high standards of code health.\n\n</details>\n\n"
