+      "description": "## **User description**\nWhen the post-processing model returns its edits, mausVoice used to apply them even when the reply was wrong in a way that damages what you said. This change makes three of those cases either safe or loud. It touches one file, `apps/desktop/src/utils/ai.utils.ts`, and its test.\n\n**A missing `replace` used to delete your words.** The cleanup prompt tells the model that an empty `replace` means \"delete this text\". The reader treated a `replace` that was absent, `null`, or any non-string as that same empty string. So when a provider dropped the key, the app deleted the phrase and reported success with no warning. A non-string replacement now marks the edit as unusable, the edit is skipped, and the skip count that already produced the warning carries it to you. Sending `\"\"` still deletes, as the prompt asks.\n\n**A `find` in the middle of a word used to splice it.** The old check only asked whether the match was unique, so `find: \"can\"` against \"I cannot attend\" turned it into \"I couldnot attend\". A match now has to land on word edges too. Spaces and punctuation at either end of `find` are still allowed, because the prompt tells the model to copy the surrounding space when deleting a word, and a match that ends on a comma is not a mid-word match. One deliberate limitation: the word test is ASCII only. A script written without spaces has no character adjacency that marks a word edge, so treating one as a violation would reject every edit in that script.\n\n**A reply that changed nothing used to be reported as a failure.** The schema requires both keys, so the model answers `{\"edits\": [], \"result\": \"\"}` when the tone already matches what you said, which is the common case for a speaker whose voice is already right. That fell through to a failure with a warning that blamed the model's token limit. The resolver now tracks whether the reply carried an edit list at all. An empty list with no rewrite resolves as your transcript unchanged and no warning. A reply carrying neither an edit list nor a rewrite is still reported as unusable, because that is the model breaking the contract rather than declining to change anything.\n\nOne related change: the \"N of M edits applied\" warning is now also emitted when nothing applied and the model sent no rewrite. Before, an edit list that produced zero edits passed as a clean success. Its wording now names all three conditions an edit has to meet.\n\n<details>\n<summary>What a user sees now, for each of the three cases</summary>\n\n| Reply | Before | After |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `{\"edits\":[{\"find\":\"the meeting\"}],\"result\":\"\"}` on \"the meeting is at noon\" | \" is at noon\", no warning | \"the meeting is at noon\", warning that 1 of 1 edits did not apply |\n| `{\"edits\":[{\"find\":\"can\",\"replace\":\"could\"}],\"result\":\"\"}` on \"I cannot attend the meeting\" | \"I couldnot attend the meeting\", no warning | \"I cannot attend the meeting\", warning that 1 of 1 edits did not apply |\n| `{\"edits\":[],\"result\":\"\"}` on \"I finished the report today\" | failure, warning that the reply may have been cut off at the token limit | the transcript, no warning |\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Tests and how they were proved</summary>\n\n`apps/desktop/src/utils/ai.utils.test.ts` went from 68 tests to 82. Each finding has a test that fails on the old code and passes on the new, and the new tests were confirmed to fail by reverting each fix on its own:\n\n- Reverting only the missing-`replace` fix: 4 tests fail.\n- Reverting only the word-edge fix: 3 tests fail.\n- Reverting only the no-change-reply fix: 2 tests fail.\n- With all three in place: 82 pass.\n\nOne existing test changed deliberately rather than being deleted. `tolerates a non-string replacement from JSON object mode` asserted that `{\"find\":\"uh \",\"replace\":null}` deleted \"uh \", which is the defect. It now asserts the edit is skipped and counted, and the same case is covered for `42`, `true`, and `{}`. A second existing test, `reports a reply with no usable text`, used `{\"edits\":[],\"result\":\"   \"}`, which is the shape the model sends when it needs no change. It now uses `{\"result\":\"\"}`, a reply with no edit list at all, and still asserts the same `unusable` result and warning. Both changes keep every original assertion, only the fixtures and the pinned wording moved.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Commands run</summary>\n\n```sh\n# in apps/desktop\n./node_modules/.bin/vitest run src/utils/ai.utils.test.ts   # 82 passed\n./node_modules/.bin/vitest run src                          # see below\n./node_modules/.bin/tsc --noEmit                           # see below\n./node_modules/.bin/prettier --check src/utils/ai.utils.ts src/utils/ai.utils.test.ts\n./node_modules/.bin/oxlint src/utils/ai.utils.ts src/utils/ai.utils.test.ts\n```\n\nThe wider `vitest run src` and `tsc --noEmit` runs are not clean, and were not clean before this change either. 35 tests fail in `src/repos/generate-text.repo.test.ts`, `src/repos/model-provider.repo.test.ts`, and `src/repos/transcribe-audio.repo.test.ts` with errors like `isGeminiTranscribeModel is not a function`, and `tsc` reports 23 errors, all of them from stale `dist` output in the sibling workspace packages. Both runs were repeated with this change stashed and the failure sets are identical, so none of it comes from here. The clean way to reproduce is to build the workspace first (`pnpm exec turbo run build --filter=desktop^...`), as `FULL-REVIEW.md` says CI does.\n\nThe live model eval in `apps/desktop/test/evals/post-processing-eval.test.ts` was not run. It needs `GROQ_API_KEY` and no key is available in this environment, so the evidence here is deterministic only. The one place model behaviour could still surprise this change is a provider that leans on mid-word `find` values; a live eval run would show that.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Known gaps this change does not close</summary>\n\n`readEdits` still drops an entry whose `find` is not a string without counting it. Nothing is lost when that happens, because there is no match to apply, so the user only misses a warning. It is left alone to keep this change to the three reported findings.\n\n`previewToneStyle` shows the sample text rather than an empty preview when the model sends the no-change shape. That is the right preview, and it needs no change in `tone-preview.actions.ts`.\n\nThe prompt at `prompt.utils.ts:820` tells the model \"Use an empty `replace` to delete text\" but never says the key must be present, and it never says the model may answer \"no change needed\". Both are one-line wording changes in a file another lane owns.\n\n</details>\n\nCommented By Sasha, on behalf of nuel (@Owie6789)\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n<a href=\"https://cubic.dev/pr/maus-inc/mausVoice/pull/237?utm_source=github\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Review in cubic\" src=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"></picture></a>\n<!-- End of auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n\n\n___\n\n## **CodeAnt-AI Description**\nPrevent post-processing edits from losing dictated words or reporting valid no-change replies as failures\n\n### What Changed\n- Skips edits with missing or non-string replacement text instead of treating them as deletions, while preserving explicit empty replacements for intentional deletion\n- Rejects matches that start or end inside a word, preventing partial replacements such as changing “can” inside “cannot”\n- Keeps valid edits when other edits fail, and reports skipped or unreadable edits with a clear warning\n- Treats an empty edit list with no rewrite as a successful no-change response\n- Reports malformed edit lists as unusable and keeps the original transcript\n\n### Impact\n`✅ Prevented accidental deletion of dictated text`\n`✅ Fewer corrupted words from partial matches`\n`✅ Clearer warnings for unusable cleanup responses`\n<details><summary><strong>💡 Usage Guide</strong></summary>\n\n### Checking Your Pull Request\nEvery time you make a pull request, our system automatically looks through it. We check for security issues, mistakes in how you're setting up your infrastructure, and common code problems. We do this to make sure your changes are solid and won't cause any trouble later.\n\n### Talking to CodeAnt AI\nGot a question or need a hand with something in your pull request? You can easily get in touch with CodeAnt AI right here. Just type the following in a comment on your pull request, and replace \"Your question here\" with whatever you want to ask:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Your question here</code>\n</pre>\nThis lets you have a chat with CodeAnt AI about your pull request, making it easier to understand and improve your code.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Can you suggest a safer alternative to storing this secret?</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Preserve Org Learnings with CodeAnt\nYou can record team preferences so CodeAnt AI applies them in future reviews. Reply directly to the specific CodeAnt AI suggestion (in the same thread) and replace \"Your feedback here\" with your input:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Your feedback here</code>\n</pre>\nThis helps CodeAnt AI learn and adapt to your team's coding style and standards.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Do not flag unused imports.</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Retrigger review\nAsk CodeAnt AI to review the PR again, by typing:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: review</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Check Your Repository Health\nTo analyze the health of your code repository, visit our dashboard at [https://app.codeant.ai](https://app.codeant.ai). This tool helps you identify potential issues and areas for improvement in your codebase, ensuring your repository maintains high standards of code health.\n\n</details>\n"
