+      "description": "## **User description**\n## What this PR is now\n\nBase `integration/0.1.6-staging` already carries the minimal fix for the red check: [#212](https://github.com/maus-inc/mausVoice/pull/212) set the Groq fallback to `openai/gpt-oss-120b` and `Test Desktop Integration` is green on base. This branch is rebased onto that base and **no longer changes the fallback id** — that part is now a duplicate.\n\nWhat is left here is the reason a hardcoded fallback id could go stale for that long in the first place, plus two real defects on the same code path.\n\n## Changes\n\n**1. The fallback is derived, not hardcoded.** `resolveFallbackModel()` picks the first entry of `GENERATE_TEXT_MODELS` that is not the primary, so a retired model cannot silently become the fallback and a new catalog entry is picked up without a second edit.\n\n**2. The default model always has a working second attempt.** The constructor resolves an unknown or unset model to `openai/gpt-oss-20b`, and the old guard was `this.model === this.fallbackModel`. With the fallback pinned to that same id, a failure of the *default* configuration rethrew instead of trying anything, so the shipped default had no retry at all. That was fixed by #212's value; the derived fallback in this PR is what keeps it from regressing.\n\n**3. Account-scoped errors fail fast instead of walking the chain.** A bad request, a bad key, or an exhausted balance fails identically on every model, so `isGroqAccountScopedError` (400, 401, 402) short-circuits both the inner `retry` and the model fallback.\n\n**403 is deliberately not in that set.** Groq publishes 403 as its own `PermissionDeniedError` class with no model-scoped variant, and the per-model access denial rides on a 404, not a 403. Treating 403 as account-fatal would therefore take away the fallback for exactly the case the fallback exists to serve: a model this organisation is not entitled to. There is a test named *\"treats 403 as model-scoped so a denial still reaches the fallback\"* that fails if 403 re-enters the set. 404 stays retryable because Groq returns `model_not_found` for it. 429 stays retryable because Groq rate-limits per model.\n\n**4. The default model id is type-checked against the catalog.** `GROQ_DEFAULT_GENERATE_TEXT_MODEL` is annotated with the literal element type, so a retired id is a build error rather than a runtime 404. Verified negatively: assigning the old `qwen/qwen3.6-27b` fails `tsc` with `TS2322: Type '\"qwen/qwen3.6-27b\"' is not assignable to type '\"openai/gpt-oss-20b\" | \"openai/gpt-oss-120b\"'`.\n\n**5. The vacuous fallback test is now real.** `it.each` case \"falls back to a model the provider still supports\" mocked a *success*, so no fallback ever ran and it asserted on the primary model. That is precisely why the dead id survived. The replacement tests drive `mockRejectedValueOnce(...).mockResolvedValueOnce(...)` and assert the exact id on the second call, in both directions (20b primary, 120b primary, no stored model) plus the account-scoped and abort short-circuits.\n\nThe abort guard is split out of the self-fallback guard rather than sharing it. The model pinned in the abort test is not what makes that test pass: `mockRejectedValueOnce` leaves the second call resolving `undefined`, so removing the abort guard makes `response.text` throw and the expectation fail for either model. The distinct model is there so the test reads as what it checks, which is the abort path.\n\n## Diff\n\n23 files, +1785 / -111 against `integration/0.1.6-staging`, across `apps/desktop/src/repos/generate-text.repo.ts` and its test, `packages/voice-ai/src/groq.utils.ts` and its test, the shared `packages/voice-ai/src/provider-error.utils.ts` that both providers now delegate to, `apps/desktop/src/strategies/dictation.strategy.ts` and its test, the new `apps/desktop/src/actions/post-process-error-category.ts` with its nine translated catalogs plus `en.json`, and the new `packages/voice-ai/src/provider-error.utils.test.ts`.\n\n## Verification\n\nLinux, Node v24.11.0, pnpm 10.34.5, on head `2b018c21`:\n\n```\npnpm --filter desktop check-types                    # exit 0\npnpm --filter desktop lint                           # prettier clean, oxlint clean, ui-lint ok\npnpm --filter @maus-inc/voice-ai test                # 25 files, 237 tests pass\npnpm --filter desktop exec vitest run src/actions/post-process-error-category.test.ts  # 19 tests pass\ngitleaks 8.18.0 detect over the branch range         # no leaks found\n```\n\nThe full `pnpm --filter desktop test` run passes except for the eval and integration suites, which need the `GROQ_API_KEY` CI secret; those run on this branch. That was confirmed against a stashed baseline rather than assumed: the same 3 files and 54 tests fail before and after this head. `check-types` and `lint` are green, and `SonarCloud Code Analysis` reports 0 new issues.\n\n## Model id provenance\n\n`openai/gpt-oss-20b` and `openai/gpt-oss-120b` are the two entries of `GENERATE_TEXT_MODELS` in `packages/voice-ai/src/groq.utils.ts:19-22`, are the two ids the bundled `groq-sdk@0.37.0` accepts in its chat model union (`node_modules/.pnpm/groq-sdk@0.37.0/node_modules/groq-sdk/src/resources/chat/completions.ts:1883-1892`), and are what Groq's own SDK README uses in every example. Both are in `JSON_SCHEMA_SUPPORTED_MODELS`, so a fallback on either keeps full `json_schema` structured output rather than degrading to `json_object`. Neither id can now be replaced by a retired one without a compile error.\n\n## What reaches the user, and what stays in the log\n\nOnly Groq and Cerebras scrub credential material, and the Groq failure text names a model id the provider chose, so a provider message is never rendered. What the user sees is one of seven fixed categories from `classifyPostProcessErrorCategory`, localized into all nine translated catalogs plus `en.json` and shown as *\"Styling failed: {reason}. The raw transcript is saved in History.\"* with an action that opens History. The full provider detail stays in the log, and it is redacted there.\n\nTwo related details worth knowing:\n\n- **The 404 wording is deliberately neutral.** A 404 body from Groq says the model \"does not exist *or you do not have access to it*\", so `GroqModelUnavailableError` says the model \"may be retired, renamed, or not enabled for your account\" rather than asserting a retirement it cannot prove.\n- **A deadline that fires during a retry sleep no longer skips redaction.** The abort branch used to test ambient `signal.aborted` and rethrow the original error untouched. It now decides by error shape, so a credential-echoing 5xx is scrubbed on that path too. The regression test asserts the logger receives no unredacted key, not just that the throw type is unchanged.\n- **A stored category that collides with `Object.prototype` no longer breaks the toast.** `postProcessErrorReason` indexed a plain object literal, so the strings `\"constructor\"` and `\"toString\"` returned an inherited member instead of `undefined`. That member is truthy, so the fallback never fired and the function returned a function where a message descriptor is declared. `formatMessage` raises on the missing `id` before the toast is shown, so the styling-failure toast and its History action would have been lost. The value is read back off the transcription row, where the remote server's own metadata is also stored, so this is reachable and not only a legacy string. Guarded with `Object.hasOwn`, with nine tests covering the inherited names.\n- **The shared module no longer offers a retry policy.** `provider-error.utils.ts` exported `isProviderTerminalStatus`, `isProviderTerminalError` and `ProviderError`, none of which anything referenced. The first returned true for `404`, which is the one status the Groq path above documents at length as never being terminal for it. An unused export in a shared module is not inert, it is the policy the next provider inherits by default. All three are removed, the module header now says why the retry decision is per provider, and a test pins the export surface so one cannot come back.\n\nCommented By Sasha, on behalf of nuel (@Owie6789)\n\nUpdated by Avery on head `2b018c21`.\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nMake Groq post-processing fallback reliable, fail fast on account errors, and provide actionable, safe failure reporting.\n\nNew Features:\n- Add reliable Groq post-processing fallback across supported models, including when the default or unset model fails.\n- Surface actionable errors for unavailable models while redacting API keys from provider messages.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Fail fast on account-scoped Groq errors instead of retrying or switching models.\n- Preserve abort behavior and report both causes when the primary and fallback models fail.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Derive the fallback model from the supported catalog and type-check the default model against it.\n- Classify Groq errors to control retry and fallback behavior for account, model, and transient failures.\n\nTests:\n- Add regression coverage for fallback selection, retry classification, abort handling, unavailable models, dual-model failures, and secret redaction.\n\n\n\n\n___\n\n## **CodeAnt-AI Description**\nMake Groq post-processing fallbacks reliable and failure messages clearer\n\n### What Changed\n- Groq retries a failed model with a distinct supported model, including when the default model is used; account-level failures and cancelled requests fail without unnecessary retries.\n- Failures from unavailable models now preserve both model attempts and actionable retry guidance in diagnostics, while provider credentials and raw provider details are scrubbed.\n- Styling failure toasts now show a localized, safe reason such as quota, authentication, timeout, network, or provider error, with a consistent fallback for unknown categories.\n- Added coverage for fallback selection, retry decisions, secret removal, abort handling, error classification, localization, and safe toast rendering.\n\n### Impact\n`✅ Working fallback after default-model failures`\n`✅ Fewer wasted requests for invalid keys and exhausted quotas`\n`✅ Clearer localized styling failure messages`\n`✅ Provider credentials excluded from failure details`\n<details><summary><strong>💡 Usage Guide</strong></summary>\n\n### Checking Your Pull Request\nEvery time you make a pull request, our system automatically looks through it. We check for security issues, mistakes in how you're setting up your infrastructure, and common code problems. We do this to make sure your changes are solid and won't cause any trouble later.\n\n### Talking to CodeAnt AI\nGot a question or need a hand with something in your pull request? You can easily get in touch with CodeAnt AI right here. Just type the following in a comment on your pull request, and replace \"Your question here\" with whatever you want to ask:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Your question here</code>\n</pre>\nThis lets you have a chat with CodeAnt AI about your pull request, making it easier to understand and improve your code.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Can you suggest a safer alternative to storing this secret?</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Preserve Org Learnings with CodeAnt\nYou can record team preferences so CodeAnt AI applies them in future reviews. Reply directly to the specific CodeAnt AI suggestion (in the same thread) and replace \"Your feedback here\" with your input:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Your feedback here</code>\n</pre>\nThis helps CodeAnt AI learn and adapt to your team's coding style and standards.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: Do not flag unused imports.</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Retrigger review\nAsk CodeAnt AI to review the PR again, by typing:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai: review</code>\n</pre>\n\n### Check Your Repository Health\nTo analyze the health of your code repository, visit our dashboard at [https://app.codeant.ai](https://app.codeant.ai). This tool helps you identify potential issues and areas for improvement in your codebase, ensuring your repository maintains high standards of code health.\n\n</details>\n\n\n<!-- greptile_comment -->\n\n<!-- greptile_summary -->\n\n<h2><a href=\"https://app.greptile.com/api/retrigger?id=69542732\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/RetriggerDark.svg?v=2\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/Retrigger.svg?v=2\"><img alt=\"Retrigger\" src=\"https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/Retrigger.svg?v=2\" align=\"right\"></picture></a>Confidence Score: 5/5</h2>\n\n<!-- greptile-risk -->\n\nThe PR appears safe to merge; no outstanding findings or new issues were identified.\n\n<details><summary>Summary</summary>\n\nThe PR derives a distinct Groq fallback from the supported model catalog, stops retries for account-scoped errors, and adds redacted diagnostics and localized post-processing failure reasons.\n- Adds tests for fallback selection, error handling, and message-catalog coverage.\n</details>\n\n<!-- greptile_confidence_score:5 -->\n\n<sub>Reviews (16) · Last reviewed commit: [\"fix(voice-ai): drop the shared terminal-...\"](https://github.com/maus-inc/mausvoice/commit/2b018c213526e704f32331a1e92074ffa3cca702)</sub>\n\n<!-- /greptile_comment -->\n"
