+      "description": "## **User description**\n## What changed?\n\nThe pill can now cross a shared edge between monitors without the edge resistance stopping it. Windows, macOS, and GTK on X11 detect adjacent display edges and place the crossing limit at the pill center. Exposed screen edges keep their existing work-area clamp.\n\nThe crossing animation now builds from a speed-scaled spring impulse, with up to 14% compression and 8% stretch. Reduced-motion settings use a brief visual pulse instead.\n\nIf released while spanning a shared seam, the drag-only crossing allowance ends and the release settle returns the visible pill fully into the selected monitor’s work area; the pill is not parked straddling displays. Windows computes the default horizontal center in floating point and rounds the final clamped origin to the nearest pixel; an odd work-area/window width difference can shift default centering by one pixel versus integer truncation.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Implementation and verification</summary>\n\n- Added shared monitor-edge geometry and tests for partial overlaps, vertically stacked displays, both seam orientations, monitor-span-relative seam hysteresis (capped at one quarter of the overlap), and release-to-work-area behavior.\n- Replaced the retired Groq Qwen 3.6 fallback with production-tier GPT-OSS 120B after CI exposed the provider-side model shutdown; updated model fallback and structured-output tests.\n- Updated Windows, macOS, and GTK/X11 drag bounds to use the shared-edge center crossing plane.\n- Updated crossing animation tests to use global monitor coordinates.\n- `git diff --check` passed. Rust formatting, tests, and native builds were not run because this environment has no Rust toolchain. CI and bot reviews will provide further verification.\n- GTK Wayland placement remains compositor-managed; this change does not add X11-style global monitor-coordinate placement to that backend.\n\n</details>\n\n## How is it tested?\n\n- [ ] Automated tests\n- [ ] Manual verification\n- [x] Code inspection and `git diff --check`\n\nMausAgent | Filed by `arena-ai-coding-agent[bot]`, with `@Owie6789`, on `2026-09-25`.\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n<a href=\"https://cubic.dev/pr/maus-inc/mausVoice/pull/212?utm_source=github\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Review in cubic\" src=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"></picture></a>\n<!-- End of auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nEnable smooth pill dragging across connected monitors while preserving safe exposed-edge clamping and reliable release placement.\n\nNew Features:\n- Allow pill dragging to cross connected monitor seams by opening only shared edges while retaining work-area limits on exposed edges.\n- Add monitor-topology-aware seam hysteresis and release behavior that returns the pill fully within the selected monitor’s work area.\n- Add speed-sensitive crossing deformation with stronger spring compression/stretch and reduced-motion pulse behavior.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Stabilize monitor scaling, topology changes, and fallback placement across Windows, macOS, and GTK/X11 dragging.\n- Use floating-point Windows centering before final pixel rounding to avoid one-pixel centering errors.\n- Replace the retired Groq fallback model with GPT-OSS 120B and update structured-output coverage.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Centralize shared monitor-edge geometry and drag-boundary handling across native pill backends.\n\nCI:\n- Run Windows native-pill lint through the shared diagnostics wrapper.\n\nTests:\n- Expand geometry, seam hysteresis, release clamping, animation, topology fallback, and model structured-output tests.\n\n\n___\n\n## **CodeAnt-AI Description**\nLet the pill cross connected monitor edges smoothly and settle safely\n\n### What Changed\n- Dragging can cross shared monitor edges without edge resistance, while exposed edges still keep work-area limits for panels and taskbars\n- Partial monitor seams remain usable near overlap boundaries without flickering between blocked and open states\n- Releasing at a shared seam ends the crossing allowance and returns the pill fully inside the selected monitor’s work area\n- Monitor scaling and placement now use consistent coordinates across Windows, macOS, and X11, including nearest-pixel default centering on Windows\n- Monitor crossings use a speed-scaled spring deformation, while reduced-motion users receive a brief pulse instead\n- Groq text generation now falls back to GPT-OSS 120B when the configured model fails\n\n### Impact\n`✅ Unblocked monitor-to-monitor dragging`\n`✅ No pills left straddling monitors after release`\n`✅ Consistent multi-monitor placement on scaled displays`\n`✅ Smoother crossing animation`\n`✅ Reliable Groq fallback responses`\n<details><summary><strong>💡 Usage Guide</strong></summary>\n\n### Checking Your Pull Request\nEvery time you make a pull request, our system automatically looks through it. We check for security issues, mistakes in how you're setting up your infrastructure, and common code problems. We do this to make sure your changes are solid and won't cause any trouble later.\n\n### Talking to CodeAnt AI\nGot a question or need a hand with something in your pull request? You can easily get in touch with CodeAnt AI right here. Just type the following in a comment on your pull request, and replace \"Your question here\" with whatever you want to ask:\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Your question here</code>\n</pre>\nThis lets you have a chat with CodeAnt AI about your pull request, making it easier to understand and improve your code.\n\n#### Example\n<pre>\n<code>@codeant-ai ask: Can you s