+      "description": "## What changed?\n\nMausAgent cut the wait between the user stopping a dictation and the text arriving. The full investigation, measurements, ranked opportunities, and roadmap are in `reports/transcription-latency-deep-dive.md`.\n\n- `stop_recording` returns raw `[u32 rate][f32 LE samples]` bytes through `tauri::ipc::Response` instead of a JSON number array (about 36 MB per minute at 48 kHz). One decoder in `recorded-audio.utils.ts` serves the dictation stop path, the microphone tester, and the composer voice recorder. The command stays registered in `app.rs` but leaves the Specta bindings, since Specta cannot describe `ipc::Response`.\n- Transcription starts as soon as audio arrives. The a11y lookup, app target registration, and style write now run alongside it instead of in front of it.\n- All 13 batch repos upload anti-aliased 16 kHz WAV, a third of the bytes. History keeps native-rate audio.\n- Batch cloud and local sessions pretranscribe pause-aligned spans while the user speaks, verify them against the final recording, and transcribe only the tail at stop. Any mismatch or span failure falls back to today's whole-recording request. Dictations under about 12 s are unchanged.\n\nModeled effect for Groq on a 10 Mbps uplink: 60 s dictation about 6.8 s to 0.9 s, 120 s about 13 s to 0.9 s.\n\nChunks carry their absolute sample offset (`audio_chunk.offset`), so spans stay aligned even though the listener attaches after capture starts. Canceling mid-finalize skips the whole-recording fallback. Span requests already in flight at cancel are not aborted; their results are discarded.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\n- [x] Automated tests: 27 new Vitest tests (decoder, resampler anti-aliasing, pretranscriber cuts, late-listener alignment, gaps, fallback, divergence, CJK and astral Han joining, 90 s continuous speech), one Rust unit test for the byte encoding. The full desktop suite passes except the 54 key-dependent eval and integration tests that also fail on the base branch.\n- [ ] Manual verification: needs a desktop smoke test of dictation, microphone test, and composer voice edit on each OS.\n- [x] Code inspection\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nCut post-stop transcription latency by optimizing audio handoff and upload size, overlapping stop processing, and pretranscribing reliable pause-aligned spans during recording.\n\nNew Features:\n- Reduce post-stop dictation latency with pause-aligned pretranscription for batch cloud and local sessions, falling back to whole-recording transcription when results cannot be trusted.\n- Use a shared audio decoding path for dictation, microphone testing, and composer voice recording.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Prevent canceled transcription work from triggering unnecessary requests, retries, or whole-recording fallbacks.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Replace JSON audio sample handoff with a compact binary IPC response and preserve absolute offsets on live audio chunks.\n- Start transcription independently of accessibility, app-target, and style-processing work.\n- Downsample batch transcription uploads to anti-aliased 16 kHz audio while retaining native-rate audio for history.\n- Propagate cancellation through transcription repositories, provider requests, and local sidecar sessions.\n\nDocumentation:\n- Add a transcription latency investigation, measurements, and follow-up optimization roadmap.\n\nTests:\n- Add coverage for binary audio decoding, resampling quality, pause-based pretranscription, stream alignment and fallback behavior, cancellation, transcript joining, and local sidecar abort handling.\n\nChores:\n- Remove the stop-recording command from generated Specta bindings because its raw IPC response is not representable there."
