+      "description": "## What this fixes\n\nThis pull request resolves the five high-priority review findings left open on PR #187 (the automatic dictionary word addition repair). The most serious risk was that enabling the feature could silently add a 20% ElevenLabs transcription surcharge, or build provider requests that exceeded documented limits. This change puts the user in control of that cost, makes the Linux settings copy honest, and keeps the speech-recognition vocabulary hints inside each provider's documented limits.\n\n## Changes\n\n<details>\n<summary>1. ElevenLabs keyterms now require an explicit opt-in (the 20% surcharge consent)</summary>\n\nA new \"ElevenLabs keyterms\" preference defaults to off. The More settings dialog shows it only when the transcription provider is ElevenLabs. Turning it on opens a confirmation dialog that states the 20% surcharge up front; only after the user accepts does the dictionary get sent as keyterms. When the preference is off, no keyterms leave the app and no surcharge is incurred, even if the dictionary is non-empty.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>2. Linux users are no longer sent to a setting that does not exist</summary>\n\nThe \"Auto-learn dictionary\" description previously told every user that corrections in other apps are handled by \"Learn from corrections\". That setting only exists on macOS and Windows, so on Linux it pointed at nothing. The description is now platform-aware: Linux shows wording that explains out-of-app corrections are not captured.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>3. The transcription prompt now biases toward replacement spellings, not just raw glossary words</summary>\n\n`buildLocalizedTranscriptionPrompt` now collects vocabulary through the shared `collectVocabularyTerms` helper, so canonical replacement destinations (for example preferring \"Soniya\" over \"soniya\") are included and source/destination collisions are resolved, instead of only the raw glossary sources.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>4. AssemblyAI streaming now pins the model that supports vocabulary hints</summary>\n\nThe AssemblyAI v3 WebSocket previously connected without a `speech_model` parameter and relied on the account default, where an older model could silently ignore `keyterms_prompt`. It now requests `speech_model=universal-3-5-pro` while still sending `keyterms_prompt`. A new contract test asserts both parameters are present on the connection.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>5. Vocabulary caps are measured in characters the way providers count them</summary>\n\nTerm lengths are now measured in Unicode code points (so emoji and other multi-byte text count as one character, as providers enforce per-term limits) rather than JavaScript's UTF-16 units. The character budgets are documented as heuristics that approximate each provider's token/byte ceiling, not exact token counts. The localized instruction is subtracted from the Whisper glossary budget so the full `initial_prompt` (instruction plus terms) stays under the model's ceiling.\n\n</details>\n\n## Verification\n\n- `pnpm --filter desktop check-types`: clean\n- `pnpm --filter desktop lint` (prettier + oxlint): clean\n- `pnpm --filter desktop test:unit`: 125 files, 1341 tests green\n- `pnpm --filter @maus-inc/voice-ai test`: 22 files, 163 tests green\n- i18n catalog: idempotent after extract + sync; translation-completeness test green\n- New regression tests: ElevenLabs keyterms gating and AssemblyAI streaming connection parameters\n\n## Docs\n\n`AUTO-LEARN-DICTIONARY-REVIEW.md` updated to reflect the opt-in, the pinned speech model, and the Unicode-aware budget.\n\nMausAgent | Filed by MausAgent, with @Owie6789, on 2026-09-09\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n<a href=\"https://cubic.dev/pr/maus-inc/mausVoice/pull/188?utm_source=github\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Review in cubic\" src=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"></picture></a>\n<!-- End of auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nGive users control over ElevenLabs keyterms costs and keep automatic transcription vocabulary hints accurate, compatible, and within provider limits.\n\nNew Features:\n- Add an opt-in ElevenLabs keyterms preference with explicit 20% surcharge confirmation.\n- Pin AssemblyAI streaming transcription to a keyterms-compatible speech model.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Prevent ElevenLabs dictionary keyterms from being sent, and surcharges from being incurred, without user consent.\n- Provide accurate Linux auto-learn settings guidance.\n- Keep transcription vocabulary hints within provider limits, including Unicode-aware term sizing and the full localized Whisper prompt budget.\n- Include canonical replacement spellings in localized transcription prompts.\n\nDocumentation:\n- Update the auto-learn dictionary review documentation for keyterms consent, AssemblyAI model selection, and Unicode-aware vocabulary budgets.\n\nTests:\n- Add regression coverage for ElevenLabs keyterms consent gating and AssemblyAI streaming connection parameters."
