+      "description": "feat(expansion): port event contracts and ephemeral sessions from PR 148\n\nPRs #148 and #149 both built the same thing. They both deliver the shared\nfoundations that every later expansion feature needs, and they both claim\nmigration number `075`. Only one can merge.\n\n#149 stores feature flags as a single JSON column instead of nine separate\ndatabase columns, so a future flag needs no database change at all. That design\nwins. This pull request takes the parts of #148 that #149 never built, rebuilds\nthem on top of #149's design, and leaves #148 ready to close.\n\nWhat #149 was missing and now has:\n\n- A list of named events that expansion features will broadcast, such as\n  `meeting_started` or `webhook_failed`. Each name is bound to the exact shape\n  of data it carries, so a listener cannot misread its own event.\n- One React hook, `useExpansionEventListener`, that subscribes to any of those\n  events and infers the data shape from the event name.\n- Six extra filters in the Rust log scrubber for LLM prompts, webhook payloads\n  and URLs, connector credentials, meeting transcripts, and translation text.\n- Ephemeral sessions, a temporary mode where nothing new is written to disk.\n- `workflows.types.ts`, the one domain shape #149 had no equivalent for.\n\nTwo real bugs surfaced while wiring this up, and both are fixed here. One would\nhave made an ephemeral session survive an app restart. The other would have made\nthe whole feature suppress nothing. Details are in the dropdowns below.\n\n<details>\n<summary>The two defects, and why they matter</summary>\n\n**1. An ephemeral session would have survived a restart.**\n\n#148 stored the live session flag in `LocalState` and described it as\n\"session-scoped, non-persistent\". That description was not true. The zustand\npersist middleware is configured with\n`partialize: (state) => ({ local: state.local })`, which writes the entire\n`local` object to `localStorage`. A user who started an ephemeral session and\nthen restarted the app would come back with persistence still suppressed and no\nsession in progress, with no visible way to tell.\n\nFix: the store now defines a `merge` handler that resets\n`ephemeralSessionActive` on rehydration. As a side effect it also overlays\nstored values on top of the defaults, so a `local` field added after a user's\nsaved snapshot now gets its initial value instead of coming back `undefined`.\n\n**2. `isPersistenceAllowed()` had no consumers on the paths that write.**\n\n#149 introduced `isPersistenceAllowed()` as the single privacy gate, but\ndeliberately left the two places that actually store transcripts and audio\nreading `incognitoModeEnabled` directly, and noted the migration as a follow-up.\nAdding an ephemeral session to a gate that nothing reads would have suppressed\nnothing at all.\n\nFix: `storeTranscription` and the remote transcript store now call\n`isPersistenceAllowed()`. Incognito behavior is preserved exactly. The separate\n`incognitoModeIncludeInStats` option still counts words toward usage statistics\nfor incognito mode only, and never for an ephemeral session, because an\nephemeral session never opted into statistics.\n\nThe audio snapshot guard inside `storeTranscription` was removed. It checked\n`!incognitoEnabled` immediately below a gate that already returns early whenever\nincognito is on, so it was provably always true and encoded the same invariant a\nsecond time.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>What was deliberately not ported from #148</summary>\n\n| #148 | Why it was dropped |\n| --- | --- |\n| `075_feature_flags.sql`, nine boolean columns | Conflicts with #149's `075_expansion_flags.sql`. The JSON column needs no migration per new flag. |\n| `packages/types/src/` domain types for connector, meeting, snippet, translation, webhook | #149's Decision 5 deliberately keeps these in `apps/desktop/src/types/`, and already ships richer versions. Porting them would create two competing definitions of `Meeting`, `SnippetVariable`, `WebhookDelivery`, and `ConnectorType`. |\n| `packages/types/src/feature-flag.types.ts` and the per-flag fields on `UserPreferences` | Superseded by `expansion_flags`. |\n| `services/feature-flags.service.ts`, `state/feature-flags.state.ts`, `utils/feature-flags.utils.ts` | Superseded by #149's `features/featureFlags.ts`, which also serializes concurrent toggles. |\n| Per-flag wiring in `preferences.repo.ts`, `onboarding.actions.ts`, `user.actions.ts`, and the Rust preferences queries | All of it exists only to carry the nine columns. |\n| `docs/.../shared-foundations-research.md` from #148 | #149 has its own doc set at the same path. |\n\nThe `ephemeralSessionEnabled` preference is the one piece of #148's flag\ntaxonomy that was kept. It became an entry in `EXPANSION_FLAG_NAMES` rather than\na database column, which is exactly what #149's design allows. No migration, no\nRust change, no repository change, because `expansion_flags` is an opaque JSON\nstring everywhere outside `expansion-flags.types.ts`.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Design change: one typed hook instead of sixteen</summary>\n\n#148 added 234 lines to `apps/desktop/src/hooks/tauri.hooks.ts`, consisting of\nsixteen hooks whose bodies were identical apart from the event constant and the\npayload type. Each body repeated the same subscription call and the same error\nrouting.\n\nThat shape duplicates logic sixteen times and lets any one hook drift from the\npayload type it claims to carry. This pull request replaces all of it with:\n\n- An `ExpansionEventPayloads` map in `packages/desktop-utils/src/tauri-events.ts`\n  that binds each event name to its payload type. It is the single source of\n  truth. Adding an event means adding one entry.\n- One hook, `useExpansionEventListener(eventName, callback)`, which infers the\n  payload type from the event name and reuses the snackbar error routing that\n  `useTauriListen` in the same file already provides.\n\nThe result is 15 lines instead of 234, and a call site such as\n`useExpansionEventListener(MEETING_STARTED_EVENT, (payload) => ...)` receives a\n`payload` that TypeScript already knows is a `MeetingStartedPayload`.\n\nThe two pre-existing events, `keys_held` and `bridge_hotkey_trigger`, stay on\ntheir own bespoke hooks in `desktop-utils`. They carry extra semantics such as\nduplicate suppression that do not generalize.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Files changed</summary>\n\nEvent contracts and the listener:\n\n- `packages/desktop-utils/src/tauri-events.ts` (+140)\n- `apps/desktop/src/hooks/tauri.hooks.ts` (+15)\n\nEphemeral sessions:\n\n- `apps/desktop/src/state/local.state.ts` (+2)\n- `apps/desktop/src/store/index.ts` (+17)\n- `apps/desktop/src/actions/ephemeral-session.actions.ts` (new, 54)\n- `apps/desktop/src/utils/incognito.utils.ts` (+13/-2)\n- `apps/desktop/src/types/expansion-flags.types.ts` (+2)\n\nPersistence gate migration:\n\n- `apps/desktop/src/actions/transcribe.actions.ts`\n- `apps/desktop/src/actions/remote-transcript.actions.ts`\n\nRust log scrubber:\n\n- `apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/utils/log_sanitizer.rs` (+97)\n\nDomain types:\n\n- `apps/desktop/src/types/workflows.types.ts` (new, 33)\n\nTests, 22 new across 5 files:\n\n- `apps/desktop/src/store/index.test.ts` (new, 4)\n- `apps/desktop/src/utils/incognito.utils.test.ts` (new, 5)\n- `apps/desktop/src/actions/ephemeral-session.actions.test.ts` (new, 6)\n- `apps/desktop/src/actions/transcribe.actions.test.ts` (+4)\n- `apps/desktop/src/types/expansion-flags.types.test.ts` (+3)\n\nDocs:\n\n- `shared-foundations-decisions.md`, Decisions 6, 7, and 8, plus a status note\n  on Decision 4\n- `shared-foundations.md`, the event contract, ephemeral session, and incognito\n  sections, acceptance criteria, and the handoff list\n- `shared-foundations-verification.md`, a dated section for this port\n- `expansion-program-status.md`, a log entry and the PR number for pole 1\n\nLocale catalogs, 10 files, one key each. See the note below.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Verification: what ran, and what did not</summary>\n\nRun locally and passing on this head:\n\n| Check | Command | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Type check | `pnpm --filter desktop check-types` | Pass |\n| Lint | `pnpm --filter desktop lint` | Pass, prettier clean and oxlint 0 warnings over 384 files |\n| Unit tests | `pnpm --filter desktop test:unit` | Pass, 418 tests across 31 files |\n| voice-ai tests | `pnpm --filter @maus-inc/voice-ai test` | Pass |\n| Repo-wide types | `pnpm run check-types` | Pass, 8 turbo tasks |\n| Formatting | `pnpm exec prettier --check` on every changed file | Pass |\n| i18n | `pnpm --filter desktop i18n` | Idempotent, 0 added on re-run |\n\n`pnpm --filter @repo/agent test` is a no-op because that package defines no test\nscript.\n\nNot run locally, left to CI:\n\n- `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` and `cargo test --lib`. This sandbox has no Rust\n  toolchain. As a substitute, a script parsed all eleven rules out of\n  `log_sanitizer.rs` and replayed every assertion in its test module against an\n  equivalent regex engine. 22 of 22 pass. The same script confirmed no raw string\n  literal in the file carries more `#` characters than its content requires,\n  which is what `clippy::needless_raw_string_hashes` checks. That lint is\n  warn-by-default and CI runs clippy with `-D warnings`, so it would have failed\n  the build. The four rules ported verbatim from #148 did carry needless hashes,\n  and #148's own check list contains none of this repository's workflows, so that\n  Rust code was likely never compiled or linted anywhere.\n- `pnpm gen:bindings`. No Tauri command and no Specta type changed, so no binding\n  change is expected.\n- `pnpm --filter desktop test:integration`. Both files throw\n  `Missing required environment variable: GROQ_API_KEY` before reaching any\n  changed code. Not runnable here. CI holds the secret and passed on #149.\n\nA note on the locale catalogs. Running the repository's own i18n script adds one\nkey, `failed_to_restart_mausvoice_as_administrator`, to all ten catalogs. That\nstring was already declared in `native.actions.ts` on this base branch and was\nmissing from every catalog before this pull request. The sync is included so the\ni18n gate reports no diff. It is pre-existing drift, not something this change\nintroduced.\n\nA note on markdown formatting. Three docs in\n`docs/competitor-research/implementation-plans/` already failed\n`prettier --check` on the base branch. Since this pull request edits them anyway,\nprettier was run on them. The changes are blank lines after headings and\nre-aligned table padding, with no content edits. `shared-foundations-research.md`\nwas left untouched because this pull request does not otherwise change it.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Still open for later poles</summary>\n\n- Emitters for the fourteen expansion events that nothing fires yet. Ephemeral\n  sessions already emit their two, `ephemeral_session_started` and\n  `ephemeral_session_ended`, through `emit` from `@tauri-apps/api/event`. The\n  `core:event:default` capability already grants emit, and\n  `end-conversation.tool.ts` uses the same API today.\n- Settings UI for the expansion flag toggles, including\n  `ephemeralSessionEnabled`.\n- A UI indicator for an active ephemeral session.\n\n`apps/docs` was intentionally not updated. The incognito page in\n`apps/docs/src/content/docs/privacy-security/history-incognito.md` describes a\nfeature users can reach through settings. Ephemeral sessions have no UI yet, so\nthere is no user-visible behavior to document. It should be documented in the\npole that ships the toggle.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Handoff notes for the next polecat</summary>\n\nBuilding meeting notes on top of this:\n\n- `isExpansionFeatureEnabled(\"meetingNotesEnabled\")` from\n  `src/features/featureFlags.ts` for the gate.\n- `isPersistenceAllowed()` from `src/utils/incognito.utils.ts` before any write.\n  It covers incognito mode and ephemeral sessions in one call.\n- `apps/desktop/src/types/meetings.types.ts` for domain types.\n- `MEETING_STARTED_EVENT`, `MEETING_STOPPED_EVENT`, and\n  `MEETING_SUMMARY_GENERATED_EVENT` from `@maus-inc/desktop-utils` to emit.\n- `useExpansionEventListener(MEETING_STARTED_EVENT, (payload) => ...)` to\n  subscribe, with `payload` inferred.\n\nTo add a new event, add the name constant and the payload type in\n`tauri-events.ts`, then add one entry to `ExpansionEventPayloads`. No hook needs\nwriting.\n\nTo add a new flag, add the name to `EXPANSION_FLAG_NAMES` and a default to\n`DEFAULT_EXPANSION_FLAGS`. The `Record` type makes the compiler reject a missing\ndefault. No migration needed.\n\n</details>\n\nMausAgent | Filed by Arena.ai Agent Mode, model slug not disclosed in this\nenvironment, with @Owie6789, on 2026-09-05\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nPort the remaining shared expansion foundations onto the JSON-backed feature-flag design, including typed events, ephemeral sessions, privacy-safe persistence, and expanded log redaction.\n\nNew Features:\n- Add typed contracts for sixteen expansion events and a generic React listener hook with payload inference.\n- Add ephemeral session controls that broadcast lifecycle events and suppress persistence for the session.\n- Add workflow domain types and an expansion flag for enabling ephemeral sessions.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Prevent ephemeral session state from surviving application restarts.\n- Apply the centralized persistence gate to local and remote transcription writes so ephemeral sessions actually suppress storage.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Extend Rust log sanitization to redact prompts, webhook data and URLs, connector credentials, meeting transcripts, and translation content.\n\nDocumentation:\n- Document the event contracts, ephemeral session behavior, privacy gating decisions, verification results, and expansion-program handoff.\n\nTests:\n- Add coverage for ephemeral session lifecycle, store rehydration, persistence suppression, feature-flag defaults, and expanded log sanitization.\n\nChores:\n- Synchronize locale catalogs and expansion status documentation."
