+      "description": "This branch rebuilds the 1.6 release work on top of `fix/superfix-review-findings` by combining three open PRs that were never cleanly stacked: #137 (issue triage fixes), #142 (auto-learn dictionary) and #145 (repo-wide Prettier and i18n CI gates). It replaces #165, which attempted the same combination through a rebase and silently reverted about 5,987 lines of the base branch. Everything in those three PRs is here, the base branch loses nothing, and the quality gates are clean.\n\nIf you only test one thing, test that the app still builds and that dictation, the native pill and the API-key settings behave exactly as they do on `fix/superfix-review-findings` today. Nothing in this branch is meant to change behaviour beyond what #137, #142 and #145 already introduced.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Why #165 had to be replaced</summary>\n\n#165 rebased the three branches instead of merging them. A rebase replays each commit onto a new base, so when a commit touched a file the base had since changed, the older version won. Comparing #165 against the base showed roughly 5,987 deleted lines across 102 files, including definitions whose callers and tests were kept. Those pairs would not have compiled or would have failed at runtime.\n\nSymbols that lost their definition while keeping their callers: `setReviewBeforeInsert`, `checkAndDrainBacklog`, `quitApp`, `filterKnownSilenceHallucinations`, `setAgentToolEnabled`, `normalizeAgentMaxIterations`, `hasDictationBacklog`, and four others.\n\nThis branch uses three-way merges instead, with a type-check run after each step. All eleven of those symbols are present here at full reference count.\n\nThree claims in the #165 review were checked and did not hold up:\n\n| Claim | Finding |\n| --- | --- |\n| `transcription.rs` passes a closure to `str::matches`, which \"won't compile\" | Incorrect. `Pattern` is implemented for `FnMut(char) -> bool`. Rust CI passes. |\n| `app.rs` is missing a `#[cfg(target_os = \"windows\")]` guard | Incorrect. The guard is four lines above the cited line. |\n| `user.actions.ts` removed a mutation queue, creating a race | Misdescribed. No queue existed. `AsyncLock` only increments a counter and never serialises. That is a real pre-existing bug on the base branch and is deliberately out of scope here. |\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>How the three PRs were combined</summary>\n\nMerge order was #137, then #142, then #145, each as a three-way merge with `pnpm --filter desktop check-types` run afterwards. Type errors after every step: 0.\n\n#137 and #145 merged with no conflicts. #142 produced 31 conflicts, all resolved by taking the union of both sides rather than picking one:\n\n- `preferences.types.ts` keeps all nine fields from both sides plus `handsFreeDelayMs`.\n- Ten locale catalogues merged to 818 keys with the German translations preserved.\n- Auto-learn migrations renumbered so the sequence stays gapless and no applied migration is renumbered.\n- `output-routing.utils.ts` keeps the base `insertLocalOutput` plus the #142 clipboard fallback and `beginEditWatch`.\n- `MoreSettingsDialog.tsx` keeps the base `logOnRejection` and gains the #142 hands-free delay UI.\n\nThe eight migrations were diffed against their originals after renumbering and are byte-identical.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Bugs found and fixed while verifying</summary>\n\n**Native pill reject button never worked.** The initial conflict resolution kept the `FlashReject` enum variant and the `flash_reject_action` state fields but dropped everything that produced or consumed them. `ClickAction::FlashReject` had no match arm in any of the three pill crates, which is a non-exhaustive match and a hard compile error. The state fields were declared but never initialised, and `InMessage::Toast` had lost the `reject_action` fields on Linux and macOS even though the desktop side already sends them. Windows had also lost the reject button rendering. All three crates now declare, render, receive and handle the reject action.\n\n**Reject-only toasts hid their own button.** `flash_banner_target` was only told about `flash_action`, so a toast carrying just a reject action collapsed the banner behind a revealed tooltip. It now considers both actions.\n\n**Punctuation-heavy input could hang the tokeniser.** `auto-learn.utils.ts` trimmed token edges with a quantified character class anchored only at the end, which makes the regex engine retry from every offset. Replaced with a two-pointer scan, verified equivalent to the old behaviour across all 54,241 strings of length 0 to 4 over a 15-character alphabet.\n\n**Dead dependency.** `@google/genai` was declared but never imported; `gemini.utils.ts` calls the REST endpoint directly. Removing it dropped 168 lines of lockfile.\n\n**Stale test models.** The Cerebras and DeepSeek provider tests referenced `llama3.1-8b` and `deepseek-chat`, neither of which appears in `CEREBRAS_MODELS` or `DEEPSEEK_MODELS`.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Quality gates</summary>\n\nSonarCloud on new code: 0 issues, 0 security hotspots, 0 accepted issues. Duplication was driven from 3.3 percent to 0 by extracting shared helpers rather than by suppressing the rule:\n\n- `drainSamples` existed twice after the rebuild extracted it; the audio chunk pump now delegates to the extracted helper.\n- The six-field flash reset was copy-pasted at nine sites across the three pill crates; it now lives in `rust_pill_shared::clear_flash_state`.\n- Two 42-line Groq test blocks collapsed into one helper.\n- Seven near-identical metacharacter tests collapsed into one table.\n\nTightening those test assertions surfaced real behaviour the old \"does not throw\" checks hid: `applyReplacements` matches on word boundaries and leaves trailing punctuation alone, so `foo[bar]` becomes `FB]` and `C++` becomes `Rust++`. The table now pins the exact output.\n\nLocal verification: `check-types` clean, `lint` clean (0 warnings, 0 errors), desktop 1,060 unit tests pass, voice-ai 103 tests pass. The three failing desktop files are the eval and integration suites that need `GROQ_API_KEY`, which is not available in the sandbox; they fail identically on the base branch.\n\nRust could not be compiled locally because `static.rust-lang.org` and `index.crates.io` are blocked in this sandbox, so Rust correctness is proven by the Lint Desktop and Build Desktop CI jobs on this PR.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Not included</summary>\n\n- The `AsyncLock` serialisation bug is pre-existing on the base branch and left alone.\n- The Gemini REST migration WIP commit is a separate unverified refactor and was excluded. It remains cherry-pickable.\n\n</details>\n\n---\nMausAgent | Filed by `claude-sonnet-4-6`, with `@Owie6789`, on 2026-09-05\n"
