+      "description": "Stacked on PR #132 (and the follow-ups #133 and #134 it already merges in).\n\nThis pull request answers the issues you raised during the deep review of the interaction feedback (thock) feature. In short, it makes the click sound tighter, adds a real volume control, keeps the desktop agent running after a tool call fails, removes a blank white page that could stall the review window, and stops a mid-dictation style switch from reverting to the old default. Every behavior change has a test, and the full CI suite passes.\n\n## What changed\n\n### Thock click and user volume\nThe old bass-heavy thock clips are replaced with short 30ms band-limited click transients, so the sound reads as a tight click instead of a thud. A new `interactionFeedbackVolume` preference flows end to end: zod schema, TypeScript user field, Rust domain model, migration 79, the new `user_profiles` column, a Rust atomic in `audio_feedback.rs`, and the `set_interaction_feedback_volume` Tauri command. The volume slider in the Audio dialog drives it, and the sink clamps the gain to the safe window so the speaker can never blow out.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Implementation detail</summary>\n\n- Migration `079_interaction_feedback_volume.sql` adds the column with a `0.35` default.\n- `user_queries.rs` binds and reads the column at position 13 of the 22-column insert.\n- `audio_feedback.rs` exposes `INTERACTION_FEEDBACK_VOLUME` (an atomic) and clamps to `[0.05, 0.5]`.\n- `AppSideEffects.tsx` mirrors the pref into Rust at startup and on every slider commit, but only on the main window (an `isMainWindow` guard) so secondary windows do not fight over the sink.\n- `UserZod` enforces the same `[0.05, 0.5]` range, and `user_queries.rs` clamps before binding to SQLite.\n- Locales (en, de, es, fr, it, ko, pt, pt-BR, zh-CN, zh-TW) get the \"Interaction feedback volume\" label; pt and pt-BR read \"Volume do feedback de interação\".\n\n</details>\n\n### Desktop agent keeps running after a tool call\nA rejected `createChatMessage` (the exact \"resource id is invalid\" shape from your diagnostics) used to escape the `for await` loop and end the run. A new `safeSideEffect(label, context, fn)` helper in `run-agent.ts` isolates that rejection. The package-level `AgentLoop` is unchanged. The loop also cleans up its in-memory streaming entry in a `finally` block, so a failed persistence can no longer strand a message as an endless \"streaming\" bubble.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Regression tests</summary>\n\n- `run-agent-integration.test.ts` forces the same `createChatMessage` rejection and asserts the loop yields the next event and resolves normally.\n- A second test asserts the streaming entry is removed even when assistant-message persistence rejects.\n\n</details>\n\n### Review window no longer stalls on a blank page\nThe pill is now told to go idle before the review window opens, so it stays usable while you review. A 15s `composer-ready` safety net cancels the review and shows the \"Open history\" recovery toast if the webview never reports ready (the blank white page class). `ComposerPage.tsx` emits `composer-ready` on mount to clear the timer for healthy webviews. The readiness gate uses a boolean `composerReady` flag plus `armReadyTimeout()` so a fast-mounting page is never killed by its own safety net.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Why the old guard raced</summary>\n\nThe previous code scheduled `setTimeout` after registering the `composer-ready` listener. A page that mounted and emitted `composer-ready` before the timeout was armed would still be torn down 15s later. The boolean flag makes `armReadyTimeout()` a no-op once the page is already mounted.\n\n</details>\n\n### Mid-dictation style switch keeps the new default\n`saveManualStyleForApp` already reads the live selection, but the contract was undocumented and untested. The code now awaits the persistence before finalization, and a regression test drives a mid-dictation switch and asserts the live selection becomes the new per-app default.\n\n### Serde null onboarding fix\nThe onboarding IPC rejected null fields. That is now handled so a missing optional value does not crash onboarding.\n\n### Review hygiene\nThis final commit clears the remaining non-blocking DeepSource findings: the two fire-and-forget IPC mirrors in `user.actions.ts` now log a verbose message instead of swallowing the error with an empty catch, and the `AgentLoop` mock in the integration test drops its useless constructor and empty `abort()` and uses a bound `run()`.\n\n## How to test\n1. Open Settings, find Interaction feedback, toggle it on, and move the volume slider. The click should sound tight and change loudness between 5 percent and 50 percent.\n2. Start a dictation, switch the app's writing style mid-recording, stop, and start again. The new style should persist as the default.\n3. Trigger a review-before-insert. The pill should stay idle and usable, and if the review window fails to load you should get the recovery toast instead of a hang.\n4. Run an agent task that calls a tool. Even if message persistence fails, the agent should keep going.\n\nMausAgent | Filed by `tencent/hy3:free` with `@owie6789` on 2026-08-26\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nImprove interaction feedback, agent reliability, review recovery, and user-preference persistence across the desktop application.\n\nNew Features:\n- Add configurable interaction-feedback click volume with persisted user settings and localized audio controls.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Keep desktop agent runs alive when non-critical message persistence fails and clean up streaming messages reliably.\n- Prevent review windows from hanging on blank webviews by adding readiness detection, timeout recovery, and transcript-processing timeout alignment.\n- Preserve a writing-style change made during dictation as the app's new default.\n- Allow nullable interaction-feedback volume values during onboarding IPC.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Replace heavy interaction-feedback sounds with shorter, tighter click transients and apply safe volume limits across audio playback paths.\n\nTests:\n- Add regression coverage for agent continuation and streaming cleanup, style persistence, nullable user onboarding data, volume clamping, database migration, and review-window readiness recovery.\n\nChores:\n- Address remaining static-analysis findings with diagnostic logging and explicit resource cleanup annotations.\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n- **New Features**\n  - Added a settings control for adjusting interaction feedback volume, with localized labels.\n  - Volume preferences are saved and applied consistently across audio playback.\n\n- **Bug Fixes**\n  - Improved composer recovery when readiness events are delayed or missing.\n  - Extended review-before-insert processing time to prevent premature timeouts.\n  - Agent workflows now continue when non-critical message persistence fails.\n  - Manual app styling updates are synchronized before subsequent dictation sessions.\n\n- **Tests**\n  - Expanded coverage for audio preferences, composer recovery, agent workflows, and user persistence.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->"
