+      "description": "## Purpose\n\nSpoken formatting commands and a conservative silence-hallucination filter.\n\n**Base is #63** (`fix/superfix-review-findings`) so we do not collide with that PR’s migrations:\n\n| Version | Owner | Column / change |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 075 | #63 | tone structured fields |\n| 076 | #63 | `hallucination_filter_enabled` + other feature prefs |\n| **077** | this PR | `spoken_commands_enabled` DEFAULT 1 |\n\nThis PR does **not** add a second hallucination column. After merge, wire the existing 076 field to the TS toggle.\n\n## What landed\n\n- Commands after replacements + hallucination strip, before hashtag/pound\n- English-only via resolved BCP-47; `primary` / `auto` / `keyboard-layout` are not English\n- Realtime: punctuation live; scratch/new-line on the **saved** transcript only\n- Phrase filter matches #63 conservatism; no invented `noSpeechProb`\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] Unit tests for commands, hallucination, sanitize pipeline, language gate\n- [ ] Rebase/CI against #63 (`cargo`, `pnpm gen:bindings`, desktop check-types)\n\n## Summary by Sourcery\n\nAdd English spoken formatting commands and conservative silence-hallucination filtering to the shared transcription sanitization pipeline.\n\nNew Features:\n- Add optional English spoken formatting commands for punctuation, line breaks, paragraphs, and “scratch that” editing.\n- Expose spoken-command behavior as a persisted user preference, enabled by default.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Conservatively filter known silence hallucinations and high-confidence silent transcription segments only for resolved English dictation languages.\n- Preserve paragraph boundaries when removing hallucinated transcript lines.\n\nEnhancements:\n- Centralize transcript sanitization so replacements, hallucination filtering, spoken commands, and symbol conversion run consistently across realtime, imported, and finalized transcripts.\n- Keep structural spoken commands out of realtime interim output while applying them to the saved transcript.\n\nDocumentation:\n- Document spoken-command availability, English-language requirements, and realtime limitations in settings.\n\nTests:\n- Add coverage for spoken commands, protected phrases, language gating, hallucination filtering, and the shared sanitization pipeline.\n\nChores:\n- Add the database migration and cross-layer preference plumbing for spoken commands."
