+      "description": "## What changed?\n\nTwo related fixes to the dictation pill's long-press interaction, tuned in a browser prototype (`docs/prototypes/long-press-ring.html`) before porting to the three native renderers.\n\n### 1. The long-press ring is one continuous comet\n\nThe old ring filled linearly, shimmered through a binary dash pattern, and switched to a *separate* \"armed\" renderer at completion — so the gesture read as a mechanical fill followed by a visible cut. It also left a bright lump parked at the top of the pill once the hold finished.\n\n**Root cause of the lump:** on a closed path, distance `0` and distance `total_len` are the same point, so a comet envelope measured backwards from the head is discontinuous there — `1.0` on one side, the trail floor on the other, an 0.86 brightness step across a single pixel. Growing the tail cannot fix it, because the falloff is always relative to the head. `ring_seal()` now crossfades the whole envelope to a uniform outline as the hold completes, so both sides of the seam match exactly at `p = 1`.\n\nEverything is driven by a single progress value; there is no armed-state branch to switch between. Per-point brightness is `ring_envelope()` (comet falloff) × `ring_glimmer()` (a travelling sine synced to the pill's waveform phase), replacing the dash on/off. Neither Cairo nor Direct2D can stroke a gradient along a path, so the shading is applied per segment to an evenly-spaced resampling of the perimeter — uniform spacing matters because the raw path concentrates its vertices in the corner arcs. The resample buffer lives on the pill state and is reused every frame.\n\nAlso in this pass:\n- the comet head is soft (concentric discs, no hard core) and **dissolves while blooming outward** before completion, so nothing bright reaches the seam\n- **inflation starts partway through the hold**, so arming continues an existing motion instead of starting one, plus a brief press-anticipation dip\n- eased ramp with an instant lead-in, and an accelerating release\n- an **arm-confirmation pulse** — on Windows this needs its own liveness check and dirty flag, because it outlives the ring's alpha and would otherwise be culled mid-flight\n\nThe three renderers share the per-frame policy via `advance_ring`/`RingAnim` so their timing cannot drift, and the triplicated linear `long_press_progress` now delegates to the shared eased version. Retires the dash constants and `update_ring_alpha`.\n\n### 2. The pill no longer collapses when you drag across without releasing\n\n`hovered` is recomputed each frame from a cursor hit test against the pill's last painted rect. That test is only meaningful when the pointer is free — during a drag the press owns the pointer and the window is chasing the cursor, so the probe misses and the pill snapped back to its collapsed size mid-gesture.\n\nThe subtlety, and the reason the first attempt at this was wrong: gating on `dragging || long_press_active` **does not cover the reported case**. Pressing arms `long_press_active`, but moving more than `LONG_PRESS_MOVE_THRESHOLD` (8px) before the 0.45s hold completes *cancels* it, and `dragging` only arms if the hold survives. A normal drag-across therefore leaves the button held with **neither flag set**.\n\nSo hover is now pinned by a `pointer_down` flag — set on press, cleared on release, independent of whether the gesture survived. Because the pin no longer rides on a gesture that can self-cancel, every release path clears it (mouse-up on all three platforms, plus `WM_CAPTURECHANGED` on Windows), and since a release event can be missed outright (stolen grab, locked session) each platform polls the real button state as a backstop.\n\nThis also stops the expand spring, the style tooltip, the cancel/pause controls and the expanded input region from flickering during a drag, since all of them are gated on the same flag.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\n- [x] Automated tests\n- [ ] Manual verification\n- [x] Code inspection\n\n**Added:** 20 unit tests in `rust_pill_shared` covering the new ring model — including regressions for the seam discontinuity (`envelope_seals_the_seam_at_completion`, `sealing_strictly_reduces_the_seam_step`), the head being gone before completion, glimmer continuity across the seam, buffer reuse, and the cancelled-long-press hover case (`hover_holds_when_a_cancelled_long_press_becomes_a_plain_drag`).\n\n**Extended:** `scripts/ci/pr28-contracts.test.mjs` from 23 to 24 tests with 5 new suites asserting the single-driver design, no per-frame allocation, mid-hold inflate, pulse liveness, and that a gesture-keyed hover gate cannot creep back in. All 24 pass.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **`cargo test` has not been run.** The sandbox this was written in has no Rust toolchain and no network access to install one, so the crates are **not compile-verified**.\n>\n> To compensate I: parsed every modified file against the real tree-sitter Rust grammar (no syntax errors); transcribed each shared function to JS and executed all the test assertions (78 passing); and replayed the drag gesture frame-by-frame, confirming the simulation reproduces the collapse under the old gate and not the new one.\n>\n> That covers syntax and math but **cannot prove type or borrow correctness**. Please run `cargo test -p rust_pill_shared` and `./scripts/verify-native-pills.sh` before merging. The two spots I'd scrutinise are the `msg_send![class!(NSEvent), pressedMouseButtons]` return type on macOS and the new `draw_line_shaded` COM brush reuse on Windows.\n\nVisual behaviour is unverified on real hardware — the prototype is the reference for how it should look, and it's kept in-tree as a tuning sandbox.\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n  * Redesigned the long-press ring with continuous comet progress, glimmering effects, soft glow, release drift, and confirmation pulses.\n  * Added press anticipation and inflation feedback during long-press gestures.\n  * Improved drag hover behavior and recovery when release events are missed across platforms.\n  * Added a standalone interactive prototype for comparing ring animations and tuning behavior.\n\n* **Documentation**\n  * Documented shared long-press geometry, animation behavior, and platform validation requirements.\n\n* **Tests**\n  * Expanded coverage for ring animation, gesture handling, hover persistence, and redraw behavior, including platform-specific animation liveness.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->"
