+## Deep-refinement pass (in progress)
+
+- **Intent and method:** This is a plan-first reliability and security review of the prevention work, not proof that the entire patch is defect-free. A specialist would inspect ownership of asynchronous resources and hostile or malformed boundary data, then reproduce failures rather than infer safety from passing happy-path tests. Raw material is the working-tree diff, its actual consumers and tests, and provider/runtime specifications. The method is boundary-by-boundary inspection, failure injection, red/green regression tests and targeted type/build gates. Tests below are the independent local check; live-provider and native/hosted execution remain hypotheses pending the environments in [sandbox coverage gaps](../../engineering/sandbox-coverage-gaps.md), including G10 for model artifacts.
+- **P1, cancellation resource ownership:** `secure-fetch.utils.ts` left an abort listener attached when an HTTPS plugin or private IPC request never settled after cancellation; it now detaches at whichever event settles the race, observes late rejections and preserves the caller's abort even if best-effort native cancellation throws synchronously. A mock transport and mock IPC reproduced both failures; the listener is detached and cancellation errors no longer escape the abort event. The manual signal-combination fallback also now detaches immediately on a null-body response, where no body read can release it. The focused desktop transport and signal suites pass 99 tests. Direct reads through `response.body` and bodies never consumed can still retain fallback listeners until an input aborts; resolving that generally without changing streaming and cloning semantics remains open. These tests mock the Tauri transport, not a native webview.
+- **P1, AssemblyAI protocol and timing boundaries:** The total timeout was passed directly to `setTimeout` after only a finite-positive check. Values beyond the signed 32-bit timer limit can fire almost immediately in [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/api/timers.html#settimeoutcallback-delay-args); the adapter now rejects them before uploading, accepts the boundary, and continues to bound polling intervals by the deadline. The adapter also rejects non-object JSON and malformed upload URL types, non-string completed transcripts, unsafe transcript IDs and unexpected polling statuses instead of crashing, polling until timeout or interpolating path/query syntax into an authenticated request. The [AssemblyAI reference](https://www.assemblyai.com/docs/api-reference/transcripts/get) documents UUID transcript IDs and four status values; this implementation accepts a wider safe ASCII ID alphabet for compatibility, and a future provider status or identifier outside that alphabet would now fail closed. Tests reproduced these cases; 39 focused provider/redirect tests and the package build pass. A nonblank upload URL is not validated for origin or syntax; it is sent back to AssemblyAI rather than fetched by this client. Live compatibility remains unverified. A transport failure after the provider has accepted an upload or job can still cause a retry and duplicate remote work; no idempotency guarantee was established.
+- **P2, Retry-After date validation:** `Date.parse` normalized impossible calendar dates instead of treating them as malformed, despite the helper’s stated contract. A regression covering all three HTTP-date forms failed first; UTC-field round-tripping now rejects those dates while preserving valid leap days. The utilities suite passes 154 tests and builds, and 13 focused provider hint tests pass. Weekday mismatch is deliberately tolerated and leap seconds remain runtime-dependent.
+- **Integration verification:** A clean-snapshot desktop run passed 4,157 tests but failed one existing AssemblyAI test because the new invalid-upload diagnostic changed the established missing-URL wording. The diagnostic now includes both the prior wording and the invalid-value explanation; all 31 desktop AssemblyAI tests and 29 focused provider tests pass after the correction. A subsequent full desktop run passed 4,158 Vitest tests in 361 files and four Node tests, in about 9 minutes 48 seconds. Source files did not change during that run, though the ignored voice-ai build output was regenerated while it was running. The current full voice-ai suite passed 631 tests, the agent suite 56, Python developer tools 15, and CI script contracts 41; desktop typecheck also passed.
+- **Native verification resumed:** The documented current toolchain payload at `3025c92b` failed its mandatory manifest check on four absolute fontconfig symlinks, so it was not used. The earlier pinned payload `82b2f983` passed its complete manifest and all seven workspace Rust lockfile hashes. With Rust 1.88 and the verified offline dependencies, transcription library tests passed 64 (one timing test ignored), and sidecar integration passed seven (four real-model tests ignored). The exact Whisper tiny end-to-end test then passed using the verified vendored model served over a local loopback override; it downloaded, validated and transcribed the fixture with a CPU sidecar. The override intentionally bypasses the code’s default pinned-download digest check, although the downloaded fixture had already passed the vendor manifest. The test only asserts nonempty output and CPU inference, not transcript accuracy or resistance to fabricated constants. The three large ONNX models are not in the payload and were not run.
+- **Desktop wrapper integration:** An actual Tauri invocation exposed that both build wrappers passed an extra `--` to the pnpm script, causing the Tauri CLI to reject `build` before compilation. The pnpm argument boundary was not represented by the earlier mock. Both wrappers now omit it, and a shell argument regression and PowerShell source contract pass with the prior stale-executable tests. PowerShell execution itself remains unverified. A Linux debug `.deb` build subsequently succeeded using the verified sysroot after adding its PulseAudio private-library directory to the linker search path. It is not a release build; the production flavor environment file was absent. The `.deb` was extracted, not installed. A virtual-display launch reached Tauri application setup, but the sandbox lacked audio and a session bus and the WebKit child reported an EGL failure, so rendering and provider connectivity were not established. The provided tauri-driver requires GLIBC 2.39 while the host has 2.36, blocking the existing WebDriver render check. This is not an end-to-end packaged-app pass.
+- **WebDriver build failure:** The WebDriver setup previously ignored a failed `npm` build and could launch a stale executable. It now throws on a failed or interrupted build; a mocked regression failed before the fix and passes afterward. This does not demonstrate a working WebDriver session.
+- **Interrupted sidecar test startup:** `std::process::Child` does not terminate on drop, and the test harness previously created its cleanup owner only after port discovery and HTTP client construction. An error or cancelled future before then could leave a sidecar and stdout-draining task behind. The process is now guarded immediately after spawn; dropping the guard kills and reaps it even before health readiness. A Unix test cancels port discovery for a real sleeping child and checks it is gone; the full integration target passed eight tests with four model tests ignored. This addresses the test harness, not all process owners elsewhere.
+- **Native pill packaging observation:** The extracted debug `.deb` contains no `mausvoice-gtk-pill`, and its startup logs report that it is missing. The current bundle configuration lists transcription sidecars and resource files but no pill executable, while the resolver expects the GTK pill in bundled resources and intentionally falls back to Tauri overlays when absent. The release workflow similarly has no explicit pill-binary packaging step, but no release artifact was inspected; do not infer behavior of a shipped release from this debug artifact alone. The fallback is documented in the architecture, so whether release packaging should require the native pill needs a product decision and platform-specific verification; a missing native pill is not evidence that the fallback rendered successfully.
+- **Software rendering retry:** Disabling WebKit compositing and requesting software GL did not remove the virtual-display EGL error. Rendering remains unverified; this is not evidence of failure on a supported desktop.
+- **SenseVoice real-model regression (unresolved):** In a freshly restored, manifest-verified vendor environment, the Linux x86-64 debug CPU sidecar built with the normal toolchain aborted with `munmap_chunk(): invalid pointer` while validating locally supplied SenseVoice files whose SHA-256 values matched both production pins. GDB located the abort in the statically linked ONNX Runtime's `ParseSemVerVersion`/C++ regex path during recognizer creation. A minimal Rust program using the same sherpa library and model could create and destroy a recognizer. The full sidecar and the minimal program selected different weak `std::__detail::_Scanner<char>` implementations; the full binary selected one from whisper.cpp compiled with GCC 12, while the ONNX archive contains a different implementation compiled with GCC 11. This supports, but does not prove, a C++ ABI/one-definition-rule collision as the cause.
+- **Experimental workaround, not a production fix:** After a clean rebuild with `CXXFLAGS=-D_GLIBCXX_USE_CXX11_ABI=0`, the sidecar validated SenseVoice and transcribed both `test.wav` and the first ten seconds of `audio.wav` on CPU; Whisper tiny also transcribed `test.wav` in the same process. The outputs differed across the two SenseVoice inputs, but no authoritative transcripts were available, so neither accuracy nor complete freedom from fabricated output is established. Rebuilding without the flag again reproduced the abort. Compiler flags are not guaranteed to invalidate this Cargo dependency's existing build cache: the experiment explicitly cleaned `whisper-rs-sys`. Do not treat that flag as a verified cross-platform or release remedy; local builds and CI builds use multiple entry points and sherpa variants. The later CI-toolchain mitigation is described below; standalone builds without it and other platforms remain unresolved.
+- **SenseVoice regression coverage:** Added an ignored exact integration test and included it in the existing manual Linux ONNX workflow. It normally tests the production download path; for offline runs it accepts a local fixture directory and verifies every copied file against the production artifact digest pins before runtime validation and inference. The test passed under the experimental ABI flag, failed with the default build as above, and rejected a deliberately corrupted token file. It was initially manual-only; a later change adds it to the Linux transcription push/PR job. No hosted run or actual SenseVoice download was verified here. The full non-ignored sidecar target subsequently passed eight tests with five ignored, and the runner's six Node tests passed.
+- **ABI research and cache experiment:** The primary GCC bug report [libstdc++/118408](https://www.mail-archive.com/gcc-bugs@gcc.gnu.org/msg845075.html) identifies `std::__detail::_Scanner` as containing an ABI-dependent string while emitting the same symbol under both libstdc++ ABIs; a [GCC maintainer discussion](https://www.mail-archive.com/gcc-bugs@gcc.gnu.org/msg845421.html) confirms the failure requires mixing `std::regex` instances across those ABIs. An independently reported [whisper.cpp/sherpa-onnx collision](https://github.com/sidevoice/sidevoice-engine/pull/40) matches the observed pattern, but does not prove every archive or platform here shares it. In a local experiment, adding only a target-specific Cargo environment flag did **not** rebuild already-cached `whisper-rs-sys`: the cached CMake flags remained unchanged. Adding a target Rust cfg marker forced a fresh build and the SenseVoice test passed, but that trial configuration was removed; repository tooling guidance prohibits committing `.cargo/config.toml`, and it would affect all x86-64 GNU Rust workspaces while external Cargo/Rust/C++ overrides can bypass it. A robust fix still needs an agreed build-wide enforcement and cache migration, plus validation of custom sherpa archives and Linux GPU builds.
+- **Two-input SenseVoice check:** The real-model test now transcribes both repository audio fixtures (up to ten seconds each), requires nonempty CPU results and rejects identical trimmed transcripts. It passed with the explicitly configured ABI workaround and verified local artifacts. This catches a single constant response for these inputs, not input-dependent fabricated text or inaccurate transcripts; the normal download path and hosted CI remain untested. The normal integration target again passed eight tests with five ignored, and the Node runner passed six tests.
+- **Linux CI-toolchain mitigation:** The Linux CI CMake toolchain now builds whisper.cpp with the old libstdc++ ABI only for Cargo target `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`, matching the pinned x86-64 static sherpa archive. From a clean target directory with that toolchain and no ABI environment override, the compile command contained the flag, the digest-verified local SenseVoice two-input CPU test passed, and the Whisper tiny download/inference test passed using a local override (which bypasses Whisper's normal download digest gate). A small CMake configuration test checks that GNU x86-64 receives the flag and aarch64, musl and an unset target do not. The transcription workflow now runs SenseVoice on Linux for matching pushes/PRs (about 237 MB of model downloads per run), while the larger models remain manual. Rust cache keys in the desktop build, transcription test and release workflows were rotated because `whisper-rs-sys` did not rebuild after a toolchain change in the earlier experiment. Hosted CI and its actual download have not run. This mitigation initially covered CI paths selecting this toolchain; a later desktop preparation change is described below. Standalone builds without it can still crash, and existing standalone targets must clean `whisper-rs-sys` before rebuilding. A fresh optimized release-profile build also passed the two-input SenseVoice test with the pinned local artifacts; its compile command contained the ABI flag, and the release non-ignored integration target passed eight tests (five ignored). This is a sidecar binary test, not a packaged-app or hosted-release test. GPU, custom sherpa archives, other architectures and release packaging are unverified.
+- **Desktop default and release gate:** On Linux x86-64 GNU, desktop sidecar preparation now selects the repository ABI toolchain when no custom one was requested and builds into an ABI-versioned subdirectory of the configured Cargo target directory. This separates it from previously cached incompatible objects, at the cost of a one-time rebuild and extra target-directory disk usage; changing the ABI requirements again requires a namespace bump. Explicit custom toolchains are preserved with a warning, not certified or rewritten. An actual debug sidecar prepared with no `CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE` set validated pinned local SenseVoice artifacts, produced nonempty distinct outputs for two inputs, and transcribed a Whisper tiny fixture on CPU. Its compile command included the ABI flag; a second preparation reused the build in 0.41 seconds. A mocked process integration test and four settings tests also pass. This tests the sidecar copied into the desktop binary directory, not the packaged webview. Direct standalone Cargo invocations still need the documented toolchain and cache clean. Conflicting custom compiler flags, custom sherpa archives, GPU and non-Linux platforms remain unverified.
+- **Release verification:** The release workflow's existing `verify-rust` prerequisite now runs the Linux SenseVoice E2E test before platform builds, with a 45-minute budget instead of 25 and a roughly 237 MB model download. It validates a separately built test sidecar, not the eventual installer artifact; network/provider availability can block release. The exact runner command passed locally with the digest-checked offline fixture and configured toolchain. Hosted CI has not run, and no production download was made in this environment.
+- **C++ flag precedence and runner drift:** A CMake compilation test showed that a caller's global `CXXFLAGS`/`CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS` definition of the new ABI appeared after the old toolchain's `add_compile_definitions` and silently won, reproducing the wrong effective ABI. The toolchain now emits an undefine and the old-ABI definition as compile options after global flags; the test failed before this change and passes afterward. On a fresh verified offline build with `CXXFLAGS=-D_GLIBCXX_USE_CXX11_ABI=1`, the real SenseVoice CPU test passed and the whisper.cpp compile command placed the corrective options after the conflicting global flag. The normal sidecar target also passed eight tests (five ignored). This does not guarantee against conflicting target-specific options or custom toolchains. The ABI target namespace and affected CI cache keys were rotated to v2 because CMake toolchain changes alone do not invalidate whisper-rs-sys artifacts. A fresh desktop preparation with no explicitly selected toolchain and the conflicting global flag produced a v2 sidecar that validated the pinned local SenseVoice bundle and returned distinct nonempty CPU results for both audio fixtures. The release Rust verification runner is now pinned to Ubuntu 24.04 like transcription CI, whose existing documentation records bindgen failures with the 26.04 image; hosted release verification remains unrun.
+- **Windows pill packaging failure paths:** The existing release policy rejects a failed native pill build, but two adjacent paths only warned: a missing pill manifest and a successful Cargo invocation that did not produce the expected executable. Both now use the same failure policy; an explicit override can use a previously built resource but cannot make a missing resource valid. The pill build also uses `--locked`. A process-level fake-Cargo test exercises missing manifest, missing output, stale-resource rejection, override, and successful replacement; it does not verify a real Windows build or installer, nor detect a stale Cargo output left in the target directory after an apparently successful build. Linux GTK pill packaging remains a separate product decision.
+- **Scoped CMake toolchain precedence:** The pinned `cmake` crate 0.1.58 resolves target-specific and host/target-specific toolchain environment variables before `CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE` ([source](https://github.com/rust-lang/cmake-rs/blob/v0.1.58/src/lib.rs)). Desktop preparation previously examined only the generic variable, so a custom scoped toolchain could silently override the ABI mitigation while the script claimed its default was in effect. Preparation now detects and preserves scoped custom settings with a warning, normalizes matching scoped paths, and conservatively treats either host/target override as caller-managed when their applicability is ambiguous. Node contracts cover priority, blank scoped values, and process-level warning/target selection using fake Cargo; this is not a real custom-toolchain inference test.
+- **Rust cache paths:** The pinned rust-cache action resolves a target path by joining it to its workspace root ([implementation](https://github.com/Swatinem/rust-cache/blob/49a0bdc70d2e1b713ca9e2869b211fcce03d3c1c/src/config.ts#L142-L150)); several workflows supplied repository-relative paths and thus targeted doubled, nonexistent directories. Corrected those paths and included the transcription workspace in desktop build caches, covering its nested ABI-versioned Linux target directory. The previous Windows release expression supplied an absolute `D:\cargo` as a joined target; it was replaced with the normal target entry. Windows builds still use `D:\cargo`, which is not cached by this setup; adding it as an uncleaned cache directory could grow the cache substantially and needs hosted measurement. The contract checks literal entries, not actual hosted cache hits; hosted performance and cache size remain unverified.
+- **Remaining work, ranked:** P0 packaged-app startup/provider smoke and real model outcome checks still require suitable platforms, artifacts and credentials; P1 inspect the rest of the large patch systematically, including stream ownership and workflow behavior on actual hosted PRs; P2 measure overhead on representative audio and controlled runners. Earlier broad desktop test runs overlapped source edits; the latest run had stable source but regenerated an ignored dependency build during execution. This pass has found and fixed concrete gaps but does not certify every implementation in the session.
+
+## Data Gaps and Assumptions
+
+- GitHub returned **168**, not 200, merged PRs at collection time. All returned PRs were accessible for the requested metadata and paginated files; no collection endpoint errors remained. Inventory size is 10,258 PR-file appearances, including repeated paths across stacked PRs. GitHub's list can change after collection.
+- [#4](https://github.com/maus-inc/mausVoice/pull/4) has an empty description in GitHub; it remains in the inventory with an empty string. Automated bot summaries embedded in other PR bodies are preserved as part of the description, not independently verified.
+- App authorship is a conservative observable identifier, not proof that every line was generated by AI. Human-authored PRs may also include agent work; review bots and human reviewers can both influence changes. Counts are not a measure of agent productivity, correctness, or total AI assistance.
+- PR descriptions, comments and commit messages are historical claims, and descriptions can become stale: [#322](https://github.com/maus-inc/mausVoice/pull/322) was returned as merged although its description still says it is open for review. Some review comments concern an earlier head and may already have been addressed before merge; stacked PR targets and merge ordering can make apparent parent/fix dates counterintuitive. The table states this where known. The report does not assert deployment or user impact without evidence.
+- The current checkout is a fixed commit rather than the final tree of every target branch, and predates #322's merge. Selected files were inspected at that snapshot; the whole repository and every historical diff were not exhaustively audited. The initial historical review did not independently run tests or cross-platform packaged builds; later local verification is described in Prevention work started. This limits conclusions about current runtime behavior and whether every historical defect remains fixed.
+- Test-file, documentation, workflow and title counts are transparent path/text heuristics, not manually adjudicated semantic labels. Inline tests and nonstandard test names can be missed; changing a test file is not necessarily adding a test.
+- Severity and mitigation are analyst judgments; review-reported issues are not presented as independently reproduced. A revert establishes removal of the cited patch from that target at that point, not that unrelated changes could never reintroduce a similar defect later.
